(window.COURSES=window.COURSES||{})["hci"]=JSON.parse("{\"id\":\"hci\",\"name\":\"Human Computer Interaction\",\"short\":\"HCI\",\"code\":\"CS314\",\"by\":\"Dr. Mona Samir Lackousha\",\"lectures\":[{\"n\":1,\"title\":\"Introduction to HCI\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"What is HCI?\",\"pts\":[\"HCI = the <b>interaction between user and computer</b>, OR the <b>relationship between users and computer systems</b>.\",\"Terms used interchangeably: <b>HCI</b> (Human Computer Interaction), <b>HMI</b> (Human Machine Interaction), <b>MMI</b> (Man Machine Interaction).\",\"HCI deals with the <b>Design</b>, <b>Implementation</b> and <b>Evaluation</b> of interactive systems.\",\"It studies humans (needs, abilities, limitations), computer technologies that improve the interaction, and the ways, preferences and difficulties of users of current or past systems.\",\"Three parts meet in HCI: the <b>User</b> (mental and physical capabilities), the <b>Computer / Machine</b> (input/output devices) and the <b>Interaction</b> (interfaces, HCI patterns, design, usability evaluation).\"]},{\"h\":\"Objectives of HCI\",\"pts\":[\"Design for <b>people</b>, for <b>tasks</b>, and for the <b>environments</b> users live and work in (e.g. an organization).\",\"Make interaction easier: design <b>easy, natural and intuitive</b> systems to <b>save time</b> and <b>reduce costs</b>.\",\"Design GUIs (learning them, using them efficiently, evaluating and comparing them) and web interfaces.\",\"Explore new paradigms/prototypes of interaction and develop models and theories of interaction.\"]},{\"h\":\"HCI in a broader hierarchy of fields\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Engineering + Psychology → Human Factors Engineering</b>: designing interactions between people and products, systems or devices.\",\"Human Factors Engineering branches into <b>Industrial Design</b>, <b>HCI</b> and <b>Product Design</b>.\",\"HCI then branches into <b>UI Design</b>, <b>UX Design</b> and <b>Interaction</b> design.\",\"Human-limit example: humans can distinguish <b>millions of colors</b> but only about <b>30 shades of gray</b>.\",\"The gap between HCI and human factors engineering is shrinking because more devices are becoming computerized.\"]},{\"h\":\"UI vs UX vs HCI\",\"pts\":[\"<b>UI Design</b> (User Interface): the <b>interactivity, look and feel</b> of a product screen or web page. It is about designing things nicely for a screen.\",\"<b>UX Design</b> (User Experience): the user's <b>overall experience</b> with the product or website. UX is about <b>dictating</b> the interactions.\",\"<b>HCI</b> is largely about <b>understanding</b> the interactions between humans and computers.\",\"Early systems ran on expensive machines, so engineers focused on efficient software and ignored usability. Today success requires <b>user-centric</b> design focused on usability and a pleasant, smooth experience.\"]},{\"h\":\"Human-Task interaction and the invisible interface\",\"pts\":[\"The user is really trying to achieve a <b>task</b> (buy online, watch a video, translate text). The system is only the means, so in reality the user interacts with the task.\",\"Example: in an online Zoom lecture, the instructor interacts with students, not with the computer.\",\"Like a sports referee, an interface is successful when it goes <b>unnoticed</b>.\",\"<b>Invisible interface</b> (ideal concept): users spend little time on the interface and focus on the task. The user should think about the task, not the tool.\"]},{\"h\":\"UI design process (3 components)\",\"pts\":[\"1. <b>User research</b>  2. <b>Design and prototyping</b> of the interface  3. <b>User interface evaluation</b> (improvement).\",\"User research means learning about the <b>users</b> (skills, distractions, special needs like kids, elderly, people with disabilities), the <b>tasks</b> they try to complete, and <b>previous research</b> on human capabilities, limitations and perception.\",\"Software development is research: it is not just creating software but also <b>learning how to create the software best suited for its purpose</b> (one of the 12 characteristics studied in Software Engineering).\"]},{\"h\":\"Case studies\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Online book library search</b>: a bad design where the user-research component is almost absent.\",\"<b>International Children's Digital Library (by HCIL)</b>: good design, more than <b>3 million unique visitors</b>; the visual search was preferred by users (<b>71% of searches</b>).\",\"Lessons: a good designer understands issues from the <b>user's point of view</b> and <b>works with users rather than guessing</b>.\",\"Bad UX example: a doctor's form asking for address, phone, email and SSN about 3 times for 10 different people took 30+ minutes, then ended with \\\"Internal server error\\\".\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"HCI (definition)\",\"The interaction between user and computer, or the relationship between users and computer systems.\"],[\"Terms used interchangeably with HCI\",\"HMI (Human Machine Interaction) and MMI (Man Machine Interaction).\"],[\"HCI deals with…\",\"Design, implementation and evaluation of interactive systems.\"],[\"UI Design\",\"Interactivity, look and feel of a product screen or web page.\"],[\"UX Design\",\"A user's overall experience with the product or website.\"],[\"Human Factors Engineering\",\"Designing interactions between people and products, systems or devices (Engineering + Psychology).\"],[\"Shades of gray humans can distinguish\",\"About 30 (versus millions of colors).\"],[\"Invisible interface\",\"An interface that vanishes so users focus on the task, not the tool.\"],[\"3 components of the UI design process\",\"User research → Design and prototyping → Evaluation (improvement).\"],[\"Children's digital library results\",\"3M+ unique visitors; visual search used in 71% of searches.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What are the objectives of HCI?\",\"Design for people, tasks and environments; make interaction easier with easy, natural and intuitive systems that save time and reduce cost; design, learn, use and evaluate GUIs and web interfaces; explore new interaction paradigms; develop models and theories of interaction.\"],[\"Differentiate between HCI, UX design and UI design.\",\"HCI is about understanding interactions between humans and computers. UX design is about dictating those interactions (the overall experience). UI design is about designing things nicely for a screen (look, feel, interactivity).\"],[\"Is the user interacting with the computer or with the task? Explain.\",\"With the task. The system is a means to achieve a task such as buying online. In a Zoom lecture the instructor interacts with students, not with the laptop. A good interface goes unnoticed, like a good referee.\"],[\"What should you learn during user research?\",\"The users (skills, distractions, special needs such as kids, elderly, disabilities), the tasks they try to complete, and existing research on human capabilities, limitations and perception.\"],[\"What lessons were learned from the children's library search design?\",\"A good designer understands issues from the user's point of view and works with users instead of guessing what they like.\"],[\"What are the three recommended components of the UI design process?\",\"1) <b>User research</b>: learning about the users (skills, possible distractions, special needs such as kids, elderly, people with disabilities), the tasks they try to complete, and existing research on human capabilities, limitations and perception. 2) <b>Design and prototyping</b> of the interface. 3) <b>User interface evaluation</b> (improvement).\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"HCI terms used interchangeably are:\",\"o\":[[\"Human Computer Interaction\",\"Human Computer Interaction is the full name of HCI itself, but the lecture lists HMI and MMI as equally valid terms, so picking only this option misses two of them (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"Human Machine Interaction\",\"HMI is one interchangeable term, but HCI and MMI are used for the same field too, so \\\"All the above\\\" is the complete answer (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"Man Machine Interaction\",\"MMI is one of the interchangeable terms, but so are HCI and HMI; the question asks for all of them, which is \\\"All the above\\\" (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"All the above\",\"Correct. HCI, HMI and MMI are all used for the same field.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"Which one refers to the interactivity, look and feel of a product screen or web page?\",\"o\":[[\"UX design\",\"UX covers the user's overall experience, which is broader than the screen's look and feel.\"],[\"UI design\",\"Correct. UI design is the interactivity, look and feel of a screen or page.\"],[\"Human factors engineering\",\"That is the parent field about people interacting with products, systems or devices.\"],[\"Industrial design\",\"A sibling of HCI under human factors engineering; it is not screen design.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The idea of an \\\"invisible interface\\\" means:\",\"o\":[[\"The interface uses no graphics\",\"It is not about removing visuals; it is about attention.\"],[\"Users focus on the task and forget about the interface\",\"Correct. The interface vanishes and the user thinks about the task, not the tool.\"],[\"The interface is hidden behind menus\",\"Hiding features in menus does not make the interaction feel direct.\"],[\"The interface only uses voice\",\"Modality is unrelated; any interface can aim to be invisible.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Humans can distinguish millions of colors but only about ___ shades of gray.\",\"o\":[[\"3\",\"Far too low: humans can tell apart about 30 shades of gray, ten times this number, which is still tiny next to millions of colors (L1, HCI hierarchy).\"],[\"30\",\"Correct. The lecture's human-limitation example is about 30 shades of gray.\"],[\"300\",\"Too high by a factor of ten. The lecture's human-limit example contrasts millions of colors with only about 30 shades of gray (L1, HCI hierarchy).\"],[\"3000\",\"Far too high. The point of the example is how few grays we can distinguish: only about 30, against millions of colors (L1, HCI hierarchy).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What is the FIRST component of the UI design process?\",\"o\":[[\"User research\",\"Correct. The order is user research → design and prototyping → evaluation.\"],[\"Evaluation and improvement\",\"Evaluation (improvement) is the third and last component: you can only evaluate an interface after researching users and designing it (L1, UI design process).\"],[\"Design and prototyping\",\"Design and prototyping is the second component; it comes after user research, because you must learn the users and their tasks before designing (L1, UI design process).\"],[\"Implementation\",\"Implementation is not one of the three listed components.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the International Children's Digital Library, visual search was used in about what share of searches?\",\"o\":[[\"17%\",\"17% reverses the digits of the real figure: visual search was used in 71% of searches, showing users preferred it (L1, Case studies).\"],[\"50%\",\"50% would mean no clear preference, but the lecture reports visual search was used in 71% of searches, a clear majority (L1, Case studies).\"],[\"71%\",\"Correct. 71% of searches used the visual search.\"],[\"91%\",\"Too high. The International Children's Digital Library reported visual search in 71% of searches, not 91% (L1, Case studies).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which statement about early computer systems is true?\",\"o\":[[\"They were designed with strong focus on usability\",\"The opposite: early machines were expensive, so engineers optimised the software for the machine and ignored usability (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"They focused on efficient software because machines were expensive\",\"Correct. Engineers optimised the expensive machine, not the user's experience.\"],[\"They were user-centric\",\"User-centric design describes today's systems, where success depends on usability. Early systems were machine-centric, focused on efficient software (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"They used invisible interfaces\",\"The invisible interface is a modern ideal where users focus on the task. Early systems ignored usability altogether, so they were far from it (L1, Invisible interface).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Human Factors Engineering is formed from which two fields?\",\"o\":[[\"Engineering and Psychology\",\"Correct. Both feed into human factors engineering in the hierarchy figure.\"],[\"HCI and UX\",\"These are below human factors engineering, not above it.\"],[\"Art and Marketing\",\"Art and Marketing do not appear in the hierarchy figure. Human factors engineering comes from Engineering + Psychology (L1, HCI hierarchy).\"],[\"Industrial and Product design\",\"These are branches of human factors engineering, not its sources.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"S/W development is not just a process of creating software, it is also a process of learning how to create the S/W best suited for its purpose.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture calls this \\\"software development is research\\\".\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is taken directly from the lecture, so it is true.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"UI Design covers a user's overall experience with the product or website.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The overall experience with a product or website is the definition of UX design. UI design is only the interactivity, look and feel of a screen or page (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. UI is look, feel and interactivity; UX is the overall experience.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary goal of HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"To make computers more complex\",\"HCI aims for the opposite: to design easy, natural and intuitive computer systems that make the user's interaction easier (L1, Objective of HCI).\"],[\"To increase software security\",\"Security is not listed among the objectives of HCI; the objectives are about designing for people, tasks and environments and making interaction easier (L1, Objective of HCI).\"],[\"To make computers faster\",\"Machine efficiency was the focus of early systems, when usability was not thought of. HCI is about the user's interaction, not machine speed (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"To improve usability and user experience\",\"Correct. Today the success of a system requires a design that is user-centric, focused on <b>usability</b> and a pleasant (smooth) <b>user experience</b> (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The study of HCI mainly focuses on:\",\"o\":[[\"Writing more complex code\",\"HCI is about the interaction between users and computers and making it easier, not about code complexity (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"The relationship between humans and computers\",\"Correct. HCI is the interaction between user and computer, or the <b>relationship between users and computer systems</b> (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"Improving computer hardware performance\",\"Hardware performance is not the focus; HCI studies humans (needs, abilities, limitations) and the technologies that improve their interaction with computers (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"How computers interact with each other\",\"HCI always involves the human: it is the interaction between <b>user</b> and computer, not computer to computer (L1, What is HCI?).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What does UX stand for in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Usability Examination\",\"This is not a term from the course. In the lecture, UX is written as UX Design (User Experience) (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"Unified Experience\",\"The U in UX stands for User, not Unified: UX Design (User Experience) covers a user's overall experience with the product (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"User Experience\",\"Correct. The lecture writes it as <b>UX Design (User Experience)</b>: it covers a user's overall experience with the product or website (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"User Execution\",\"The X stands for Experience, not Execution: UX is about the user's overall experience with the product or website (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"In HCI, what does UI stand for?\",\"o\":[[\"Usability Examination\",\"This is not a term from the course. The lecture writes UI as UI Design (User Interface) (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"User Execution\",\"The I stands for Interface: UI design refers to the interactivity, look and feel of a product screen or web page (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"User Interface\",\"Correct. The lecture writes it as <b>UI Design (User Interface)</b>: the interactivity, look and feel of a product screen or web page (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"Unified Experience\",\"This mixes up the terms: Experience belongs to UX (User Experience), and the U means User, not Unified (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a key factor in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"User interface (UI)\",\"UI Design is one of the branches of HCI in the lecture's hierarchy of fields, so it is a key part of HCI (L1, HCI in a broader hierarchy of fields).\"],[\"Usability\",\"The lecture says success of a system today requires a design focused on <b>usability</b>, so usability is central to HCI (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"Machine learning\",\"Correct. Machine learning is not part of HCI in the course; HCI branches into UI design, UX design and interaction design, and focuses on usability and user experience (L1, HCI in a broader hierarchy of fields).\"],[\"User experience (UX)\",\"UX Design is one of the branches of HCI in the lecture's hierarchy, and a pleasant user experience is required for success (L1, HCI in a broader hierarchy of fields).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best defines Human-Computer Interaction (HCI)?\",\"o\":[[\"The study of computer hardware and software\",\"HCI is not the study of the machine alone; it is the interaction between user and computer, and it also studies humans (needs, abilities, limitations) (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"The study of algorithms and their performance\",\"Algorithm performance is a software-efficiency concern, the focus of early systems when usability was not thought of; HCI is about the user's interaction (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"The study of networking and databases\",\"Networking and databases are not part of HCI's definition; HCI is the relationship between users and computer systems (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"The study of how humans interact with computers and how to design better systems\",\"Correct. HCI is the interaction between user and computer, and it deals with the <b>design, implementation and evaluation</b> of interactive systems whose users get the maximum benefit with ease (L1, What is HCI?).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a goal of HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Make technology more accessible\",\"This is a goal: HCI designs for people, including users with special needs such as kids, the elderly and people with disabilities, and aims to make interaction with the computer easier (L1, Objectives of HCI; User research).\"],[\"Improve usability\",\"This is a goal: the lecture says success of a system today requires a user-centric design <b>focused on usability</b> (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"Increase system complexity\",\"Correct. HCI aims for the opposite: to <b>design easy, natural and intuitive</b> systems that save time and reduce costs (L1, Objectives of HCI). L2 also shows that more and more features make an app feel complicated.\"],[\"Enhance user experience\",\"This is a goal: the lecture says success requires a pleasant (smooth) user experience, and UX design is one of the branches of HCI (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes the difference between UI and UX?\",\"o\":[[\"UI is the interface; UX is the overall experience\",\"Correct. UI design refers to the 'interactivity, look and feel of product screen or web page', while UX design 'covers a user's <b>overall experience</b> with the product or website' (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"UI and UX are identical\",\"The lecture defines them separately: UI is the look and feel of a screen, UX is the overall experience, and they are separate branches under HCI (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"UI is about coding; UX is about graphics\",\"Neither definition is about coding. UI is about designing things nicely for a screen, and UX is about the overall experience and dictating the interactions (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"UX replaces UI entirely\",\"The lecture shows UI design and UX design as two separate branches of HCI; one does not replace the other (L1, HCI in a broader hierarchy of fields).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main goal of the UI Design Process overall?\",\"o\":[[\"To focus only on coding speed\",\"Focusing on efficient software was how early systems were built, when 'usability was not thought of'; today the design must be user-centric (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"To create interfaces that are easy, effective, and enjoyable to use\",\"Correct. HCI aims to 'design easy, natural and intuitive computer systems', and success requires a design 'focused on usability and pleasant (smooth) user experience' (L1, Objectives of HCI; UI vs UX vs HCI).\"],[\"To avoid user feedback\",\"The process starts with user research and ends with user interface evaluation; the lecture says to work with users rather than guess (L1, UI design process; Case studies).\"],[\"To make the software more complex\",\"The goal is the opposite: easy, natural and intuitive systems that save time and reduce costs (L1, Objectives of HCI).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What comes after user research in the UI Design Process?\",\"o\":[[\"Design and prototyping of the interface\",\"Correct. The three components are 1- user research, 2- <b>design and prototyping of the interface</b>, 3- user interface evaluation (improvement) (L1, UI design process).\"],[\"Marketing\",\"Marketing is not one of the three components of the UI design process (L1, UI design process).\"],[\"Coding\",\"Coding is not listed as a component; the three components are user research, design and prototyping, and evaluation (L1, UI design process).\"],[\"Evaluation\",\"Evaluation (improvement) is the third component, which comes after design and prototyping, not directly after user research (L1, UI design process).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the goal of user research in the UI design process?\",\"o\":[[\"To create animations\",\"Animations are not part of user research. User research is about learning about the users and their tasks before designing (L1, User Research).\"],[\"To test the final product only\",\"Testing and improving the interface is the third component, user interface evaluation, not user research, which comes first (L1, UI Design Process).\"],[\"To focus only on coding\",\"Coding is not mentioned in the UI design process at all. Its components are user research, design and prototyping, and evaluation (L1, UI Design Process).\"],[\"To understand users’ needs, goals, and behaviors\",\"Correct. In user research you learn about the <b>users</b> (their skills, distractions, special needs) and the <b>tasks</b> they try to complete, so the design is based on them (L1, User Research).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What lesson about usability was learned from the success of the International Children's Digital Library (ICDL)?\",\"o\":[[\"Children prefer reading long explanations\",\"The lecture reports the opposite kind of finding: users preferred the <b>visual</b> search, used in 71% of searches (L1, Example Case).\"],[\"Visual navigation helps young users search effectively\",\"Correct. The ICDL search page designed by HCIL was a good design with more than 3 million unique visitors, and its <b>visual search was preferred by users (71% of searches)</b> (L1, Example Case).\"],[\"Users dislike interactive designs\",\"Nothing in the lecture supports this. The ICDL's interactive visual search was the preferred way to search (L1, Example Case).\"],[\"Simplicity reduces user satisfaction\",\"The lecture presents the ICDL as a good design that users liked, and HCI aims at easy, natural and intuitive systems (L1, Objective of HCI).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following best describes an invisible interface?\",\"o\":[[\"One that lets users feel they are interacting directly with the task\",\"Correct. The lecture says our goal is to help humans <b>feel they are interacting directly with the task</b> while the interface vanishes in the middle of that interaction (L1, Invisible Interface).\"],[\"One that hides all functionality permanently\",\"'Invisible' does not mean hiding functions. It means users spend little time focusing on the interface and focus on their task (L1, Invisible Interface).\"],[\"One that constantly prompts the user with instructions\",\"Constant prompts pull attention to the interface, while an invisible interface is one the user forgets about, like an unnoticed sports referee (L1, Human-Computer or Human-Task interaction).\"],[\"One that requires intensive manual input for every action\",\"Heavy input makes the user think about the tool. The lecture says the user should think about the task rather than the tool (L1, Invisible Interface).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the third component in the UI design process?\",\"o\":[[\"App installation\",\"Installation is not one of the components. The three are user research, design and prototyping, and user interface evaluation (L1, UI Design Process).\"],[\"Marketing\",\"Marketing is not part of the UI design process. Its three components are user research, design and prototyping, and evaluation (L1, UI Design Process).\"],[\"User interface evaluation (improvement)\",\"Correct. The three recommended components are 1- user research, 2- design and prototyping of the interface, 3- <b>user interface evaluation (improvement)</b> (L1, UI Design Process).\"],[\"Data entry\",\"Data entry is not a component of the UI design process; the third component is user interface evaluation (L1, UI Design Process).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"In the invisible interface concept, the user should:\",\"o\":[[\"Think about the task, not the tool\",\"Correct. The slide says '<b>The user should think about the task rather than thinking about the tool</b>' (L1, Invisible Interface).\"],[\"Spend more time learning the interface\",\"The goal is the reverse: users should spend little time focusing on the interface and focus on the task instead (L1, Invisible Interface).\"],[\"Frequently check for updates\",\"Checking for updates is attention on the tool. In an invisible interface the user forgets about the interface and works on the task (L1, Human-Computer or Human-Task interaction).\"],[\"Focus on the buttons and menus\",\"Buttons and menus are the tool. The lecture wants users to focus on the task they are trying to accomplish, not on the interface (L1, Invisible Interface).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"HCI focuses on designing computer hardware rather than software.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"HCI is not about hardware design. It is the interaction (relationship) between users and computer systems, and it deals with the design, implementation and evaluation of <b>interactive systems</b> (L1, What is HCI?).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. HCI is the <b>interaction between user and computer</b>, OR the relationship between users and computer systems. It deals with design, implementation and evaluation of interactive systems, such as GUIs and web interfaces (L1, What is HCI? / Objective of HCI).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The primary goal of HCI is to improve the interaction between humans and computers.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. HCI is about the interaction between user and computer, and its objectives include '<b>How to make the user interaction with computer easier?</b>' by designing easy, natural and intuitive systems (L1, What is HCI? / Objective of HCI).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture's objectives of HCI centre on making user interaction with the computer easier, and HCI studies computer technologies that can enhance the interaction between users and computers (L1, Objective of HCI).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Usability is a key factor in HCI, ensuring that systems are easy and efficient to use.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says the success of a computer system today requires the design to be <b>user-centric, focused on usability</b> and a smooth user experience, and HCI aims for easy, natural, intuitive systems and efficient use of interfaces (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI / Objective of HCI).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says the opposite: early systems ignored usability, but today success requires a design focused on usability, and HCI aims at easy and efficient use (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The primary goal of HCI is to improve system security, not usability.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"HCI's objectives are about making user interaction easier with easy, natural and intuitive systems, and success requires a design focused on <b>usability</b>. Privacy and security appear only as a rule for handling user data in research (L1, Objective of HCI; L4, Privacy and Security).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The objectives of HCI are to make the user interaction with computers easier and to design easy, natural and intuitive systems; success requires a user-centric design <b>focused on usability</b> (L1, Objective of HCI / UI vs UX vs HCI).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The field of HCI includes elements from psychology, design, and computer science.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. In the hierarchy of fields, <b>Engineering + Psychology</b> form Human Factors Engineering, of which HCI is a branch, and HCI branches into UI, UX and interaction <b>design</b>. HCI also studies humans (needs, abilities, limitations) and computer technologies (L1, HCI in a broader hierarchy of fields).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture places HCI under Human Factors Engineering (Engineering + Psychology), says HCI deals with the design of interactive systems, and says it studies both humans and computer technologies, so it does draw on all three (L1, HCI in a broader hierarchy of fields / What is HCI?).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The main goal of HCI is to make systems as complex as possible to challenge users.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"HCI aims for the opposite. One objective of HCI is to make interaction easier by designing <b>easy, natural and intuitive</b> systems that save time and reduce costs (L1, Objective of HCI).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The lecture says HCI should make the user's interaction with the computer easier and design <b>easy, natural and intuitive</b> systems to save time and reduce costs, not complex ones (L1, Objective of HCI).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"HCI principles do not apply to web design.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"One of the listed objectives of HCI is <b>designing web interfaces</b>, so HCI clearly applies to web design (L1, Objective of HCI).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The lecture lists <b>designing web interfaces</b> as an objective of HCI, next to designing GUIs (L1, Objective of HCI). The form, layout and navigation guidelines for web pages are also part of the course (L8, L9).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"UX Design(User Experience): covers a user’s overall experience with the product or website.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's definition: <b>UX Design</b> (User Experience) covers a user's overall experience with the product or website (L1, UI vs UX).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is the slide's own definition of UX Design. It is <b>UI Design</b> that refers to the interactivity, look and feel of a product screen or web page (L1, UI vs UX).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The three main components of HCI are the user, the computer, and the interaction between them.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture shows three parts meeting in HCI: the <b>User</b> (mental and physical capabilities), the <b>Computer / Machine</b> (input/output devices) and the <b>Interaction</b> (interfaces, HCI patterns, design, usability evaluation) (L1, What is HCI).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture's HCI figure has exactly these three parts: the User, the Computer / Machine and the Interaction between them (L1, What is HCI).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"HCI only applies to desktop computers and does not include mobile devices or other technologies.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The course applies HCI well beyond desktops: the Qualcomm touch-screen device for truckers (L2), mobile app design activities (L2, Activity), and the note that the gap between HCI and human factors engineering is shrinking because <b>more and more devices are becoming computerized</b> (L1).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. HCI covers all computerized devices. The lecture notes that <b>more and more devices are becoming computerized</b> (L1, HCI in a broader hierarchy), and the course uses examples such as the Qualcomm touch-screen device for truckers and a mobile app for football lovers (L2).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"According to the lecture, HCI deals with which three activities for interactive systems?\",\"o\":[[\"Design, Implementation and Evaluation\",\"Correct. The lecture says HCI deals with the Design, Implementation and Evaluation of interactive systems.\"],[\"Manufacturing, Shipping and Repair of hardware\",\"HCI studies the interaction between users and computers, not the hardware supply chain.\"],[\"Marketing, Sales and Customer support\",\"These are business activities, not the three activities the lecture lists for HCI.\"],[\"Coloring, Typography and Animation only\",\"These are visual details; HCI covers the whole design, implementation and evaluation of interactive systems.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In the lecture's hierarchy of fields, which of these is <b>NOT</b> a direct branch of Human Factors Engineering?\",\"o\":[[\"Industrial Design\",\"Industrial Design is one of the three branches of Human Factors Engineering.\"],[\"UX Design\",\"Correct. UX Design branches from HCI (HCI splits into UI, UX and Interaction design), not directly from Human Factors Engineering.\"],[\"HCI\",\"HCI is one of the three branches of Human Factors Engineering.\"],[\"Product Design\",\"Product Design is one of the three branches of Human Factors Engineering.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"The gap between HCI and human factors engineering is growing, because fewer devices are becoming computerized.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Both halves are reversed: the lecture says the gap is shrinking, because more devices (not fewer) are becoming computerized (L1, HCI hierarchy).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The gap is <b>shrinking</b>, because more devices are becoming computerized.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which statement matches the lecture's distinction between UX and HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"HCI dictates the interactions, while UX only studies them\",\"It is the other way round: UX dictates, HCI understands.\"],[\"UX and HCI are both only about the look and feel of a screen\",\"Look and feel is UI design. UX is the overall experience and HCI is about understanding interactions.\"],[\"UX is about dictating the interactions, while HCI is largely about understanding them\",\"Correct. The lecture states UX is about dictating the interactions and HCI is largely about understanding the interactions between humans and computers.\"],[\"HCI only covers the computer's input/output devices\",\"Input/output devices are only the \\\"computer\\\" part. HCI also covers the user and the interaction.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> Before drawing any screen, a team finds out that many of their users are elderly and often distracted, and they read earlier studies on human perception. Which component of the UI design process is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Design and prototyping\",\"Nothing is being designed or prototyped yet; the team is learning about users.\"],[\"User interface evaluation\",\"Evaluation tests and improves an existing design; here no design exists yet.\"],[\"Implementation\",\"Implementation is not one of the three UI design process components, and no code is being written.\"],[\"User research\",\"Correct. User research means learning about the users (skills, distractions, special needs like the elderly) and previous research on human capabilities and perception.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"One objective of HCI is to design for the environments users live and work in, such as an organization.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The objectives are to design for people, for tasks, and for the environments users live and work in (e.g. an organization).\"],[\"False\",\"Designing for the environment is explicitly one of the listed objectives of HCI.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"In an online Zoom lecture, the instructor is really interacting with the students, not with the computer.\\\" This example illustrates that:\",\"o\":[[\"The user really interacts with the task; the system is only the means\",\"Correct. This is the human-task interaction idea: the user is trying to achieve a task and the system is just the means.\"],[\"The computer should be the main focus of the user's attention\",\"The point is the opposite: the user should think about the task, not the tool.\"],[\"Zoom has a bad user interface\",\"The example is not a criticism of Zoom; it shows that users interact with the task.\"],[\"HCI is only about hardware\",\"The example is about the task and the people involved, not hardware.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"HCI has three parts: User, Computer/Machine, and Interaction. Interfaces, HCI patterns, design and usability evaluation belong to which part?\",\"o\":[[\"User\",\"The User part is about mental and physical capabilities.\"],[\"Interaction\",\"Correct. The Interaction part covers interfaces, HCI patterns, design and usability evaluation.\"],[\"Computer / Machine\",\"The Computer/Machine part is about input/output devices.\"],[\"Human Factors Engineering\",\"Human Factors Engineering is the parent field of HCI, not one of its three parts.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Like a sports referee, an interface is successful when users notice it a lot.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The referee comparison means the opposite.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Like a referee, an interface is successful when it goes <b>unnoticed</b>, so users can focus on the task (the invisible interface).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A doctor's online form asks for the address, phone, email and SSN about 3 times for each of 10 people. It takes over 30 minutes and then shows \\\"Internal server error\\\". In the lecture, this is an example of:\",\"o\":[[\"An invisible interface\",\"An invisible interface lets users focus on the task. This form forces the user to fight the tool.\"],[\"A good case of user research\",\"Asking for the same data repeatedly and then failing shows the users were not understood.\"],[\"A bad user experience (UX)\",\"Correct. The lecture gives this form as a bad UX example: repetitive, slow, and it ends with an error.\"],[\"Human factors engineering in industrial design\",\"Industrial design is a separate branch; this example is about a poor user experience on a web form.\"]],\"a\":2}]},{\"n\":2,\"title\":\"User Research in Practice: Truckers and MS Office\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Recap\",\"pts\":[\"Interaction design has 3 components: user research, design and prototyping, evaluation.\",\"Work with users instead of guessing (<b>user-centric design</b>). Aim for invisible interfaces (<b>task-oriented design</b>).\"]},{\"h\":\"Qualcomm device for truckers\",\"pts\":[\"The early version had <b>small buttons</b>.\",\"User research found that truckers often have <b>big hands</b> and often <b>wear gloves</b>.\",\"Better design: a <b>large touch screen</b> and a <b>stylus pen</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"The challenge of full-featured systems (MS Office)\",\"pts\":[\"Full-featured productivity apps include many features to meet all possible needs.\",\"More features lead to: the app feels complicated, things are harder to find or learn, and there is more focus on the tool than the task.\",\"<b>Bloatware</b>: a product with too many features, too many megabytes, too slow, too difficult to use, or simply too much.\",\"Office 97–2003: press called it \\\"bloated\\\"; a miles-long list of feature requests; people felt less in control; menus and toolbars were full and not scalable; people did not notice or use new features.\"]},{\"h\":\"Office 2000 mechanisms (and why they failed)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Adaptive (personalized) menus</b>: a top-level menu shows a short list of the most likely used commands; a <b>chevron</b> at the end expands the full menu.\",\"<b>Rafted toolbars</b>: two or more toolbars share one line; an algorithm predicts the least-used buttons and moves them to an <b>overflow</b> area.\",\"Why they failed: customization was not easy or accurate. Wrong short menus forced an extra scan of the full menu, so <b>scanning took twice as long</b>. They were later turned off by default.\",\"Rule: <b>\\\"Auto-customization, unless it does a perfect job, is usually worse than no customization at all.\\\"</b>\"]},{\"h\":\"From guesswork to data\",\"pts\":[\"Before 2003, decisions were mostly <b>guesswork</b>: \\\"based on feel, estimation, and guess work\\\".\",\"Office 2003 introduced the <b>CEIP</b> (Customer Experience Improvement Program): a balloon \\\"Help make Office better\\\" enrols users and collects <b>anonymous</b> usage and hardware data.\",\"For Office 2007 the team had <b>1.3+ billion sessions</b> of data. They got so much Word and Outlook data that <b>70% was thrown away</b>.\",\"They learned which commands are used often, which are used in sequence, which are used <b>7x more with the keyboard</b> than the mouse, how many documents people open at once, and how big screens are.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Qualcomm trucker device: problem and fix\",\"Small buttons, but truckers have big hands and wear gloves. Fix: large touch screen and stylus pen.\"],[\"Bloatware\",\"A product with too many features, megabytes, too slow, too difficult, or simply too much.\"],[\"Adaptive (personalized) menus\",\"A short list of likely-used commands; a chevron expands the full menu.\"],[\"Rafted toolbars\",\"Toolbars share a line; least-used buttons move to an overflow area.\"],[\"Auto-customization rule\",\"Unless it does a perfect job, it is usually worse than no customization at all.\"],[\"CEIP\",\"Customer Experience Improvement Program: collects anonymous usage data from Office 2003 users.\"],[\"Office 2007 data size\",\"More than 1.3 billion sessions; 70% of Word/Outlook data was thrown away.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Why did the adaptive menus and rafted toolbars fail?\",\"Customization was not easy or accurate. A bad short menu forced users to scan the full menu again (scanning took twice as long), adding complexity and inefficiency. They were later turned off by default.\"],[\"How did Microsoft base Office 2007 design on data instead of guessing?\",\"Through CEIP, which collected anonymous usage data (1.3B+ sessions): which commands are used, in what sequence, keyboard versus mouse, number of open documents and screen sizes.\"],[\"What problems come with increasing the number of features?\",\"The app feels complicated, it is harder to find or learn how to do things, and users focus more on the tool than on the task.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"What surprising finding did user research discover about truckers?\",\"o\":[[\"They preferred small keypads\",\"Small buttons were the problem, not a preference.\"],[\"They disliked touch screens\",\"The better design actually used a large touch screen.\"],[\"They often have big hands and wear gloves\",\"Correct. This is why the small buttons failed.\"],[\"They preferred voice-only systems\",\"Voice systems are not part of the Qualcomm case. Research found truckers have big hands and wear gloves, so the fix was a large touch screen with a stylus (L2, Qualcomm device).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"The early version of the Qualcomm device for truckers had:\",\"o\":[[\"Voice command input\",\"The trucker case never mentions voice input. The early device's problem was small buttons that big, gloved hands could not press (L2, Qualcomm device).\"],[\"Small buttons that were hard to use\",\"Correct. The early Qualcomm device had small buttons, which failed because user research showed truckers often have big hands and wear gloves (L2, Qualcomm device).\"],[\"A large touch screen\",\"The large touch screen with a stylus pen was the improved design that came after user research, not the early version (L2, Qualcomm device).\"],[\"No display at all\",\"The device did have an interface; its problem was small buttons. The fix kept a screen and made it a large touch screen (L2, Qualcomm device).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In adaptive menus, what expands the menu to show its full contents?\",\"o\":[[\"A chevron at the end of the menu\",\"Correct. An adaptive menu first shows a short list of the most likely used commands; clicking the chevron at its end expands the full menu (L2, Office 2000 mechanisms).\"],[\"A double right-click\",\"A double right-click is not part of adaptive menus. The lecture says a chevron at the end of the short menu expands it (L2, Office 2000 mechanisms).\"],[\"The overflow area\",\"The overflow area belongs to rafted toolbars.\"],[\"Holding the mouse for 5 seconds\",\"There is no hold-to-expand timer in the lecture. The full adaptive menu is opened by clicking the chevron at its end (L2, Office 2000 mechanisms).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Why did auto-customization in Office 2000 make scanning menus slower?\",\"o\":[[\"Menus had no icons\",\"Icons were never the issue. The problem was that a wrong short menu forced users to scan the full menu as well (L2, Office 2000 mechanisms).\"],[\"A wrong short menu forced users to scan the full menu again\",\"Correct. When the predicted short menu lacked the command, users scanned it, expanded it and scanned the full menu again, so scanning took about twice as long (L2, Office 2000 mechanisms).\"],[\"The CEIP program slowed the computer\",\"CEIP has nothing to do with Office 2000: it was introduced later, in Office 2003, to collect usage data, and it did not slow menu scanning (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"Toolbars were removed\",\"Toolbars were not removed: rafted toolbars shared one line and moved predicted least-used buttons to an overflow area (L2, Office 2000 mechanisms).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"How much Office 2007 usage data did the design team have?\",\"o\":[[\"1.3 million sessions\",\"Too small by a factor of 1000: the team had more than 1.3 billion sessions, not million (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"13 million sessions\",\"13 million is far below the real figure. For Office 2007 the team had more than 1.3 billion sessions of usage data (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"More than 1.3 billion sessions\",\"Correct. CEIP gave the Office 2007 team more than 1.3 billion sessions; there was so much Word and Outlook data that 70% was thrown away (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"Exactly 70 sessions\",\"70 comes from a different fact: 70% of the Word and Outlook data was thrown away. The total was more than 1.3 billion sessions (L2, From guesswork to data).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Before 2003, most Office design decisions were based on:\",\"o\":[[\"Usage data\",\"Usage data only became available with the CEIP in Office 2003. Before that, the team had no real data to base decisions on (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"Guesswork, feel and estimation\",\"Correct. The Office team admitted decisions before 2003 were \\\"based on feel, estimation, and guess work\\\", until CEIP brought real usage data (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"Usability labs only\",\"The lecture does not say decisions came from usability labs. It says they were based on feel, estimation and guesswork (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"Competitor analysis\",\"Competitor analysis is not mentioned. Pre-2003 decisions were based on feel, estimation and guesswork, which CEIP data later replaced (L2, From guesswork to data).\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> User research showed that truckers often have big hands and often wear gloves. What was the better design for the Qualcomm device?\",\"o\":[[\"Smaller buttons placed closer together\",\"Small buttons were the problem in the early version.\"],[\"A voice-only device with no screen\",\"The lecture does not mention voice input; the redesign used a screen.\"],[\"A physical keyboard with more keys\",\"More keys would mean even smaller targets for gloved hands.\"],[\"A large touch screen and a stylus pen\",\"Correct. The better design was a large touch screen with a stylus pen.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"What is <b>bloatware</b> according to the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"A product with too many features, too many megabytes, too slow, too difficult to use, or simply too much\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of bloatware.\"],[\"A program that collects anonymous usage data\",\"That describes the CEIP, which Microsoft used to replace guesswork with data.\"],[\"A menu that shows only the most likely used commands\",\"That is an adaptive (personalized) menu.\"],[\"Two or more toolbars sharing one line\",\"Two or more toolbars sharing one line is a rafted toolbar, an Office 2000 mechanism. Bloatware is a product with too many features or simply too much (L2, Full-featured systems).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"In Office 2000, what were <b>rafted toolbars</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"Menus that show only a short list of commands with a chevron\",\"That is the adaptive (personalized) menu.\"],[\"Two or more toolbars sharing one line, with an algorithm moving the predicted least-used buttons to an overflow area\",\"Correct. Rafted toolbars put two or more toolbars on one line, and an algorithm predicted the least-used buttons and moved them to an overflow area (L2, Office 2000 mechanisms).\"],[\"Toolbars that were removed completely from the window\",\"The toolbars stayed; they just shared a line and hid some buttons.\"],[\"Toolbars that could only be used from the keyboard\",\"Nothing in the lecture says that; keyboard usage was a CEIP finding.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Office 2000's adaptive menus and rafted toolbars were later turned off by default.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Customization was not easy or accurate, so these mechanisms were later turned off by default.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says they failed and were turned off by default.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of these is <b>NOT</b> a consequence of adding more and more features to a full-featured app, according to the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"The app feels complicated\",\"This is listed as a consequence of more features.\"],[\"Things are harder to find or learn\",\"This is listed as a consequence of more features.\"],[\"Users feel more in control\",\"Correct. The opposite happened: with Office 97–2003, people felt <b>less</b> in control.\"],[\"There is more focus on the tool than on the task\",\"This is listed as a consequence of more features.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"What did the CEIP (Customer Experience Improvement Program), introduced in Office 2003, collect?\",\"o\":[[\"Users' names and email addresses so the team could contact them\",\"CEIP collected no names or emails: its usage and hardware data was anonymous, gathered from users who joined via the \\\"Help make Office better\\\" balloon (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"Video recordings of users working\",\"The lecture does not mention video; CEIP collected usage and hardware data.\"],[\"Only the press reviews calling Office \\\"bloated\\\"\",\"Press opinion was part of the problem, not CEIP data.\"],[\"Anonymous usage and hardware data from users who joined through the \\\"Help make Office better\\\" balloon\",\"Correct. A balloon enrolled users and the CEIP collected anonymous usage and hardware data.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"CEIP data showed that some commands are used about how many times more with the keyboard than with the mouse?\",\"o\":[[\"7 times\",\"Correct. The team learned that some commands are used 7x more with the keyboard than the mouse.\"],[\"2 times\",\"\\\"Twice\\\" relates to wrong short menus making scanning take twice as long, not keyboard use.\"],[\"70 times\",\"70 is the percentage of Word and Outlook data that was thrown away.\"],[\"1.3 times\",\"1.3 relates to the 1.3+ billion sessions of data, not keyboard use.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"For Office 2007, the team kept every bit of the Word and Outlook usage data they collected.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"They did not keep it all: they got so much Word and Outlook data that 70% was thrown away (L2, From guesswork to data).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. They got so much Word and Outlook data that <b>70% was thrown away</b>.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A music app automatically hides the options it predicts you rarely use. Its guesses are often wrong, so users end up scanning the full list again. Which lesson from the Office 2000 case applies?\",\"o\":[[\"More features always make an app better\",\"The Office case showed that too many features lead to bloatware.\"],[\"\\\"Auto-customization, unless it does a perfect job, is usually worse than no customization at all.\\\"\",\"Correct. This is the rule drawn from adaptive menus and rafted toolbars, where wrong predictions made scanning take twice as long.\"],[\"Design decisions should be based on feel and estimation\",\"The Office team moved away from guesswork to data.\"],[\"Use a large touch screen and stylus\",\"That is the Qualcomm truckers solution, not the auto-customization lesson.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"In Office 97–2003, menus and toolbars were full and not scalable, and people often did not notice or use new features.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. These are among the problems listed for Office 97–2003.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture lists exactly these problems for Office 97–2003.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":3,\"title\":\"Design Life Cycle and Types of User Research\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"User-centered design\",\"pts\":[\"We used to design systems to meet some <b>functional specification</b>.\",\"<b>User-centered design</b>: design that takes the user's needs into consideration at <b>every step</b>.\",\"Ignoring user research is common. Involve users so decisions are based on reality, not guesses.\"]},{\"h\":\"Design life cycle (Joyner)\",\"pts\":[\"An <b>iterative</b> process with four phases: <b>Need finding → Design alternatives → Prototyping → Evaluation</b> → back to need finding.\",\"There is no phase called \\\"user research\\\" because it is part of every phase.\"]},{\"h\":\"User-centered design process (Google)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Understand → Specify → Design → Evaluate</b>, and iteration is key.\",\"<b>Understand</b>: how users experience the product (a lot of research). <b>Specify</b>: the most important user problem to solve. <b>Design</b>: solutions, then start building. <b>Evaluate</b>: test with real users.\"]},{\"h\":\"Where is \\\"user research\\\"?\",\"pts\":[\"Joyner and Google do not give it a separate step because it is <b>integral to every phase</b>, and to avoid seeing it as a one-time step at the beginning.\",\"Joyner: <b>\\\"you need users early and often\\\"</b>.\",\"Google: user research is continuous and happens <b>before, during and after</b> the design phase.\",\"Making it an explicit component still helps: it avoids underestimating it, and it is increasingly a role for specialized professionals.\"]},{\"h\":\"Who does UI/UX design?\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>software engineer</b> designing the whole system, often knowing little about HCI.\",\"A <b>UI/UX designer</b> on the development team, increasingly common in small and medium companies.\",\"<b>Specialists</b> for each role (UX researcher, UX designer, visual designer, UX engineer) in big companies like Google.\"]},{\"h\":\"Types of user research by WHEN\",\"pts\":[\"<b>1. Foundational (strategic / generative)</b>: happens <b>before anything is designed</b>. The goal is to identify user problems and what to build. It involves talking to users about their pain points and reveals opportunities the team would not think of.\",\"<b>2. Tactical (design)</b>: happens <b>during the design phase</b>. It informs how the product should be built and needs user feedback on prototypes.\",\"<b>3. Post-launch</b>: Did we succeed? Does the system meet user needs?\"]},{\"h\":\"Types of user research by WHO\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Primary research</b>: research you conduct yourself. Methods: interviews, focus groups, surveys, usability studies, participant observations.\",\"<b>Secondary research</b>: research done by someone else. Sources: books, journals and similar.\",\"Secondary research is important early to gather statistics, facts and figures. It saves effort and time and backs up primary findings. It is often done by experienced researchers at credible organizations, which is hard to replicate.\",\"Many practitioners do not know how to use it. Courses (psychology, cognition) and HCI/UX communities help.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"User-centered design\",\"Design that considers the user's needs at every step.\"],[\"Joyner's design life cycle\",\"Need finding → Design alternatives → Prototyping → Evaluation (iterative).\"],[\"Google UCD process\",\"Understand → Specify → Design → Evaluate (iteration is key).\"],[\"Joyner's quote about users\",\"\\\"You need users early and often.\\\"\"],[\"Foundational research\",\"Strategic/generative. Before anything is designed; finds user problems and what to build.\"],[\"Tactical research\",\"Design research. During the design phase; uses prototype feedback to decide how to build.\"],[\"Post-launch research\",\"After release: did we succeed and does it meet user needs?\"],[\"Primary research\",\"Research you conduct yourself: interviews, focus groups, surveys, usability studies, observations.\"],[\"Secondary research\",\"Research done by someone else: books, journals and similar.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What is meant by user research based on \\\"When\\\"?\",\"1) Foundational (strategic/generative): before anything is designed, to find user problems and what to build. 2) Tactical (design): during design, using prototype feedback to decide how to build. 3) Post-launch: after launch, to check whether we succeeded and the system meets user needs.\"],[\"Why don't Joyner and Google show \\\"user research\\\" as a separate phase?\",\"They consider it an integral part of every phase and want to avoid the premature view that it is a single step at the beginning. Google says it happens before, during and after design.\"],[\"Who does UI/UX design in a company?\",\"A software engineer who designs the whole system; a UI/UX designer on the development team (common in small and medium companies); or several specialists (UX researcher, UX designer, visual designer, UX engineer) in big companies.\"],[\"Describe the design life cycle according to Joyner.\",\"Joyner's design life cycle is an <b>iterative process with four phases</b>: 1) Need finding 2) Design alternatives 3) Prototyping 4) Evaluation, then back to need finding. There is no separate \\\"user research\\\" phase because user research is an integral part of every phase (Joyner: \\\"you need users early and often\\\").\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"],[\"Explain the steps of Google's user-centered design process.\",\"Understand, Specify, Design, Evaluate, and <b>iteration is key</b> as you go through the process.<br>1) <b>Understand</b>: how users experience the product (a lot of research).<br>2) <b>Specify</b>: the most important user problem to solve.<br>3) <b>Design</b>: solutions to the user's problem, and start building.<br>4) <b>Evaluate</b>: test with real users.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"],[\"What are the types of user research based on \\\"Who\\\"? Mention the common methods or sources of each.\",\"1) <b>Primary research</b>: research that you conduct yourself. Common methods: interviews, focus groups, surveys, usability studies, participant observations.<br>2) <b>Secondary research</b>: research done by someone else. Common sources of information: books, journals and similar.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"],[\"Why is secondary research important, and how can practitioners benefit from it better?\",\"1) It is important especially <b>early in the project</b> to gather useful statistics, facts and figures about the users.<br>2) If such information exists, it <b>saves enormous effort and time</b> and <b>backs up primary research findings</b>.<br>3) Its findings are usually produced by experienced, specialized researchers in credible research organizations, so they are difficult for you to replicate.<br>4) Many practitioners are not aware of it or do not know how to benefit from available information relevant to their project's users. Courses and training on things like psychology and cognition, and being a member of HCI and UX communities, help.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"When does foundational (strategic/generative) research take place?\",\"o\":[[\"Only during usability testing\",\"Usability tests belong to tactical or post-launch work.\"],[\"After a product is fully designed\",\"Research after the product is designed and released is post-launch research (did we succeed?). Foundational research happens before anything is designed (L3, Types by WHEN).\"],[\"During marketing campaigns\",\"Marketing campaigns are not one of the research timings. The three are foundational (before design), tactical (during design) and post-launch (L3, Types by WHEN).\"],[\"Before anything is designed\",\"Correct. It identifies problems and what to build.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"Tactical research takes place after the design phase.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Tactical (design) research takes place DURING the design phase. After launch is post-launch research.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Tactical research happens during design and uses feedback on prototypes.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26 (also 2024/25)\"},{\"q\":\"Joyner's design life cycle has which four phases?\",\"o\":[[\"Understand, Specify, Design, Evaluate\",\"That is Google's user-centered design process.\"],[\"Need finding, Design alternatives, Prototyping, Evaluation\",\"Correct. Joyner's iterative design life cycle is Need finding → Design alternatives → Prototyping → Evaluation, then back to need finding (L3, Design life cycle).\"],[\"Plan, Build, Test, Deploy\",\"Plan, Build, Test, Deploy is a generic software development cycle. Joyner's cycle is Need finding, Design alternatives, Prototyping, Evaluation (L3, Design life cycle).\"],[\"Research, Wireframe, Code, Launch\",\"These are not Joyner's phases; Joyner has no coding or launch phase. His cycle is Need finding, Design alternatives, Prototyping, Evaluation (L3, Design life cycle).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Primary research is done by someone else.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Research done by someone else (books, journals) is secondary research. Primary research is research you conduct yourself, such as interviews or surveys (L3, Types by WHO).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Primary research is research you conduct yourself.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT a method of primary research?\",\"o\":[[\"Literature reviews\",\"Correct. Reading existing literature is secondary research.\"],[\"Focus groups\",\"Focus groups are primary research: you run them yourself with users. The lecture lists them with interviews, surveys, usability studies and observations (L3, Types by WHO).\"],[\"Interviews\",\"Interviews are a primary method, since you conduct them yourself. Only the literature review relies on research done by someone else (L3, Types by WHO).\"],[\"Surveys\",\"Surveys are a primary method: you design and run them yourself. Reading existing literature is the secondary method here (L3, Types by WHO).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Focus groups and interviews are examples of primary research methods.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Both are listed as common primary methods.\"],[\"False\",\"They are explicitly listed under primary research.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"Analyzing academic papers and previous research studies is an example of secondary research.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Using research done by someone else is secondary research.\"],[\"False\",\"Papers written by others are secondary sources.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"Survey is a common method used in primary research.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Surveys are listed among primary methods.\"],[\"False\",\"Surveys are one of the listed primary methods.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"According to Google, user research takes place:\",\"o\":[[\"Only before design\",\"Too narrow: \\\"before design\\\" is only foundational research. Google says user research is continuous: before, during and after design (L3, Where is user research?).\"],[\"Only after launch\",\"Too narrow: after launch is only post-launch research. Google says user research happens before, during and after the design phase (L3, Where is user research?).\"],[\"Before, during and after the design phase\",\"Correct. Google calls it a continuous part of the life cycle.\"],[\"Only when there is budget\",\"The lecture never ties research to budget. Google treats it as a continuous part of the life cycle, before, during and after design (L3, Where is user research?).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a source of secondary research in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Academic journals\",\"Journals are named in the lecture as a common source of secondary research, which is research done by someone else (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Online research papers\",\"Published research papers are research done by someone else, so they are secondary research, like the books and journals the lecture lists (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Interview\",\"Correct. An interview is research you conduct yourself, so it is a <b>primary</b> research method, not a secondary source (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Books on interface design\",\"Books are named in the lecture as a common source of secondary research (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is an example of secondary research in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Reading previous research papers on user experience\",\"Correct. Secondary research is research done by someone else, found in sources such as books and journals (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Conducting a focus group discussion\",\"Focus groups are research you conduct yourself, listed as a <b>primary</b> research method (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Performing usability testing\",\"Usability studies are listed as a <b>primary</b> research method, because you run them yourself (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Observing users interacting with a system\",\"Participant observation is listed as a <b>primary</b> research method, because you collect the data yourself (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a key source of secondary research in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Interviews with users\",\"Interviews are research you conduct yourself, so they are a <b>primary</b> research method (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Case studies\",\"A case study can be either kind: one you run yourself is primary, and one you read that someone else published is secondary. The sources the lecture actually names for secondary research are books and journals, so that is the best answer (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Prototype testing\",\"Testing a prototype with users is research you conduct yourself, like the usability studies listed under <b>primary</b> research (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"books and journals\",\"Correct. Secondary research is research done by someone else, and its common sources of information are <b>books and journals</b> (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Why is secondary research useful in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"It always gives real-time user feedback\",\"Secondary research is research done by someone else and uses existing data, which can even be too old; it does not give live feedback from your users (L3, Types of user research by Who; L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"It eliminates the need for primary research\",\"Secondary research <b>backs up</b> primary research findings; it does not replace them (L3, Secondary research).\"],[\"It provides background knowledge, cheap and quick\",\"Correct. Early in a project it gathers useful statistics, facts and figures about users, and it is usually cheap and quick, saving enormous effort and time (L3, Secondary research; L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"It focuses only on new technologies\",\"Secondary research answers questions about human abilities and limitations (e.g. color-blindness facts) and about user groups with common issues, not only about technology (L4, Secondary research benefits).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"In HCI research, secondary data is typically collected from:\",\"o\":[[\"Books, journals, and past studies\",\"Correct. Secondary research is research done by someone else, and its common sources are <b>books and journals</b> (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Real-time behavioral observations\",\"Watching users yourself is participant observation, a <b>primary</b> research method (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Direct user interactions\",\"Working directly with users (interviews, focus groups, usability studies) is research you conduct yourself, so it is <b>primary</b> research (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Experiments conducted by the researcher\",\"Experiments you run yourself are listed as examples of <b>primary</b> research, where the data is collected by you (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Secondary research is useful because it allows researchers to:\",\"o\":[[\"Conduct interviews directly with customers\",\"Interviews you conduct yourself are <b>primary</b> research; secondary research is research done by someone else (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Avoid data analysis\",\"The lecture never says this. Secondary research gathers statistics, facts and figures and backs up primary findings; it does not remove the need to analyse data (L3, Secondary research).\"],[\"Use already existing data to save time\",\"Correct. Secondary research looks for <b>existing data</b>; 'if such information exists, it saves enormous effort and time' (L3, Secondary research; L4, Primary vs secondary table).\"],[\"Collect first-hand information\",\"Collecting data yourself is primary research ('research that you conduct yourself'); secondary data was collected by someone else (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main goal of foundational user research?\",\"o\":[[\"To test visual elements of the interface\",\"Foundational research happens before anything is designed, so there is no interface to test yet; testing with prototypes belongs to tactical (design) research (L3, Types of user research by When).\"],[\"To code the software faster\",\"User research is about understanding users and their problems, not coding speed (L3, Types of user research by When).\"],[\"To identify user problems and what should be built to solve them\",\"Correct. Foundational (strategic/generative) research has the 'goal to <b>identify user problems and what should we build</b> to solve them' (L3, Types of user research by When).\"],[\"To compare competitors’ marketing\",\"The lecture describes foundational research as talking with users and identifying their pain points, not studying competitors' marketing (L3, Types of user research by When).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"A business can benefit from secondary research because it:\",\"o\":[[\"Requires field experiments\",\"Experiments are listed under primary research (data you collect yourself). Secondary research uses data collected by someone else (L4, Primary vs secondary table).\"],[\"Always produces original data\",\"Secondary research is research done by someone else from sources like books and journals, so it is not original data (L3, Types of user research based on Who).\"],[\"Helps compare results with existing studies\",\"Correct. Secondary research uses existing work by others, and the lecture says it saves effort and time and <b>backs up primary research findings</b> (L3, Secondary Research).\"],[\"Is less reliable than primary data\",\"This is not a benefit, and the lecture does not say it: secondary findings are usually done by experienced, specialized researchers that are difficult to replicate. Its stated drawback is data that is too old or not specific enough (L3, Secondary Research; L4 table).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Observing user behavior in usability testing is considered secondary research.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Usability studies and participant observations are listed as methods of <b>primary</b> research, which is research you conduct yourself (L3, Types of user research based on Who).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Observing users in a usability study is research you conduct yourself, so it is <b>primary</b> research. The lecture lists usability studies and participant observations as primary methods; secondary research is research done by someone else, such as books and journals (L3, Types of user research based on Who).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Using secondary research allows researchers to access large amounts of historical data.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Secondary research uses <b>existing data</b> collected by someone else (books, journals and similar). It is important early in a project to gather statistics, facts and figures about users, and it saves enormous effort and time (L3, Secondary research; L4, Primary vs secondary).\"],[\"False\",\"Secondary research is exactly the use of existing data done by someone else: statistics, facts and figures from books, journals and research organizations (L3, Secondary research). Its known drawback is that this existing data can be too old, which confirms it is data from the past (L4, Primary vs secondary).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Conducting user interviews is an example of secondary research.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Interviews are listed as a method of <b>primary</b> research, research that you conduct yourself, not secondary research (L3, Types of user research based on Who).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Conducting interviews yourself is <b>primary</b> research; the lecture lists interviews first among primary methods. Secondary research is research done by someone else, such as books and journals (L3, Types of user research based on Who).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Secondary research cannot be used in HCI research.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Secondary research is one of the two types of user research by \\\"Who\\\" in the course, and it is important early in a project to gather statistics, facts and figures about the users (L3, Secondary research; L4, Secondary research benefits).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. <b>Secondary research</b> (research done by someone else, e.g. books and journals) is a type of user research in the course. It saves effort and time, backs up primary findings, and answers questions about human abilities and limitations, such as color blindness (L3, Secondary research; L4, Secondary research benefits).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"What is the correct order of Google's user-centered design process?\",\"o\":[[\"Need finding → Design alternatives → Prototyping → Evaluation\",\"That is Joyner's design life cycle, not Google's process.\"],[\"Specify → Understand → Design → Evaluate\",\"You must understand users before specifying the problem.\"],[\"Understand → Specify → Design → Evaluate\",\"Correct. Google's process is Understand, Specify, Design, Evaluate, and iteration is key.\"],[\"Design → Evaluate → Understand → Specify\",\"Designing before understanding users is exactly what user-centered design avoids.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"In Google's process, what happens in the <b>Specify</b> step?\",\"o\":[[\"Testing with real users\",\"Testing with real users is the Evaluate step, the last of Understand → Specify → Design → Evaluate (L3, Google UCD process).\"],[\"Designing solutions and starting to build\",\"Designing solutions and starting to build is the Design step, which comes after Specify has chosen the problem to solve (L3, Google UCD process).\"],[\"Learning how users experience the product\",\"Learning how users experience the product is the Understand step, the first step and the one with the most research (L3, Google UCD process).\"],[\"Identifying the most important user problem to solve\",\"Correct. Specify means choosing the most important user problem to solve.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"User-centered design means designing a system to meet a functional specification.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Designing to a functional specification is how systems <i>used</i> to be designed.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. User-centered design takes the user's needs into consideration at <b>every step</b>.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Why do Joyner and Google NOT give \\\"user research\\\" its own separate step?\",\"o\":[[\"Because it is part of every phase, and they want to avoid it being seen as a one-time step at the beginning\",\"Correct. User research is integral to every phase, so it is not a single step.\"],[\"Because user research is not needed in an iterative process\",\"Both say users are needed throughout, not that research is unnecessary.\"],[\"Because only the marketing team does user research\",\"The lecture says nothing of the kind; research is integral to design.\"],[\"Because secondary research replaces it completely\",\"Secondary research supports primary research; it does not replace user research.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Complete Joyner's quote: \\\"you need users ___\\\".\",\"o\":[[\"only at the end\",\"Waiting until the end is what Joyner warns against.\"],[\"early and often\",\"Correct. Joyner says \\\"you need users early and often\\\".\"],[\"once at the beginning\",\"This is the \\\"one-time step\\\" view that the lecture rejects.\"],[\"only when there is budget\",\"This is not Joyner's quote; users are needed throughout.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> Before any screen exists, a team talks to delivery drivers about their daily pain points to decide what product to build. Which type of research is this (by WHEN)?\",\"o\":[[\"Tactical (design) research\",\"Tactical research happens during design and needs feedback on prototypes.\"],[\"Post-launch research\",\"Post-launch research happens after release to check success.\"],[\"Foundational (strategic / generative) research\",\"Correct. It happens before anything is designed, talks to users about pain points, and decides what to build.\"],[\"Secondary research\",\"The team is talking to users themselves, so it is primary; \\\"secondary\\\" also classifies by WHO, not WHEN.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"Post-launch research asks questions like: Did we succeed? Does the system meet user needs?\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. These are exactly the questions of post-launch research.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture defines post-launch research by these questions.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> Midway through the design phase, a team shows two prototypes of a checkout screen to users to decide how it should be built. Which type of research is this (by WHEN)?\",\"o\":[[\"Foundational research\",\"Foundational research happens before anything is designed.\"],[\"Post-launch research\",\"The product has not been launched yet.\"],[\"Secondary research\",\"The team collects feedback themselves, and secondary is a WHO category.\"],[\"Tactical (design) research\",\"Correct. Tactical research happens during design, informs how the product should be built, and uses feedback on prototypes.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Why is secondary research important early in a project?\",\"o\":[[\"It gathers statistics, facts and figures, saving time and effort and backing up primary findings\",\"Correct. The lecture lists these benefits of secondary research early on.\"],[\"It is always more specific to your needs than primary research\",\"Primary research is the one specific to your needs.\"],[\"It removes the need to ever talk to users\",\"Secondary research backs up primary findings; it does not replace them.\"],[\"Primary research is not allowed early in a project\",\"Foundational research is primary research done before anything is designed.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Even though Joyner and Google don't give it a separate step, making user research an explicit component still helps, because it avoids underestimating it.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says an explicit component avoids underestimating it, and it is increasingly a role for specialists.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture lists benefits of making user research explicit.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":4,\"title\":\"Qualitative vs Quantitative, Privacy and Surveys\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Secondary research benefits\",\"pts\":[\"Answers questions about <b>human abilities and limitations</b>. Example: designing for color-blind users using color-blindness facts.\",\"Answers questions about <b>user groups with common issues</b>. Example: an app for a poor country where users likely have <b>low-end devices</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Types of user research by WHAT\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Quantitative</b>: data of <b>numeric</b> nature (measures, counts). Easier for engineers and technical people.\",\"<b>Qualitative</b>: observations about <b>why and how</b> things happen, often based on interviews. Harder for technical people, but it is what we need for real understanding.\"]},{\"h\":\"Primary vs secondary research\",\"table\":[[\"\",\"Primary\",\"Secondary\"],[\"Data collected by\",\"You (or someone you hire)\",\"Someone else\"],[\"Examples\",\"Surveys, focus groups, interviews, observations, experiments\",\"Looking for existing data\"],[\"Qualitative or quantitative?\",\"Can be either\",\"Can be either\"],[\"Key benefits\",\"Specific to your needs, and you control the quality\",\"Usually cheap and quick\"],[\"Key disadvantages\",\"Usually costs more and takes longer\",\"Data can be too old and/or not specific enough\"]]},{\"h\":\"General tips for research methods\",\"pts\":[\"User research should be <b>planned ahead</b>. For an interview, write a <b>script</b> of questions beforehand instead of asking random questions.\",\"Planning is not strictness: be flexible during the activity (<b>the script is not a bible</b>).\",\"Methods are not alternatives. Use a <b>combination</b> in a relevant order.\",\"<b>Testing and iteration</b> improve any technique.\",\"<b>Privacy and security</b> of user data are essential.\"]},{\"h\":\"Privacy and security\",\"pts\":[\"Only collect the data you need. Do not associate collected data with PII or SPII. Do not keep data after you no longer need it.\",\"<b>PII (Personally Identifiable Information)</b>: details that could identify a user: <b>names, home address, email address, phone number</b>.\",\"<b>SPII (Sensitive PII)</b>: data that could cause financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination if lost or stolen: <b>social security number, driver's license, passport numbers, financial account numbers, date of birth</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"1) Surveys\",\"pts\":[\"Quick responses from a <b>large number of users</b> in a short time (a broad view). Often online and easy to create. One of the <b>cheapest</b> methods.\",\"Can be an initial step for insights on motivations, preferences, priorities and pain points, to plan later activities such as interviews. Can also follow an interview or usability study.\",\"Tips: <b>keep it as small as possible</b> (too many questions hurt response rate and reliability); ask only what you need and will use; <b>be aware of bias</b>; get feedback from participants about the survey.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Quantitative research\",\"Numeric data: measures and counts. Easier for technical people.\"],[\"Qualitative research\",\"Observations about why and how things happen, often from interviews.\"],[\"PII examples\",\"Names, home address, email address, phone number.\"],[\"SPII examples\",\"SSN, driver's license, passport number, financial account numbers, date of birth.\"],[\"Primary research: benefit / drawback\",\"Specific to your needs and you control quality / costs more and takes longer.\"],[\"Secondary research: benefit / drawback\",\"Cheap and quick / may be too old or not specific enough.\"],[\"\\\"The script is not a bible\\\"\",\"Plan ahead, but stay flexible during the activity.\"],[\"Survey strengths\",\"Many users, short time, online, cheap; a broad view.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Compare primary research and secondary research.\",\"Primary is collected by you (surveys, focus groups, interviews, observations, experiments); it is specific to your needs and you control quality, but it costs more and takes longer. Secondary is collected by someone else (existing data such as books and journals); it is cheap and quick, but it may be too old or not specific enough. Both can be qualitative or quantitative.\"],[\"What are the rules for privacy and security of user data?\",\"Collect only what you need, do not associate collected data with PII or SPII, and do not keep data stored after it is no longer needed.\"],[\"Give general tips for conducting user research.\",\"Plan ahead (e.g. an interview script); be flexible (the script is not a bible); combine methods in a relevant order; improve through testing and iteration; protect privacy and security of user data.\"],[\"Give tips for a good survey design.\",\"Keep it as small as possible, since too many questions reduce the response rate and reliability. Ask the minimum questions for data you will use. Be aware of bias. Get feedback from participants about the survey.\"],[\"Differentiate between quantitative and qualitative research (types of user research based on \\\"What\\\").\",\"<b>Quantitative research</b>: involves data of numeric nature, e.g. measures and counts. It is easier for engineers and technical people to handle.<br><b>Qualitative research</b>: focuses on observations about why and how things happen, and is often based on interviews. Qualitative analysis is more difficult for technical people, but it is what we need for real understanding.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"],[\"Differentiate between PII and SPII, giving examples of each.\",\"<b>PII</b> (Personally Identifiable Information): specific details that could be used to identify a user, e.g. names, home address, email address, phone number.<br><b>SPII</b> (Sensitive Personally Identifiable Information): data that, if lost, compromised or stolen, could cause your users financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination, e.g. social security number, driver's license, passport numbers, financial account numbers, date of birth.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"],[\"What are surveys? Describe them as a user research method.\",\"A survey is a powerful way to gather quick responses in a short time from a large number of users (a <b>broad view</b>).<br>1) It is often done online and easily created.<br>2) It is one of the <b>cheapest</b> methods of user research.<br>3) It can be an initial step to gather general insights about users' motivations, preferences, priorities and pain points; its findings help in planning later activities such as interviews.<br>4) It can also follow another activity (an interview or a usability study), using the findings of the earlier method to plan the survey.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"Sensitive Personally Identifiable Information (SPII) includes:\",\"o\":[[\"Names\",\"Names are ordinary PII: they identify a user but are not in the sensitive list. SPII is data whose loss could cause financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Home address\",\"Home address is listed as PII, together with names, email and phone. It is not among the SPII examples like SSN or financial account numbers (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Email address\",\"Email address is plain PII in the lecture's list. SPII covers SSN, driver's license, passport, financial account numbers and date of birth (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Financial account numbers\",\"Correct. Losing these could cause financial harm.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"PII is specific details that could be used to identify a user such as names, phone number.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. PII is details that could identify a user, and the lecture's examples are names, home address, email address and phone number (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement is true: it matches the lecture's PII definition, and names and phone number are both in its PII examples (L4, Privacy and security).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"Qualitative research involves data of numeric nature.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Numeric data (measures, counts) defines quantitative research. Qualitative research is observations about why and how things happen (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Qualitative research is about why and how, not numbers.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25 & 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Data that includes descriptions from interviewees is:\",\"o\":[[\"Quantitative data\",\"Quantitative data is numeric: measures and counts. Interviewees' descriptions explain why and how, which makes them qualitative (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"Qualitative data\",\"Correct. Descriptions and explanations are qualitative.\"],[\"Average data\",\"\\\"Average data\\\" is not a type of research data. By WHAT, research is only quantitative (numeric) or qualitative (why and how) (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"Gathering data\",\"\\\"Gathering data\\\" is not a data type; gathering is just collecting. Descriptions from interviews are qualitative data (L4, Types by WHAT).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"Quantitative research is often easier for:\",\"o\":[[\"Designers only\",\"The lecture does not single out designers. It says quantitative (numeric) research is easier for engineers and technical people (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"Engineers and technical people\",\"Correct. Quantitative research is numeric (measures, counts), so the lecture says it is easier for engineers and technical people; qualitative is harder for them (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"Marketing teams only\",\"Marketing teams are not mentioned. The lecture says quantitative research is easier for engineers and technical people (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"Children\",\"Children are not part of this point at all. Quantitative research is said to be easier for engineers and technical people (L4, Types by WHAT).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Why is privacy and security important in user research?\",\"o\":[[\"It ensures compliance and protects user data\",\"Correct. Privacy and security of user data are essential: collect only what you need, avoid linking it to PII/SPII and delete it when no longer needed (L4, General tips).\"],[\"It makes research faster\",\"Privacy adds care; it does not speed things up.\"],[\"It allows sharing all data publicly\",\"The opposite: the privacy guidelines limit what you collect, keep it apart from PII/SPII, and delete it after use, so nothing is shared publicly (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"It reduces the need for interviews\",\"Privacy does not replace any method; interviews are still used. Privacy matters because it protects user data (L4, Privacy and security).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement reflects good practice in user research?\",\"o\":[[\"Prepare questions ahead but adapt as needed during the session\",\"Correct. Plan ahead, but the script is not a bible.\"],[\"Never change the script under any circumstance\",\"The lecture says planning is not strictness: \\\"the script is not a bible\\\", so you should stay flexible during the session (L4, General tips).\"],[\"Use only quantitative methods\",\"Combine methods; qualitative is needed for real understanding.\"],[\"Focus on convenience over privacy\",\"Privacy and security of user data are listed as essential, so they must never be traded for convenience (L4, General tips).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Before a user interview, what should researchers do?\",\"o\":[[\"Ask random questions without preparation\",\"The lecture says to plan ahead and write a script of questions instead of asking random questions (L4, General tips).\"],[\"Write a script of questions to guide the interview\",\"Correct. User research should be planned ahead: for an interview, write a script of questions beforehand, while staying flexible since the script is not a bible (L4, General tips).\"],[\"Ignore the interviewee's context\",\"Ignoring context is not a preparation step. The lecture's advice is to plan ahead with a script of questions (L4, General tips).\"],[\"Focus only on technical details\",\"User research is about the user's needs and pain points, not only technical details. The preparation step is writing a question script (L4, General tips).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"Survey is more expensive than interview.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Surveys are one of the cheapest methods; interviews are more expensive.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Interviews are synchronous and often need incentives.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"A well-designed survey should take a long time to complete to ensure detailed responses.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Long surveys hurt response rate and reliability.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The survey tip is to keep it as small as possible, because too many questions reduce the response rate and the reliability of the data (L4, Surveys).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Asking too many questions in a survey can:\",\"o\":[[\"Make analysis faster\",\"More questions give more data to process, so analysis becomes slower. The real effect is a lower response rate and less reliable data (L4, Surveys).\"],[\"Make it more accurate automatically\",\"More questions do not add accuracy: tired respondents answer worse, which lowers reliability. That is why surveys should be as small as possible (L4, Surveys).\"],[\"Improve user engagement\",\"Long surveys tire respondents, so fewer people finish them. The lecture says too many questions reduce the response rate (L4, Surveys).\"],[\"Reduce the response rate and affect reliability of data\",\"Correct. The lecture warns that too many questions reduce the response rate and affect reliability, so keep the survey as small as possible (L4, Surveys).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a quantitative primary research method in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Focus group discussion\",\"A focus group is primary research, but it is a group discussion that gives descriptions and opinions; research based on interviews is <b>qualitative</b> (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Reading published usability reports\",\"Reading reports someone else published is <b>secondary</b> research, not primary (L3, Types of user research by Who).\"],[\"Interviewing experts in HCI\",\"Interviews are primary research, but qualitative research is \\\"often based on interviews\\\", so this is <b>qualitative</b>, not quantitative (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"collecting and testing data of numeric nature\",\"Correct. Quantitative research involves data of numeric nature, such as measures and counts; when you collect it yourself it is primary research (L4, Types of user research by What).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main difference between primary and secondary research in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Secondary research is always more reliable than primary research\",\"The course says secondary data can be too old and/or not specific enough, while in primary research you control the quality (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"There is no difference between them\",\"The lecture contrasts them: primary is collected by you, secondary by someone else, with different benefits and disadvantages (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"Primary research collects new data, while secondary research uses existing data\",\"Correct. Primary data is collected by you (surveys, interviews, observations, experiments), while secondary research means looking for <b>existing data</b> collected by someone else (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"Primary research is cheaper than secondary research\",\"It is the reverse: primary research usually costs more and takes longer, and secondary research is usually cheap and quick (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary benefit of conducting surveys in HCI research?\",\"o\":[[\"They allow collecting data from a large number of users\",\"Correct. Surveys are a powerful way to gather quick responses in a short time from a <b>large number of users</b> (a broad view) (L4, Surveys).\"],[\"They always provide exact answers\",\"Answers to asked questions can suffer from recall bias and reporting bias, where people give answers more ideal than reality (L6, Challenges of data gathered by asking questions).\"],[\"They are faster than secondary research\",\"The course describes secondary research as the one that is \\\"usually cheap and quick\\\"; a survey is primary research, which usually costs more and takes longer (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"They eliminate the need for further testing\",\"Methods are not alternatives: use a combination in a relevant order, and survey findings help plan later activities such as interviews (L4, General tips; Surveys).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What does PII stand for?\",\"o\":[[\"Protected Individual Information\",\"Not the course term. The lecture writes PII as Personally Identifiable Information: specific details that could be used to identify a user (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Private Internet Information\",\"Not the course term. PII is Personally Identifiable Information, such as names, home address, email address and phone number (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Personal Identified Information\",\"Close but not the exact term: it is <b>Personally Identifiable</b> Information, meaning details that <b>could</b> be used to identify a user (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Personally Identifiable Information\",\"Correct. PII is <b>Personally Identifiable Information</b>: specific details that could be used to identify a user, such as names, home address, email address and phone number (L4, Privacy and security).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is considered PII?\",\"o\":[[\"All of the following\",\"Correct. Phone number, home address and email address are all in the lecture's PII list: specific details that could be used to identify a user (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Phone number\",\"Phone number is PII, but it is not the only one here: home address and email address are also on the PII list, so \\\"All of the following\\\" is the full answer (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Home address\",\"Home address is PII, but phone number and email address are PII too, so this option alone is incomplete (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Email address\",\"Email address is PII, but so are phone number and home address, so the complete answer is \\\"All of the following\\\" (L4, Privacy and security).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is a key advantage of secondary research in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"It is less expensive and less time-consuming\",\"Correct. The key benefit of secondary research is that it is <b>usually cheap and quick</b>; if the information exists, it saves enormous effort and time (L4, Primary vs secondary research; L3, Secondary research).\"],[\"It provides personalized insights for a new system\",\"Being specific to your needs is the benefit of <b>primary</b> research; secondary data can be not specific enough (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"It is always more accurate than primary research\",\"The course says secondary data can be too old and/or not specific enough, while in primary research you control the quality (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"It eliminates the need for user studies\",\"Secondary research backs up primary research findings; it does not replace them (L3, Secondary research). Methods should be combined, not used as alternatives (L4, General tips).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is SPII?\",\"o\":[[\"Secure Private Identifiable Information\",\"Not the course term. The S stands for <b>Sensitive</b>: data that could cause financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination if lost or stolen (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"System-Protected Information Infrastructure\",\"Not the course term. SPII is about users' personal data such as social security and passport numbers, not a system infrastructure (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Secure Personal Identity Index\",\"Not the course term. SPII is Sensitive Personally Identifiable Information, a type of user data, not an index (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Sensitive Personally Identifiable Information\",\"Correct. SPII is <b>Sensitive Personally Identifiable Information</b>: data that, if lost, compromised or stolen, could cause users financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination (L4, Privacy and security).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the main advantage of online surveys in HCI research?\",\"o\":[[\"They allow researchers to reach a larger number of users\",\"Correct. Surveys are often done online and gather quick responses in a short time from a <b>large number of users</b> (a broad view) (L4, Surveys).\"],[\"They do not require data analysis\",\"Survey data is collected to be used: the course says to ask only the minimum questions to get the data you need and know you will use (L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"They eliminate the need for qualitative research\",\"Methods are not alternatives; use a combination. Surveys often help plan later qualitative activities such as interviews (L4, General tips; Surveys).\"],[\"They are more accurate than usability testing\",\"The course says the opposite about asking questions: answers can suffer from recall bias and reporting bias, and the solution is to combine them with usability studies and observation (L6, Challenges of data gathered by asking questions).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What makes SPII different from regular PII?\",\"o\":[[\"SPII requires additional security measures due to its sensitivity\",\"Correct. SPII is data that, if lost, compromised or stolen, could cause users <b>financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination</b>, so it needs extra protection beyond ordinary PII (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"SPII is only used for marketing purposes\",\"The course defines SPII by the harm it can cause if lost or stolen, not by any marketing use (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"SPII does not contain personal details\",\"SPII is personal data: social security number, driver's license, passport numbers, financial account numbers and date of birth (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"SPII can be shared freely without risk\",\"The opposite: losing SPII can cause financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination, and the course says not to associate collected data with PII and SPII (L4, Privacy and security).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"In HCI, why might researchers choose primary research over secondary research?\",\"o\":[[\"To save time and costs\",\"Saving time and cost is the benefit of <b>secondary</b> research (usually cheap and quick); primary research usually costs more and takes longer (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"To gain direct insights from real users\",\"Correct. Primary research is conducted by you with your own users (interviews, surveys, observations), so it is <b>specific to your needs and you control the quality</b> (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"Because secondary research is illegal\",\"Nothing in the course says this; secondary research is recommended, especially early in a project (L3, Secondary research).\"],[\"Because secondary research is unreliable\",\"The course calls secondary research valuable: it is usually done by experienced researchers at credible organizations and backs up primary findings. Its weakness is that data may be too old or not specific enough (L3, Secondary research; L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is a common disadvantage of primary research?\",\"o\":[[\"It is always biased\",\"The course does not say primary research is always biased; one of its benefits is that you control the quality (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"It does not allow customization\",\"The opposite: a key benefit of primary research is that it is <b>specific to your needs</b> (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"It is often time-consuming and expensive\",\"Correct. The key disadvantage of primary research is that it <b>usually costs more and takes longer</b> (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"It provides outdated information\",\"Data that can be too old is the disadvantage of <b>secondary</b> research, not primary (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which method is often used in qualitative research?\",\"o\":[[\"Spreadsheet calculations\",\"Calculations work on numbers, which is <b>quantitative</b> research (data of numeric nature, measures and counts) (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Interviews and observations\",\"Correct. Qualitative research 'focuses on <b>observations</b> about why and how things happen' and is 'often based on <b>interviews</b>' (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Automated data logging\",\"Logged usage data, like the counts Microsoft collected through CEIP, is numeric, so it is quantitative, not qualitative (L4, Types of user research by What; L2, CEIP).\"],[\"Surveys with numeric ratings\",\"Numeric ratings are data of numeric nature, which is quantitative research (L4, Types of user research by What).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What type of data does quantitative research involve?\",\"o\":[[\"Observations and opinions\",\"Observations about why and how things happen describe <b>qualitative</b> research (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Numeric data, measures, and counts\",\"Correct. Quantitative research 'involves <b>data of numeric nature</b>. Examples: measures and counts' (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Only text from interviews\",\"Interview text is qualitative data; qualitative research is 'often based on interviews' (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"User stories only\",\"User stories are descriptive, not numeric, and the lecture defines quantitative data as measures and counts (L4, Types of user research by What).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What does qualitative research focus on?\",\"o\":[[\"Counts and measures\",\"Measures and counts are the lecture's examples of <b>quantitative</b> research (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Automated analytics\",\"Automated analytics produce numeric data, which is quantitative research, not qualitative (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Only numeric trends\",\"Numeric data describes quantitative research; qualitative research is about why and how things happen (L4, Types of user research by What).\"],[\"Observations about why and how things happen\",\"Correct. Qualitative research 'focuses on <b>observations about why and how things happen</b>', often based on interviews (L4, Types of user research by What).\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the role of testing and iteration in user research?\",\"o\":[[\"To improve techniques and refine understanding\",\"Correct. Among the general tips: 'Testing and iteration is your way to <b>improve any technique</b>' (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"To reduce user involvement\",\"User research is about involving and working with users rather than guessing; testing and iteration do not reduce that (L3, User-centered design; L4, General tips).\"],[\"To avoid using multiple methods\",\"The tips say the opposite: methods are not alternatives, so 'use a combination of methods in a relevant order' (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"To strictly follow the initial plan without changes\",\"Planning ahead 'does not mean to be strict'; flexibility is required and 'the script is not bible' (L4, General tips for research methods).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"How should different research methods be used in a project?\",\"o\":[[\"Only one method should ever be used\",\"The lecture says the opposite: methods are not alternatives, so you should use a combination of them (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"In combination and in a relevant order\",\"Correct. The lecture says different methods are not alternatives in a one-step process: <b>use a combination of methods in a relevant order</b> (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"Randomly, without order\",\"The tip says the combination must be in a <b>relevant order</b>, and that research should be planned ahead, not random (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"As alternatives in a single step process\",\"This is exactly what the slide rejects: 'Different methods are not alternatives in a one step process' (L4, General tips for research methods).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Why should a survey be short and focused?\",\"o\":[[\"To increase response rate and accuracy\",\"Correct. The lecture says keep the survey as small as possible because <b>asking too many questions affects the response rate and the reliability of data</b>; ask the minimum number of questions you need (L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"To make it harder to complete\",\"A short survey is easier to complete, not harder. The point of keeping it small is to protect the response rate (L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"To collect unnecessary information\",\"The opposite: the tip is to ask the minimum number of questions to get the data you need and know you will use (L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"To confuse the respondents\",\"Keeping it short aims at clear, reliable answers. Confusing respondents goes against the L5 principles Be Clear and Be Concise (L5, Be Clear / Be Concise).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Does planning ahead in research mean you cannot be flexible?\",\"o\":[[\"Yes, the script must be followed strictly\",\"The lecture says the opposite: planning ahead does not mean being strict, because the <b>script is not a bible</b> (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"Planning ahead replaces iteration\",\"The lecture lists both: plan ahead, and also 'testing and iteration is your way to improve any technique'. Planning does not replace iteration (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"No, flexibility is required during the activity\",\"Correct. 'Planning ahead does not mean to be strict, <b>flexibility is required during the activity</b> (script is not bible)' (L4, General tips for research methods).\"],[\"Yes, there is no room for adaptation\",\"Wrong: the slide says flexibility is required during the activity, so you adapt as needed (L4, General tips for research methods).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a major benefit of secondary research?\",\"o\":[[\"It is quick and inexpensive\",\"Correct. The primary vs secondary table lists the key benefit of secondary research as <b>usually cheap and quick</b> (L4, The difference between Primary and Secondary Research).\"],[\"It provides completely new data\",\"Secondary research uses existing data collected by someone else ('looking for existing data'), so it does not provide new data (L4, Primary vs secondary table).\"],[\"It is expensive\",\"Cost is a disadvantage of primary research ('usually costs more and takes longer'). Secondary research is usually cheap (L4, Primary vs secondary table).\"],[\"It is time-consuming\",\"Taking longer is the key disadvantage of primary research. Secondary research is quick and saves enormous effort and time (L4 table; L3, Secondary Research).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Primary research involves collecting new data directly from sources, while secondary research uses existing data.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. In the comparison table, primary data is <b>collected by you</b> (surveys, focus groups, interviews, observations, experiments), while secondary research means <b>looking for existing data</b> collected by someone else (L4, Primary vs secondary table).\"],[\"False\",\"The statement matches the lecture: primary research is research you conduct yourself, and secondary research is research done by someone else, using existing data (L3, Types of user research based on Who; L4 table).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Primary research methods are typically more expensive than secondary research methods.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. In the primary vs secondary table, the key disadvantage of primary research is that it <b>usually costs more and takes longer</b>, while secondary research is usually cheap and quick (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture's table says the opposite: primary research usually costs more and takes longer, and secondary research is <b>usually cheap and quick</b> (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Secondary research is generally less time-consuming and less expensive than primary research.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The primary vs secondary table gives the key benefit of secondary research as <b>usually cheap and quick</b>, and the key disadvantage of primary research as usually costing more and taking longer (L4, Primary vs secondary research).\"],[\"False\",\"The table says secondary research is usually cheap and quick, while primary research <b>usually costs more and takes longer</b> (L4, Primary vs secondary research). L3 also says existing secondary information saves enormous effort and time.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Which of these is <b>PII but NOT SPII</b>?\",\"o\":[[\"Social security number\",\"Social security number is Sensitive PII, since losing it could cause financial harm. The PII-only items are names, home address, email and phone (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Email address\",\"Correct. Email address is listed under PII (with names, home address, phone number), not under SPII.\"],[\"Passport number\",\"Passport numbers are in the SPII list, data whose loss could cause harm or discrimination. Email address is the plain PII choice (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Date of birth\",\"Date of birth is listed as Sensitive PII.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A team designing a charting app reads published facts about color blindness before choosing colors. According to the lecture, this secondary research answers questions about:\",\"o\":[[\"Whether the product succeeded after launch\",\"That is post-launch research, not what color-blindness facts tell you.\"],[\"Numbers from the team's own survey\",\"The team's own survey would be primary research.\"],[\"Human abilities and limitations\",\"Correct. The lecture gives designing for color-blind users as an example of secondary research on human abilities and limitations.\"],[\"The PII of the team's users\",\"Reading published color-blindness facts collects no user data at all.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"According to the primary vs secondary table, what is a key disadvantage of <b>secondary</b> research?\",\"o\":[[\"It usually costs more and takes longer\",\"That is the disadvantage of primary research; secondary is usually cheap and quick.\"],[\"You control the quality\",\"That is a benefit of primary research.\"],[\"It can only be qualitative\",\"The table says secondary research can be either qualitative or quantitative.\"],[\"The data can be too old and/or not specific enough\",\"Correct. This is the key disadvantage of secondary research in the table.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"Primary research can only produce quantitative data.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The primary vs secondary table says both can be qualitative or quantitative. Interviews, for example, are primary and qualitative (L4, Primary vs secondary).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Both primary and secondary research can be either qualitative or quantitative.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A team needs quick responses from a large number of users in a short time, with a very small budget. Which method fits best?\",\"o\":[[\"A survey\",\"Correct. Surveys give quick responses from many users in a short time and are one of the cheapest methods.\"],[\"One-on-one interviews with each user\",\"Interviews take far more time per user and cost more.\"],[\"Sitting with each user for a full day of observation\",\"This is slow and cannot reach a large number of users quickly.\"],[\"Running several group interviews\",\"Group interviews still take much more time per participant than a survey.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"According to the table, what is a key benefit of <b>primary</b> research?\",\"o\":[[\"It is usually cheap and quick\",\"That is the benefit of secondary research.\"],[\"It is specific to your needs, and you control the quality\",\"Correct. This is the key benefit of primary research in the table.\"],[\"The data already exists, so nothing has to be collected\",\"That describes secondary research (looking for existing data).\"],[\"It never needs planning\",\"The lecture says user research should be planned ahead.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Research methods are alternatives: you should pick one method and use it alone.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says methods are not alternatives.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Methods are not alternatives; use a <b>combination</b> of them in a relevant order.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which of these is <b>NOT</b> one of the lecture's privacy guidelines for user research?\",\"o\":[[\"Only collect the data you need\",\"\\\"Only collect the data you need\\\" is the first privacy guideline, so it is not the answer (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Do not associate collected data with PII or SPII\",\"\\\"Do not associate collected data with PII or SPII\\\" is one of the three privacy guidelines, so it is not the answer (L4, Privacy and security).\"],[\"Keep all collected data permanently in case it is useful later\",\"Correct. This breaks the rule \\\"do not keep data after you no longer need it\\\".\"],[\"Do not keep data after you no longer need it\",\"\\\"Do not keep data after you no longer need it\\\" is a real guideline. The option that breaks it, keeping data permanently, is the answer (L4, Privacy and security).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"A survey can be used after an interview or a usability study, not only as an initial step.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Surveys can be an initial step to plan interviews, and can also follow an interview or usability study.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture explicitly says a survey can also follow an interview or usability study.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A short survey about a notes app's search feature also asks for the respondent's full name, home address and phone number, which the team will never use. What does it break?\",\"o\":[[\"Use a combination of methods\",\"The problem is not the choice of methods but the data being collected.\"],[\"Testing and iteration improve any technique\",\"That is a general tip, but it is not what this survey breaks.\"],[\"Get feedback from participants about the survey\",\"That tip is about improving the survey, not about what data to collect.\"],[\"Ask only what you need, and only collect the data you need (avoid unneeded PII)\",\"Correct. The survey asks for PII it will never use, breaking both the survey tip and the privacy rule.\"]],\"a\":3}]},{\"n\":5,\"title\":\"How to Ask Good Survey Questions\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Six principles: Clear · Concise · Specific · Expressive · Unbiased · Usable\",\"pts\":[\"Each principle has \\\"Don't ask this… / Instead, ask this…\\\" examples. Examiners often ask you to evaluate a question with them.\"]},{\"h\":\"Be Clear\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Label numeric scales</b>: not \\\"1–5\\\" alone but \\\"1 = Highly dissatisfied … 5 = Highly satisfied\\\".\",\"<b>Avoid overlapping ranges</b>: 0-2, 2-5, 5-10 overlap. Use 0-2, 3-5, 6-10, 11-19, 20+.\",\"<b>When in doubt, give extra detail</b>: \\\"tablet computer (i.e. any computer with a touchscreen and detachable keyboard)\\\".\",\"<b>Timebox frequency questions</b>: instead of \\\"Never / Rarely / Often…\\\", ask \\\"In the past seven days, how many times have you exercised? 0, 1-2, 3-4, 5-7, 7 or more\\\".\"]},{\"h\":\"Be Concise\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Plain language</b>: \\\"What was the overall level of cleanliness that you observed within the car?\\\" becomes \\\"How clean was the car?\\\"\"]},{\"h\":\"Be Specific\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Avoid super-big ideas</b>: \\\"How satisfied were you with the interface?\\\" becomes \\\"…with how quickly the interface responded?\\\" plus follow-up questions.\",\"<b>Avoid double-barrel questions</b> (two things in one): \\\"speed and availability of your connection\\\" should be split into two questions.\",\"<b>Avoid internal conflict</b>: \\\"How satisfied with your food?\\\" should be split into temperature, appearance and flavor.\"]},{\"h\":\"Be Expressive\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Emphasize the user's opinion</b>: \\\"Is our price too high?\\\" becomes \\\"Do you feel our price is too high, too low, or about right?\\\"\",\"<b>Use ranges instead of yes/no</b>: \\\"Do you use social media?\\\" becomes \\\"In the past seven days, how much time have you spent on social media? 0 / &lt;1 / 1-2 / 3-5 / 6-9 / 10+ hours\\\".\",\"<b>Give levels of frequency or agreement</b>: a 5-level satisfaction scale instead of Dissatisfied/Satisfied.\",\"<b>Allow multiple selections</b> when possible (checkboxes instead of radio buttons).\"]},{\"h\":\"Be Unbiased\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Allow users to add nominal categories</b>: add \\\"Other: ____\\\".\",\"<b>Leave open-ended questions open</b>: \\\"Why did you choose our service?\\\" should not force fixed options.\",\"<b>Avoid leading questions</b>: \\\"Did our brand-new AI-based interface generate better recommendations?\\\" becomes \\\"How satisfied were you with the recommendations?\\\"\",\"<b>Avoid loaded questions</b>: \\\"how much time have you <i>wasted</i> on social media?\\\" becomes \\\"…<i>spent</i>…\\\".\"]},{\"h\":\"Be Usable\",\"pts\":[\"Provide a <b>progress bar</b>. Keep <b>page lengths consistent</b>. <b>Order questions logically</b>. <b>Alert users about unanswered questions</b>. <b>Preview the survey yourself</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Exercise: evaluate these survey questions\",\"pts\":[\"\\\"On a scale of 1 to 4 with 1 meaning 'a lot' and 4 meaning 'not at all', how much do you enjoy exercising?\\\" is an <b>ambiguous / reversed scale</b>.\",\"\\\"Why do you like to exercise?\\\" is a <b>leading question</b> because it assumes you like it.\",\"\\\"On a scale of 1 to 6 with 1 meaning 'not at all'…\\\" has a <b>changing number of options</b> (4 vs 6) and a <b>reversed scale</b> compared with the previous question.\",\"\\\"Have you listened to an audiobook this year?\\\" is a <b>yes/no question</b>. Use ranges.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"6 principles of good survey questions\",\"Be Clear, Concise, Specific, Expressive, Unbiased, Usable.\"],[\"Double-barrel question\",\"Asks about two things at once, e.g. \\\"speed and availability\\\". Split it (Be Specific).\"],[\"Leading question\",\"Pushes toward an answer (\\\"our brand-new AI interface…\\\"). Fix: neutral wording (Be Unbiased).\"],[\"Loaded question\",\"Contains a judgment word (\\\"wasted\\\"). Fix: neutral word (\\\"spent\\\").\"],[\"Overlapping ranges fix\",\"0-2, 3-5, 6-10, 11-19, 20+ (Be Clear).\"],[\"Instead of yes/no…\",\"Ask ranges (Be Expressive): \\\"In the past 7 days, how much time…\\\".\"],[\"Nominal categories\",\"Add an \\\"Other: ___\\\" option (Be Unbiased).\"],[\"Be Usable checklist\",\"Progress bar, consistent page lengths, logical order, alert unanswered, preview yourself.\"],[\"Timebox frequency\",\"\\\"In the past seven days, how many times…\\\" instead of \\\"Rarely/Often\\\" (Be Clear).\"]],\"qa\":[[\"Mention the points to consider when asking good survey questions, with examples.\",\"Be Clear (label scales, no overlapping ranges, extra detail, timeboxed frequency); Be Concise (plain language: \\\"How clean was the car?\\\"); Be Specific (no super-big ideas, no double-barrel, no internal conflict); Be Expressive (emphasize opinions, ranges instead of yes/no, levels of agreement, multiple selections); Be Unbiased (add \\\"Other\\\", keep open-ended questions open, avoid leading and loaded words); Be Usable (progress bar, consistent pages, logical order, alert unanswered, preview).\"],[\"Evaluate: \\\"In the past seven days, how much time have you wasted on social media?\\\"\",\"A loaded question: \\\"wasted\\\" judges the respondent. Ask \\\"…how much time have you spent on social media?\\\"\"],[\"Evaluate: \\\"Do you use social media?\\\"\",\"A yes/no question that gives little information. Use ranges: \\\"In the past seven days, how much time have you spent on social media? 0 / <1 / 1-2 / 3-5 / 6-9 / 10+ hours\\\".\"],[\"Evaluate: \\\"How satisfied are you with the speed and availability of your mobile connection?\\\"\",\"A double-barrel question: it asks two things. Split it into one question about speed and one about availability.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"\\\"How satisfied are you with the speed and availability of your mobile connection?\\\" violates which rule?\",\"o\":[[\"Avoid overlapping ranges\",\"Overlapping ranges (like 0-2, 2-5) is a Be Clear rule about numeric answer options. This question has no ranges; it combines speed and availability in one question (L5, Be Specific).\"],[\"Avoid double-barrel questions\",\"Correct. It asks about two things at once, so split it.\"],[\"Allow multiple selections\",\"Allowing multiple selections (checkboxes) is a Be Expressive tip about answer controls. The flaw here is asking about two things, speed and availability, at once (L5, Be Specific).\"],[\"Provide a progress bar\",\"That is about survey usability, not this question.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"How much time have you WASTED on social media?\\\" is an example of:\",\"o\":[[\"A loaded question\",\"Correct. \\\"Wasted\\\" carries a judgment. Use \\\"spent\\\".\"],[\"A double-barrel question\",\"A double-barrel question asks about two things at once. This asks about one thing, time on social media; the problem is the judgment word \\\"wasted\\\" (L5, Be Unbiased).\"],[\"An open-ended question\",\"Being open-ended is not the issue; the lecture even says to leave open-ended questions open. The flaw is the loaded word \\\"wasted\\\" (L5, Be Unbiased).\"],[\"A timeboxed question\",\"Timeboxing is good practice; the issue is the word choice.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which option set avoids overlapping ranges?\",\"o\":[[\"0-2, 2-5, 5-10, 10-20, 20+\",\"These ranges share their boundaries: 2, 5, 10 and 20 each fall in two ranges, so a respondent with exactly 5 has two valid answers (L5, Be Clear).\"],[\"0-2, 3-5, 6-10, 11-19, 20+\",\"Correct. Every value belongs to exactly one range.\"],[\"0-5, 5-10, 10+\",\"5 and 10 each appear in two ranges (0-5 and 5-10, 5-10 and 10+), so these ranges overlap, which Be Clear says to avoid (L5, Be Clear).\"],[\"Some, Many, A lot\",\"Some / Many / A lot are vague words with no numbers, so they are not ranges at all and different people read them differently (L5, Be Clear).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Adding \\\"Other: ____\\\" to a list of social media platforms follows which principle?\",\"o\":[[\"Be Concise\",\"It is about fairness to all answers, not brevity.\"],[\"Be Unbiased\",\"Correct. It allows users to add nominal categories.\"],[\"Be Usable\",\"Usable covers progress bars, ordering and similar.\"],[\"Be Clear\",\"Be Clear covers labeling scales, non-overlapping ranges, extra detail and timeboxing. Adding \\\"Other: ____\\\" lets users add nominal categories, a Be Unbiased rule (L5, Be Unbiased).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Why do you like to exercise?\\\" is a problem because it is:\",\"o\":[[\"A yes/no question\",\"It is not yes/no: \\\"why\\\" invites an open answer. The flaw is that it assumes you like exercising, which makes it leading (L5, Exercise).\"],[\"A leading question\",\"Correct. It assumes the person likes exercising.\"],[\"An overlapping range\",\"Overlapping ranges only apply to numeric answer options, and this question has none. Its flaw is assuming the person likes exercise (L5, Exercise).\"],[\"Too concise\",\"Being short is good (Be Concise). The problem is that it assumes the respondent likes exercising, so it leads them (L5, Exercise).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25 Q2B\"},{\"q\":\"Which is part of \\\"Be Usable\\\"?\",\"o\":[[\"Provide a progress bar\",\"Correct. Also consistent page lengths, logical order, alerts for unanswered questions, and previewing.\"],[\"Avoid double-barrel questions\",\"Avoiding double-barrel questions belongs to Be Specific (split questions that ask two things), not to Be Usable (L5, Be Specific).\"],[\"Use plain language\",\"Plain language is the Be Concise rule (\\\"How clean was the car?\\\"). Be Usable is about the survey itself: progress bar, page length, ordering, alerts, preview (L5, Be Concise).\"],[\"Avoid loaded questions\",\"Avoiding loaded words like \\\"wasted\\\" is a Be Unbiased rule, not a Be Usable one (L5, Be Unbiased).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"How often do you exercise? Never / Rarely / Occasionally / Frequently / Always\\\" should be replaced with:\",\"o\":[[\"\\\"Do you exercise? Yes / No\\\"\",\"A yes/no question gives even less information; Be Expressive says to use ranges instead of yes/no (L5, Be Expressive).\"],[\"\\\"In the past seven days, how many times have you exercised? 0 / 1-2 / 3-4 / 5-7 / 7 or more\\\"\",\"Correct. Be Clear says to timebox frequency questions: words like Rarely or Often mean different things to different people, while \\\"in the past seven days\\\" with counts is exact (L5, Be Clear).\"],[\"\\\"Why do you exercise?\\\"\",\"This asks something else (reasons, not frequency), and \\\"Why do you…\\\" assumes the person exercises, making it leading (L5, Be Unbiased).\"],[\"\\\"Rate exercise 1-5\\\"\",\"An unlabeled 1-5 scale breaks Be Clear's \\\"label numeric scales\\\" rule, and it still does not measure how often someone exercises (L5, Be Clear).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which best summarizes a good survey design principle?\",\"o\":[[\"Long surveys with complex questions\",\"The opposite of the tips: surveys should be as small as possible and questions concise and clear, since long surveys hurt response rate and reliability (L4, Surveys; L5).\"],[\"Short, clear, unbiased surveys with participant feedback considered\",\"Correct. Keep the survey as small as possible, word questions clearly, avoid bias, and get feedback from participants about the survey (L4, Surveys; L5, six principles).\"],[\"Surveys without planning\",\"User research must be planned ahead; a survey without planning breaks the general tips and the six question principles (L4, General tips).\"],[\"Focus only on quantitative questions\",\"Methods should be combined, and the lecture says to leave open-ended questions open, so quantitative-only questions are not the principle (L5, Be Unbiased).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"Be biased\\\" is an important point for a good survey.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The principle is \\\"Be UNbiased\\\". (The 2024/25 answer sheet marks this True, which looks like a typo in the question. Answer from the lecture.)\"],[\"False\",\"Correct per the lecture: the principle is Be Unbiased.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"Which factor is most important when designing an HCI survey?\",\"o\":[[\"Making the survey as long as possible\",\"The course says the opposite: keep a survey as small as possible, because too many questions affect the response rate and the reliability of the data (L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"Ensuring clarity and simplicity in questions\",\"Correct. Good survey questions must <b>Be Clear</b> and <b>Be Concise</b>, using plain language such as \\\"How clean was the car?\\\" (L5, How to ask good survey questions).\"],[\"Asking irrelevant questions to increase engagement\",\"The course says to ask the minimum number of questions to get the data you need and know you will use, so irrelevant questions should be removed (L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"Using technical language to test user knowledge\",\"This breaks <b>Be Concise</b>: questions should use plain language, not wordy or technical phrasing (L5, Be Concise). A survey gathers users' views, it does not test them.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Why is it important to test a survey before distributing it in HCI research?\",\"o\":[[\"To increase response time\",\"The course wants surveys to be quick to answer: keep them as small as possible, and a well-designed survey should not take a long time (L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"To identify unclear or unspecific questions\",\"Correct. Under <b>Be Usable</b> the course says \\\"Preview the survey yourself\\\", and it asks for feedback from participants about the survey, so problems with the Be Clear and Be Specific rules are caught before release (L5, Be Usable; L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"To reduce the number of responses needed\",\"Surveys are meant to gather quick responses from a large number of users; testing the survey does not change how many responses you need (L4, Surveys).\"],[\"To remove all multiple-choice questions\",\"The course recommends choice questions such as ranges, levels of agreement and allowing multiple selections (L5, Be Expressive), so testing is not about removing them.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the best way to ensure a survey collects accurate user feedback in HCI?\",\"o\":[[\"Use clear and unbiased wording in questions\",\"Correct. Two of the six principles of good survey questions are <b>Be Clear</b> and <b>Be Unbiased</b> (avoid leading and loaded questions), so answers reflect the user's real view (L5, How to ask good survey questions).\"],[\"Ask technical questions that users may not understand\",\"This breaks <b>Be Clear</b> and <b>Be Concise</b>: questions should use plain language, and when in doubt you add extra detail so users understand (L5, Be Clear; Be Concise).\"],[\"Make all questions mandatory\",\"The course does not say this. Under <b>Be Usable</b> it says to alert users about unanswered questions, and its survey tips are about keeping the survey small and unbiased (L5, Be Usable; L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"],[\"Only ask open-ended questions\",\"The course recommends ranges, levels of frequency or agreement, and multiple selections (L5, Be Expressive). It only says to leave questions that are open-ended by nature open.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following is important when designing a good survey?\",\"o\":[[\"Keeping questions simple and clear\",\"Correct. Two of the six principles of good survey questions are <b>Be Clear</b> and <b>Be Concise</b> (plain language, e.g. 'How clean was the car?') (L5, How to ask good survey questions).\"],[\"Using unclear questions\",\"This breaks 'Be Clear', which asks you to label scales, avoid overlapping ranges and give extra detail when in doubt (L5, Be Clear).\"],[\"Asking multiple things in one question\",\"This is a <b>double-barrel</b> question, which 'Be Specific' tells you to avoid by splitting it into separate questions (L5, Be Specific).\"],[\"Using long and complex sentences\",\"This breaks 'Be Concise': 'What was the overall level of cleanliness that you observed within the car?' should become 'How clean was the car?' (L5, Be Concise).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the benefit of using a logical question order in a survey?\",\"o\":[[\"It discourages participation\",\"'Order your questions logically' is listed under <b>Be Usable</b>, a principle meant to make the survey easier to complete, not to discourage it (L5, Be Usable).\"],[\"It makes the survey confusing\",\"Logical order is a 'Be Usable' guideline for good survey questions; it removes confusion rather than adding it (L5, Be Usable).\"],[\"It helps respondents answer easily and consistently\",\"Correct. 'Order your questions logically' is one of the <b>Be Usable</b> tips (with a progress bar, consistent page lengths, alerts for unanswered questions and previewing), which make the survey easy for respondents to complete (L5, Be Usable).\"],[\"It reduces response accuracy\",\"The lecture's survey principles aim at clear, reliable answers; a logical order is a 'Be Usable' guideline, not something that harms accuracy (L5, Be Usable).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What should be avoided when writing survey questions?\",\"o\":[[\"Leading or biased questions\",\"Correct. Under <b>Be Unbiased</b> the lecture says to avoid leading questions (e.g. 'Did our brand-new AI-based interface generate better recommendations?') and loaded questions (e.g. 'wasted') (L5, Be Unbiased).\"],[\"Consistent format\",\"Consistency is recommended, not avoided: Be Usable says to keep page lengths consistent, and L5's exercise criticises scales whose number of options and direction change between questions (L5, Be Usable / Exercise).\"],[\"Logical order\",\"Ordering questions logically is one of the Be Usable tips, so it should be done, not avoided (L5, Be Usable).\"],[\"Neutral wording\",\"Neutral wording is the fix the lecture gives for loaded and leading questions, e.g. 'spent' instead of 'wasted' (L5, Be Unbiased).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Yes/No questions in surveys provide detailed insights into user opinions.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture treats yes/no questions as a problem: under <b>Be Expressive</b> it says to use ranges instead of yes/no, because a yes/no answer gives little information (L5, Be Expressive).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Yes/no questions give little information. The <b>Be Expressive</b> principle says to use ranges instead, e.g. replace \\\"Do you use social media?\\\" with \\\"In the past seven days, how much time have you spent on social media?\\\" (L5, Be Expressive).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Yes/No questions are useful for collecting qualitative data in surveys.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Qualitative research focuses on observations about <b>why and how</b> things happen (L4, Types of user research based on What). A yes/no answer explains neither, and the lecture says to replace yes/no questions with ranges (L5, Be Expressive).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Qualitative data is about <b>why and how</b> things happen, often from interviews (L4). Yes/no questions give little information, so the lecture says to use ranges instead and to leave open-ended questions like \\\"Why did you choose our service?\\\" open (L5, Be Expressive; Be Unbiased).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Surveys should always include a mix of open-ended and Yes/No questions for better insights.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The course does not ask for yes/no questions at all. Under <b>Be Expressive</b> it says to use ranges instead of yes/no, and gives \\\"Have you listened to an audiobook this year?\\\" as a weak yes/no question to replace with ranges (L5, Be Expressive; Exercise).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The lecture never says surveys should always mix in yes/no questions; it tells you to <b>replace yes/no questions with ranges</b> (Be Expressive) and to keep the survey as small as possible, asking only what you need (L5, Be Expressive; L4, Tips for a good survey design).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A survey asks \\\"Rate the app from 1 to 5\\\" with no meaning given for the numbers. Which guideline does it break?\",\"o\":[[\"Be Clear: label numeric scales\",\"Correct. Scales should be labeled, e.g. \\\"1 = Highly dissatisfied … 5 = Highly satisfied\\\".\"],[\"Be Concise: use plain language\",\"The question is already short; the issue is the unlabeled scale.\"],[\"Be Unbiased: avoid loaded questions\",\"There is no emotionally loaded word in the question.\"],[\"Be Usable: provide a progress bar\",\"A progress bar is about the whole survey, not about one unlabeled scale.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"What was the overall level of cleanliness that you observed within the car?\\\" should become \\\"How clean was the car?\\\". Which principle is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Be Specific: avoid double-barrel questions\",\"The original asks about only one thing.\"],[\"Be Concise: use plain language\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's plain-language example under Be Concise.\"],[\"Be Expressive: allow multiple selections\",\"The change is about wording, not the answer format.\"],[\"Be Unbiased: avoid leading questions\",\"The original does not push the user toward an answer.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"How satisfied are you with your food?\\\" is a problem because:\",\"o\":[[\"It is a loaded question\",\"It uses no emotionally loaded word like \\\"wasted\\\".\"],[\"It has overlapping ranges\",\"Overlapping ranges need numeric options like 0-2, 2-5; this question has none. Its flaw is mixing temperature, appearance and flavor (L5, Be Specific).\"],[\"It has internal conflict; split it into temperature, appearance and flavor\",\"Correct. This is the \\\"avoid internal conflict\\\" example under Be Specific: one may like the flavor but not the temperature.\"],[\"It is a yes/no question\",\"It asks for a level of satisfaction, not yes/no.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"Allowing multiple selections when possible means using radio buttons instead of checkboxes.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Radio buttons allow only one selection.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Allowing multiple selections means using <b>checkboxes instead of radio buttons</b>.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> \\\"Did our brand-new AI-based interface generate better recommendations?\\\" Which problem does this question have?\",\"o\":[[\"It is a double-barrel question\",\"It asks about one thing (the recommendations).\"],[\"It is not timeboxed\",\"Timeboxing applies to frequency questions; this is not one.\"],[\"It does not allow nominal categories\",\"\\\"Other: ____\\\" is for lists of categories, not this question.\"],[\"It is a leading question; ask \\\"How satisfied were you with the recommendations?\\\"\",\"Correct. Praising the \\\"brand-new AI-based\\\" interface and suggesting \\\"better\\\" leads the user. This is the lecture's leading-question example.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"Is our price too high?\\\" should become \\\"Do you feel our price is too high, too low, or about right?\\\". Which guideline is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Be Expressive: emphasize the user's opinion\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's example of emphasizing the user's opinion.\"],[\"Be Usable: order questions logically\",\"Ordering is about the sequence of questions, not this wording.\"],[\"Be Clear: avoid overlapping ranges\",\"Overlapping ranges concern numeric answer options, and this question has none. The rewrite adds \\\"too low / about right\\\" to capture the user's opinion (L5, Be Expressive).\"],[\"Be Concise: use plain language\",\"The new version is actually longer; the goal is to capture the user's opinion.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"What is the problem with \\\"Have you listened to an audiobook this year?\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"It is a leading question\",\"It does not assume an answer or praise anything.\"],[\"It is a yes/no question; use ranges instead\",\"Correct. The lecture's exercise flags it as yes/no; ranges (as in \\\"Be Expressive\\\") give richer answers.\"],[\"It is a loaded question\",\"A loaded question contains a judgment word like \\\"wasted\\\"; this question is neutral. Its flaw is the yes/no format (L5, Exercise).\"],[\"It has overlapping ranges\",\"It has no ranges at all, which is the actual problem.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Why did you choose our service?\\\" should be left as an open-ended question instead of forcing fixed options.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Under Be Unbiased: leave open-ended questions open.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says this question should not force fixed options.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A survey asks enjoyment \\\"on a scale of 1 to 4, 1 = a lot, 4 = not at all\\\", then the next question uses \\\"1 to 6, 1 = not at all\\\". What is wrong?\",\"o\":[[\"It is a double-barrel question\",\"A double-barrel question asks two things at once, but each of these asks only about enjoyment. The flaw is the inconsistent scales (L5, Exercise).\"],[\"The questions are too concise\",\"Being concise is good (Be Concise). The real problem is that the scale changes from 4 to 6 options and its direction is reversed (L5, Exercise).\"],[\"The number of options changes (4 vs 6) and the scale direction is reversed\",\"Correct. The lecture's exercise flags the changing number of options and the reversed scale.\"],[\"It is missing an \\\"Other: ____\\\" option\",\"\\\"Other\\\" is for lists of categories, not numeric scales.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"Writing 'tablet computer (i.e. any computer with a touchscreen and detachable keyboard)' follows the Be Clear tip: when in doubt, give extra detail.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's example of giving extra detail under Be Clear.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture lists exactly this example under Be Clear.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":6,\"title\":\"Interviews and Starting Design\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"2) Interviews\",\"pts\":[\"The <b>most common</b> research technique.\",\"Deeper, more thorough understanding: more open-ended and \\\"why\\\" questions (Joyner: focus on the <b>6 Ws</b>: who, what, where, when, why, how), room for follow-up questions, and reflection on rare events.\",\"Far fewer users can be interviewed than surveyed.\",\"<b>More expensive</b>: <b>synchronous</b> (the interviewer devotes time to each interviewee) and <b>incentives</b> (interviewees often expect compensation).\",\"Interviews and surveys can be done in sequence in either order, using the results of one to plan the other.\"]},{\"h\":\"Tips for a good interview\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Plan ahead</b> and prepare questions before the interview.\",\"During the interview, two keys: <b>listen well</b> (the user should talk most of the time) and <b>take notes</b> (one person asks and one takes notes; audio recording is not enough; note emotions and reactions).\",\"<b>Leave some time between interviews.</b>\"]},{\"h\":\"Interviewing individuals vs groups\",\"table\":[[\"Individuals\",\"Groups\"],[\"More time/effort spent with each participant\",\"Easier to get more feedback\"],[\"Easier to coordinate the logistics\",\"Participants' discussion can highlight assumptions\"],[\"More privacy for participants\",\"May need to deal with confounding personalities\"],[\"\",\"\\\"Presentation of self\\\" issues\"]]},{\"h\":\"Challenges of data gathered by asking questions\",\"pts\":[\"Planning takes more time and effort than the team may have.\",\"Analysis of qualitative data is not easy.\",\"<b>Recall bias</b>: inaccurate answers about things in the past.\",\"<b>Reporting bias</b>: people give answers more ideal than reality.\",\"Solution: combine with other techniques such as <b>participant observation, usability studies and secondary research</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Design: can I?\",\"pts\":[\"Scott Klemmer: \\\"Designing great user interfaces requires enormous creativity and a lot of hard work…\\\", but pretty good interfaces are easy if you know basic methods, techniques and principles.\",\"Joyner: \\\"design is very hard\\\".\",\"Designers say: \\\"we are not artists; in most cases we are not even talented in drawing.\\\" Design does not produce wall-hanging artwork.\",\"Talent and creativity are an advantage. Do not turn off creativity; follow tips and avoid the biggest design mistakes.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Most common research technique\",\"Interviews.\"],[\"Joyner's 6 Ws\",\"Who, What, Where, When, Why, How.\"],[\"Why interviews are expensive\",\"They are synchronous (time per interviewee) and need incentives.\"],[\"Two keys during an interview\",\"Listen well (the user talks most) and take notes (audio is not enough).\"],[\"Recall bias\",\"Inaccurate answers about things that happened in the past.\"],[\"Reporting bias\",\"People give answers more ideal than reality.\"],[\"Individual interviews: advantages\",\"More time per person, easier logistics, more privacy.\"],[\"Group interviews: advantages\",\"More feedback; discussion highlights assumptions.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What are the tips for a good interview?\",\"Plan ahead and prepare questions before the interview. During it, listen well so the user talks most of the time, and take notes (one asks, one writes; audio is not enough; note emotions and reactions). Leave some time between interviews.\"],[\"Compare interviewing individuals versus groups.\",\"Individuals: more time per participant, easier logistics, more privacy. Groups: easier to get more feedback and discussion highlights assumptions, but you may face confounding personalities and \\\"presentation of self\\\" issues.\"],[\"What are the challenges of data gathered by asking questions and how do you solve them?\",\"Planning takes time; qualitative analysis is hard; recall bias; reporting bias (idealised answers). Solve by combining with participant observation, usability studies and secondary research.\"],[\"Compare interviews and surveys.\",\"Surveys reach many users quickly and cheaply (broad view). Interviews reach fewer users but give deeper understanding (why questions, follow-ups, rare events) and cost more (synchronous, incentives). They can be combined in either order.\"],[\"What are interviews? Describe them as a user research method.\",\"Interviews are the <b>most common</b> user research technique.<br>1) They allow a deeper and more thorough understanding of users: more open-ended and \\\"why\\\" questions (Joyner: focus on the <b>6 Ws</b>: who, what, where, when, why, how), room for follow-up questions, and reflection on rare activities and events.<br>2) Far fewer users can be interviewed compared to surveys.<br>3) They are more expensive: <b>synchronous</b> (the interviewer devotes time to every interviewee) and <b>incentives</b> (interviewed users often expect some compensation).<br>4) Interviews and surveys can be done in sequence in either order, using the results of one to plan the other.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"\\\"Interview is the most common research technique.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture opens its interviews section by calling the interview the most common research technique, valued for deeper understanding through open \\\"why\\\" questions (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture calls interviews the most common technique.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"Tips for a good interview:\",\"o\":[[\"Listen well\",\"Listen well is one of the two keys during an interview, but take notes is the other, so the complete answer is \\\"a & c\\\" (L6, Tips for a good interview).\"],[\"Don't leave some time between interviews\",\"This reverses the real tip: the lecture says to leave some time between interviews (L6, Tips for a good interview).\"],[\"Take notes\",\"Take notes is one of the two keys, but listening well is the other, so choosing it alone is incomplete; the answer is \\\"a & c\\\" (L6, Tips for a good interview).\"],[\"a & c\",\"Correct. Listen well AND take notes are the two keys.\"]],\"a\":3,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"\\\"Individual interviews are easier to coordinate than group interviews.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Individuals: \\\"easier to coordinate the logistics\\\".\"],[\"False\",\"The comparison table lists easier logistics for individuals.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25 & 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Why are interviews more expensive than surveys?\",\"o\":[[\"They need special software\",\"Special software is not mentioned. Interviews cost more because they are synchronous (time per interviewee) and interviewees often expect incentives (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"They are synchronous and interviewees often expect incentives\",\"Correct. Interviews are synchronous, so the interviewer devotes time to each person, and interviewees often expect compensation (incentives) (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"They must be done online\",\"Interviews do not have to be online, and that is not the cost reason. It is the synchronous time per interviewee plus incentives (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"They collect only numbers\",\"Interviews collect mostly qualitative data (open-ended \\\"why\\\" answers), not numbers, and that has nothing to do with their cost (L6, Interviews).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"People giving answers that are more ideal than reality is called:\",\"o\":[[\"Recall bias\",\"Recall bias is inaccurate memory of past events.\"],[\"Reporting bias\",\"Correct. Reporting bias is when people give answers more ideal than reality; recall bias is inaccurate answers about past events (L6, Challenges of asking questions).\"],[\"Selection bias\",\"Selection bias is not one of the lecture's biases. People reporting an idealised version of reality is reporting bias (L6, Challenges of asking questions).\"],[\"Confirmation bias\",\"Confirmation bias is not covered here. The lecture names two biases, recall and reporting; answers more ideal than reality are reporting bias (L6, Challenges).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is an advantage of GROUP interviews?\",\"o\":[[\"More privacy for participants\",\"That is an advantage of individual interviews.\"],[\"Participants' discussion can highlight assumptions\",\"Correct. In group interviews participants' discussion can highlight assumptions, and it is easier to get more feedback (L6, Individuals vs groups).\"],[\"Easier logistics\",\"Individual interviews are easier to coordinate.\"],[\"No confounding personalities\",\"The reverse: with groups you may need to deal with confounding personalities, which is listed as a group drawback (L6, Individuals vs groups).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which type of research gives a deeper understanding of user motivations and behaviors?\",\"o\":[[\"Quantitative research\",\"Quantitative research gives numbers (measures, counts) that show what happens, not why. Motivations need qualitative research (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"Secondary research only\",\"Secondary research is someone else's data, which may be too old or not specific enough to your users, so it cannot give deep insight on its own (L4, Primary vs secondary).\"],[\"Experimental research only\",\"Experiments are just one primary method, not one of the WHAT categories. Understanding why and how users behave is qualitative research (L4, Types by WHAT).\"],[\"Qualitative research\",\"Correct. It covers why and how things happen.\"]],\"a\":3}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"Joyner says interviews should focus on the \\\"6 Ws\\\". Which of these is <b>NOT</b> one of them?\",\"o\":[[\"Where\",\"\\\"Where\\\" is one of Joyner's 6 Ws (who, what, where, when, why, how), so it is not the odd one out (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"How\",\"\\\"How\\\" is in the 6 Ws list (who, what, where, when, why, how), even though it does not start with W (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"Why\",\"\\\"Why\\\" is one of the 6 Ws, and interviews are valued for exactly these open \\\"why\\\" questions (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"Which\",\"Correct. The 6 Ws are who, what, where, when, why and how. \\\"Which\\\" is not one of them.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> In an interview, a user is asked how many times they used the app last March, and gives an inaccurate number because they don't remember well. This is:\",\"o\":[[\"Recall bias\",\"Correct. Recall bias is giving inaccurate answers about things in the past.\"],[\"Reporting bias\",\"Reporting bias is giving answers more ideal than reality, not forgetting.\"],[\"A \\\"presentation of self\\\" issue\",\"That is a group-interview issue about how people want to appear to others.\"],[\"A confounding personality\",\"That is a group-interview issue, not memory error.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Individual interviews give participants more privacy than group interviews.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. \\\"More privacy for participants\\\" is listed as an advantage of interviewing individuals.\"],[\"False\",\"The table lists more privacy as an advantage of individual interviews.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"What is the lecture's solution to the challenges of data gathered by asking questions (recall bias, reporting bias, etc.)?\",\"o\":[[\"Replace interviews with surveys only\",\"Surveys also gather data by asking questions, so they have the same biases.\"],[\"Combine with other techniques such as participant observation, usability studies and secondary research\",\"Correct. This is the solution given in the lecture.\"],[\"Ask more leading questions to get clearer answers\",\"Leading questions add bias instead of reducing it.\"],[\"Skip the analysis of qualitative data\",\"Analysis is hard, but skipping it is not the solution.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> An interviewer works alone, asks all the questions, and relies only on an audio recording. Which interview tip is NOT being followed?\",\"o\":[[\"Leave some time between interviews\",\"Nothing in the scenario is about timing between interviews.\"],[\"Plan ahead and prepare questions\",\"Nothing suggests the questions were not prepared.\"],[\"Take notes: one person asks, one takes notes; audio recording is not enough\",\"Correct. The lecture says one person should ask while another takes notes (including emotions and reactions), and audio alone is not enough.\"],[\"Interview groups instead of individuals\",\"This is not a tip; individual and group interviews each have pros and cons.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"Which is a <b>disadvantage</b> of group interviews?\",\"o\":[[\"More privacy for participants\",\"That is an advantage of individual interviews.\"],[\"Easier to coordinate the logistics\",\"That is an advantage of individual interviews.\"],[\"More time and effort spent with each participant\",\"That is a feature of individual interviews.\"],[\"You may need to deal with confounding personalities and \\\"presentation of self\\\" issues\",\"Correct. These are the issues listed for group interviews.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"Joyner says design is very easy if you follow a few principles.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Joyner says the opposite: \\\"design is very hard\\\". It is Klemmer who adds that pretty good interfaces are easy if you know basic methods and principles (L6, Design: can I?).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Joyner's quote is \\\"design is very hard\\\". Klemmer adds that great UIs need creativity and hard work, while pretty good ones are easy with basic methods (L6, Design: can I?).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"During a good interview, who should talk most of the time?\",\"o\":[[\"The user (interviewee)\",\"Correct. \\\"Listen well\\\": the user should talk most of the time.\"],[\"The interviewer\",\"The interviewer should listen well, not dominate.\"],[\"The note-taker\",\"The note-taker records; they should not lead the talk.\"],[\"Whoever is most senior on the team\",\"The lecture says the user should talk most of the time.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Scott Klemmer says designing great user interfaces requires enormous creativity and a lot of hard work, but:\",\"o\":[[\"good interfaces are impossible without artistic drawing talent\",\"Designers say \\\"we are not artists\\\"; drawing talent is not required.\"],[\"pretty good interfaces are easy if you know basic methods, techniques and principles\",\"Correct. This is the rest of Klemmer's point.\"],[\"only professional artists should design interfaces\",\"The lecture says design does not produce wall-hanging artwork.\"],[\"creativity should be turned off to follow the rules\",\"The lecture says not to turn off creativity.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Interviews and surveys can be done in sequence in either order, using the results of one to plan the other.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says interviews and surveys can be done in sequence in either order, with the results of one used to plan the other (L6, Interviews).\"],[\"False\",\"Either order is allowed; each can be used to plan the other.\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":7,\"title\":\"Design Alternatives and Prototyping\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Biggest mistakes a designer can make (Joyner)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Biggest mistake</b>: jumping straight to designing an interface <b>without understanding the users or the task</b>.\",\"<b>Second biggest</b>: settling on a <b>single design idea</b> or a single genre of ideas <b>too early</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Design alternatives\",\"pts\":[\"A separate phase in Joyner's cycle. Explore a wide range of alternatives instead of focusing on one from the start.\",\"The mistake is common when <b>enhancing an existing system</b>, because you are captured by its current interface. Advice: <b>distance yourself from existing solutions</b>, at least during brainstorming.\",\"The final design is often a <b>combination</b> of alternatives explored earlier.\",\"How many alternatives? <b>As many as you can.</b>\",\"Example: the <b>first Microsoft mouse</b> had <b>more than 100 prototypes</b>.\"]},{\"h\":\"Prototyping\",\"pts\":[\"Most people think a prototype is a quick or small version of the real thing. That is not a useful understanding.\",\"For designers: <b>\\\"Rapidly creating an approximation of a design so that you can quickly get feedback.\\\"</b>\",\"Klemmer: <b>\\\"A prototype is not about the artifact, it's about feedback and iteration.\\\"</b>\",\"Joyner: design is an iterative process of brainstorming, combining, abandoning, revising and improving ideas, which requires several ideas to start with.\",\"A good prototype enables <b>useful (constructive) feedback</b> and iteration. It does not need to resemble the final product.\",\"Prototypes that give no new knowledge waste the design process. Klemmer: <b>\\\"prototypes are questions, ask a lot of them.\\\"</b>\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Joyner: biggest design mistake\",\"Jumping straight to designing without understanding the users or the task.\"],[\"Joyner: second biggest mistake\",\"Settling on a single design idea (or genre) too early.\"],[\"Advice when enhancing an existing system\",\"Distance yourself from existing solutions during brainstorming.\"],[\"How many design alternatives?\",\"As many as you can.\"],[\"First Microsoft mouse\",\"More than 100 prototypes.\"],[\"Prototype (designer definition)\",\"Rapidly creating an approximation of a design to get feedback quickly.\"],[\"Klemmer on prototypes\",\"Not about the artifact; about feedback and iteration. \\\"Prototypes are questions, ask a lot of them.\\\"\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What are the biggest mistakes a designer can make?\",\"According to Joyner: (1) jumping straight to designing without understanding the users or the task; (2) settling on a single design idea or genre too early.\"],[\"What is a prototype and why is it important?\",\"Rapidly creating an approximation of a design to get quick feedback. It matters because of feedback and iteration, not the artifact itself. A good prototype enables useful, constructive feedback.\"],[\"Why should designers explore many alternatives?\",\"Focusing on one alternative early is a big mistake. Exploring widely leads to better designs, and the final design is often a combination of alternatives. Aim for as many as possible (e.g. 100+ prototypes for the first Microsoft mouse).\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"According to Joyner, the biggest mistake a designer can make is:\",\"o\":[[\"Not using enough prototypes\",\"Too few prototypes is not one of Joyner's two named mistakes. The biggest is jumping to design without understanding the users or the task (L7, Biggest mistakes).\"],[\"Jumping straight to designing an interface without understanding the users\",\"Correct. Joyner's biggest mistake is jumping straight to designing an interface without understanding the users or the task; the second is settling on one idea too early (L7, Biggest mistakes).\"],[\"Focusing too much on user feedback\",\"User feedback is encouraged throughout: prototypes exist to get feedback and iterate. Joyner's biggest mistake is designing without understanding the users (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"Settling on a single design idea too late\",\"The second mistake is settling on one idea too EARLY.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"A common mistake when enhancing an existing system is:\",\"o\":[[\"Exploring too many design alternatives\",\"Exploring many alternatives is the advice, not the mistake: aim for as many as you can. The mistake is being captured by the current interface (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"Being captured by the current interface\",\"Correct. Distance yourself from existing solutions.\"],[\"Ignoring user feedback\",\"Ignoring feedback is bad, but the mistake tied to enhancing an existing system is being captured by its current interface (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"Spending too much time on prototypes\",\"Time on prototypes is not the named mistake; prototypes are encouraged. Enhancing a system tempts you to stay captured by its current interface (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"How many prototypes were produced for the first Microsoft mouse?\",\"o\":[[\"10\",\"Far too few. The lecture's example of exploring alternatives is the first Microsoft mouse, with more than 100 prototypes (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"50\",\"Too few: the first Microsoft mouse went through more than 100 prototypes, used to show you should explore as many alternatives as you can (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"100\",\"Close, but the lecture says more than 100, not exactly 100, so \\\"More than 100\\\" is the precise answer (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"More than 100\",\"Correct. The first Microsoft mouse had more than 100 prototypes, the lecture's example of exploring as many design alternatives as you can (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"According to Klemmer, prototypes should be considered as:\",\"o\":[[\"Final products\",\"Prototypes are the opposite of final products: rapidly made approximations of a design for quick feedback (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"Questions that need to be answered\",\"Correct. \\\"Prototypes are questions, ask a lot of them.\\\"\"],[\"Artifacts to showcase\",\"Klemmer says \\\"a prototype is not about the artifact, it's about feedback and iteration\\\", so they are not things to show off (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"Simple mock-ups\",\"Seeing a prototype as just a simple mock-up misses its purpose. Klemmer treats prototypes as questions that gather feedback (L7, Prototyping).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"A prototype is one that enables you to get useful feedback and iterate on your design.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture defines a good prototype as one that enables useful (constructive) feedback and lets you iterate on the design (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"False\",\"This is the lecture's definition of a good prototype.\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25\"},{\"q\":\"A good prototype is characterized by:\",\"o\":[[\"Resembling the final product closely\",\"The lecture says it need not resemble the final product.\"],[\"Enabling useful feedback and iteration\",\"Correct. A good prototype enables useful, constructive feedback and iteration; it does not need to resemble the final product (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"Being a quick version of the final design\",\"\\\"A quick or small version of the real thing\\\" is the common view the lecture calls not useful. A prototype is an approximation made to get feedback (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"Having a polished appearance\",\"Polish is not the goal: a prototype need not resemble the final product, and finished-looking ones get less constructive feedback than sketches (L7; L8, Lo-fi).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"How many design alternatives should you aim for early on?\",\"o\":[[\"One\",\"Settling on one is the second biggest mistake.\"],[\"As many as possible\",\"Correct. Explore a wide range rather than one idea from the start: aim for as many alternatives as you can, like the 100+ prototypes of the first Microsoft mouse (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"Exactly three\",\"The lecture sets no fixed number like three. Its answer is \\\"as many as you can\\\" (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"None\",\"Design alternatives is a whole phase of Joyner's cycle, and skipping it means settling on one idea early, the second biggest mistake (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What is the purpose of prototyping according to designers?\",\"o\":[[\"To create a final product quickly\",\"This is the common but not useful understanding the lecture rejects: 'a quick / small version of the real thing' (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"To get feedback and iterate on designs\",\"Correct. For designers, prototyping is 'rapidly creating an approximation of a design so that you can quickly <b>get feedback</b>', and Klemmer says a prototype is about <b>feedback and iteration</b> (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"To replace the need for user testing\",\"Prototypes are how you get feedback from users, not a replacement for it; tactical research 'requires user feedback through use of prototype' (L3, Types of user research; L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"To finalize the design immediately\",\"The lecture describes design as an iterative process of brainstorming, combining, abandoning, revising and improving ideas, so a prototype is for iterating, not finalizing at once (L7, Prototyping).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What should designers do during brainstorming sessions?\",\"o\":[[\"Focus on existing solutions\",\"This is the opposite of the advice: being captured by the current interface is a very common mistake, so you should distance yourself from existing solutions (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"Distance themselves from current designs\",\"Correct. The lecture's advice is to 'try to <b>distance yourself from the existing solutions</b>, at least initially during the brainstorming sessions' (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"Settle on one idea quickly\",\"Settling on a single design idea too early is Joyner's second biggest mistake; brainstorming aims to generate a lot of ideas (L7, Biggest mistakes; L8, Brainstorming).\"],[\"Avoid feedback\",\"The design process depends on feedback: prototypes exist to get feedback and iterate, and team sessions share and add ideas (L7, Prototyping; L8, Brainstorming). Nothing says to avoid feedback.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The biggest mistake a designer can make is jumping straight to designing an interface without understanding the users or the task.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. According to Joyner, the <b>biggest mistake</b> is jumping straight to designing an interface without understanding the users or understanding the task (L7, Biggest mistakes a designer can make).\"],[\"False\",\"This is exactly Joyner's biggest mistake. Settling on a single design idea too early is his <b>second</b> biggest mistake, not the biggest (L7, Biggest mistakes a designer can make).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"It is advisable to settle on a single design idea early in the design process.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Joyner calls this the <b>second biggest mistake</b>: settling on a single design idea or a single genre of design ideas too early (L7, Biggest mistakes a designer can make).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Settling on a single design idea too early is Joyner's <b>second biggest mistake</b>. Instead, explore a wider range of design alternatives, as many as you can (L7, Biggest mistakes; Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Exploring a wider range of design alternatives is important for reaching a good design.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says: to reach a good design, <b>explore a wider range of possible design alternatives</b> (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"False\",\"The slide states the opposite: to reach a good design you should explore a wider range of possible design alternatives, as many as you can (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Focusing too strongly on one design alternative from the beginning is a common mistake.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The slide lists focusing too strongly on one alternative from the very beginning as a <b>big mistake</b>, and says it is very common when you enhance an existing system, because you are captured by its current interface (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture calls focusing too strongly on one alternative from the start a big mistake and says this mistake is <b>very common</b> when enhancing an existing system (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"It is beneficial to distance yourself from existing solutions during brainstorming sessions.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture's advice is to \\\"try to <b>distance yourself from the existing solutions</b>, at least initially during the brainstorming sessions\\\", so you are not captured by the current interface (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"False\",\"The slide gives this as advice: distance yourself from existing solutions, at least during brainstorming, because being captured by the current interface is a very common mistake (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Having several design alternatives is not important in the early stages of design.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The slide says the opposite: success in having several alternatives <b>is important in the early stages</b> of the design (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The lecture says success in having several alternatives is <b>important in the early stages</b> of the design, and you should have as many as you can (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The final design is usually a combination of the design alternatives explored earlier.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. You may choose one alternative and finalize it, but the lecture says it is <b>more common</b> that the final design is some combination of the alternatives you explored earlier (L7, Design alternatives).\"],[\"False\",\"The slide says choosing one alternative is possible, but it is more common that the final design is a <b>combination</b> of the alternatives explored earlier (L7, Design alternatives).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"The first Microsoft mouse had only a few prototypes produced.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture's example is the opposite: <b>more than 100 prototypes</b> were produced for the first Microsoft mouse (L7, Example).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The first Microsoft mouse had <b>more than 100 prototypes</b>, the lecture's example of exploring as many alternatives as you can (L7, Example).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"A prototype is simply a quick or small version of the final product.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says most people think of a prototype as a quick or small version of the real thing, but <b>this is not a useful understanding</b>. For designers it is rapidly creating an approximation of a design to get feedback quickly (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The slide says the \\\"quick / small version of the real thing\\\" view <b>is not a useful understanding</b>. For designers, prototyping is \\\"rapidly creating an approximation of a design so that you can quickly get feedback\\\" (L7, Prototyping).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Prototyping is important for obtaining feedback and iterating on designs.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Klemmer: \\\"prototype is not about the artifact, it's about <b>feedback and iteration</b>\\\". A good prototype is one that enables you to get useful feedback and iterate on your design (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says the value of a prototype is exactly feedback and iteration (Klemmer), and defines prototyping as getting feedback quickly (L7, Prototyping).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Good prototypes are those that closely resemble the final product.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The slide says a good prototype is one that enables useful feedback and iteration, <b>\\\"not a one that resembles your final product\\\"</b> (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. A good prototype enables you to get useful feedback and iterate on your design, <b>not one that resembles your final product</b> (L7, Prototyping).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Prototypes should generate useful and constructive feedback.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. A good prototype enables you to get <b>useful feedback</b>, and the slide equates useful feedback with <b>constructive feedback</b> (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says the purpose of a prototype is to get useful, constructive feedback; prototypes that give no new knowledge waste the design process (L7, Prototyping).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Prototypes are considered questions that should be asked frequently during the design process.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Klemmer says <b>\\\"prototypes are questions, ask a lot of them\\\"</b> (L7, Prototyping).\"],[\"False\",\"This is Klemmer's own advice: prototypes are questions, and you should ask a lot of them (L7, Prototyping).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> After good user research, a team sketches only three small variations of the same hamburger-menu layout on day one and commits to one. Which of Joyner's mistakes is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Producing too many prototypes\",\"Joyner wants as many alternatives as possible; the team made too few.\"],[\"Distancing themselves from existing solutions\",\"Distancing yourself is the advice, not the mistake.\"],[\"Settling on a single design idea or a single genre of ideas too early (the second biggest mistake)\",\"Correct. Three variations of one layout is one genre of ideas, chosen too early.\"],[\"Jumping to design without understanding the users\",\"The scenario says they did user research first; that is the biggest mistake, not this one.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A team redesigning a university's existing registration portal keeps sketching versions that look almost like the current portal. What does the lecture advise?\",\"o\":[[\"Copy the current interface, since users already know it\",\"Being captured by the current interface is exactly the mistake.\"],[\"Skip the design alternatives phase\",\"Design alternatives is a separate phase and should not be skipped.\"],[\"Go straight to a high-fidelity prototype of the current design\",\"This locks in the existing design even more.\"],[\"Distance yourself from existing solutions, at least during brainstorming\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's advice when enhancing an existing system.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"According to Joyner, the biggest mistake a designer can make is settling on a single design idea too early.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Settling on one idea too early is Joyner's second biggest mistake. The biggest is jumping to design without understanding the users or the task (L7, Biggest mistakes).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The biggest mistake is jumping to design without understanding the users or the task; settling on one idea too early is the <b>second</b> biggest.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"According to the lecture, the final design is often:\",\"o\":[[\"A combination of alternatives explored earlier\",\"Correct. The final design is often a combination of earlier alternatives.\"],[\"Always the very first idea\",\"Settling on the first idea is a mistake.\"],[\"The alternative with the most features\",\"The lecture does not choose designs by feature count.\"],[\"Chosen without any feedback\",\"Design is iterative and driven by feedback.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"What is the designer's definition of prototyping given in the lecture?\",\"o\":[[\"A quick or small version of the real thing\",\"The lecture says this common view is not a useful understanding.\"],[\"Rapidly creating an approximation of a design so that you can quickly get feedback\",\"Correct. This is the definition for designers.\"],[\"The final polished version handed to developers\",\"A prototype is an approximation for feedback, not the final product.\"],[\"A piece of art to impress clients\",\"Klemmer says a prototype is not about the artifact.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Complete Klemmer's quote: \\\"A prototype is not about the artifact, it's about ___.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"appearance and polish\",\"Polish is about the artifact, which Klemmer says is not the point.\"],[\"the code\",\"The quote has nothing to do with code.\"],[\"feedback and iteration\",\"Correct. \\\"A prototype is not about the artifact, it's about feedback and iteration.\\\"\"],[\"the final product\",\"A good prototype does not need to resemble the final product.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"Prototypes that give no new knowledge waste the design process.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says this, which is why Klemmer says \\\"prototypes are questions, ask a lot of them.\\\"\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture states that such prototypes waste the design process.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A team spends weeks building a polished prototype, shows it to users, and learns nothing new. How does the lecture judge this?\",\"o\":[[\"It is good, because it closely resembles the final product\",\"A good prototype does not need to resemble the final product.\"],[\"It is a success, because the artifact looks beautiful\",\"A prototype is not about the artifact.\"],[\"It proves that no design alternatives are needed\",\"You should explore as many alternatives as you can.\"],[\"It wasted part of the design process; prototypes should be questions that bring feedback\",\"Correct. Prototypes that give no new knowledge waste the process; \\\"prototypes are questions, ask a lot of them.\\\"\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"Joyner describes design as an iterative process of brainstorming, combining, abandoning, revising and improving ideas. This requires:\",\"o\":[[\"Several ideas to start with\",\"Correct. Joyner says this process requires several ideas to start with.\"],[\"One perfect idea from the beginning\",\"Settling on one idea early is a big mistake.\"],[\"Finished code before any feedback\",\"Prototypes are meant for quick feedback, not finished code.\"],[\"A high-fidelity prototype first\",\"Nothing in this lecture requires hi-fi first; ideas come first.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Design alternatives is a separate phase in Joyner's design life cycle.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The lecture says design alternatives is a separate phase in Joyner's cycle.\"],[\"False\",\"It is its own phase (Need finding → Design alternatives → Prototyping → Evaluation).\"]],\"a\":0}]},{\"n\":8,\"title\":\"Fidelity, Brainstorming, Wireframes and Forms\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Prototypes and fidelity\",\"pts\":[\"Prototypes communicate with stakeholders. Their four audiences are <b>colleagues, clients, users and yourself</b>.\",\"<b>Fidelity</b>: how closely a design matches the look and feel of the final product. There are <b>low-fidelity (lo-fi)</b> and <b>high-fidelity (hi-fi)</b> prototypes.\",\"Start with lo-fi, and move to hi-fi as the design proceeds.\"]},{\"h\":\"Power of low-fidelity prototypes\",\"pts\":[\"They are <b>faster, easier and cheaper</b> to make.\",\"Hi-fi is not possible at the beginning because many details have not been decided.\",\"<b>People are much more willing to talk</b> when given a sketch than something that looks finished.\",\"Konstan: a prototype that looks like little effort (e.g. hand-drawn) gets more <b>constructive feedback</b>.\",\"Too many details make people give feedback at a <b>lower level</b> than needed.\",\"Google: present <b>high-level ideas</b> early; focus on details and screens later.\"]},{\"h\":\"Design activities and brainstorming\",\"pts\":[\"Every design activity either helps the <b>designer think and visualize</b>, or helps <b>team members and stakeholders visualize and communicate</b>, or both.\",\"Brainstorming aims to generate a lot of ideas. Joyner: <b>start with individual brainstorming</b>, because groups coalesce on ideas too early.\",\"Tips: write as many ideas as you can; each idea is a few words or a sentence; <b>aim for 20</b>; <b>do not evaluate or exclude</b> any idea; take breaks or use several sessions; team sessions are for sharing and adding ideas, not narrowing.\"]},{\"h\":\"Why study design? And the design-development process\",\"pts\":[\"Some students will shine as designers; developers often do design when there are no designers; developers work better with designers when they understand the process.\",\"<b>Handoff process</b>: design finishes, then it is handed over to development (sequential).\",\"<b>Handshake process</b>: design and development run <b>in parallel</b> with frequent sync points.\"]},{\"h\":\"Wireframes\",\"pts\":[\"A <b>schematic for the basic outline of a screen</b>. It is built from lines and basic shapes (rectangles, circles) with some text, and the lines look like wires.\",\"It intentionally has <b>no colors or font styles</b>, to focus on structure. Wireframe = <b>skeleton with placeholders</b>.\",\"Google calls it a \\\"key part of the design process\\\".\",\"Wireframes are not art. They organize and communicate information clearly.\",\"Purposes: (1) decide the basic page structure before colors and images; (2) get the team on the same page early, saving time and resources; (3) direct feedback to the right level (structure and function, not colors and typography); (4) catch forgotten or disorganized elements early.\",\"When: <b>after foundational user research</b>, as soon as there is an initial <b>site map</b> (content hierarchy).\"]},{\"h\":\"Wireframing standards and process\",\"pts\":[\"Standards: text as horizontal lines or placeholder text; images as rectangles or circles with an <b>X</b>; calls to action as rectangles or circles; proper <b>annotation</b>.\",\"Process: start with <b>pen and paper</b>. The first goal is <b>reaching</b> a design, not showing one. Sketch <b>at least 5 versions</b> per screen. Iterate with feedback. The second goal is to move an idea from your mind to paper quickly, so ignore straight lines, spacing and alignment at this stage.\",\"Then use tools: <b>Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq, Pencil</b>.\",\"Example in the lecture: a dog-walking app.\"]},{\"h\":\"Form design guidelines\",\"pts\":[\"Important commands are <b>buttons, not links</b>, because buttons catch the eye. But do <b>not</b> display all commands as buttons.\",\"<b>Breadcrumbs</b> show <b>where</b> users are, when there are multiple choices. <b>Progress bars</b> show where users are <b>and what is left</b>, for a predefined unique path.\",\"A successful form submission is confirmed with a <b>confirmation page</b>.\",\"<b>Error messages sit next to the field</b> where the error occurred. Changing the field border color (e.g. red) is preferred.\",\"Text boxes provide <b>plenty of space</b> for the answer.\",\"Fields contain <b>hints, examples or required formats</b>.\",\"<b>Remove repetitive actions</b>: remember customers' preferences.\",\"Make a <b>clear distinction between primary and secondary calls-to-action</b> (e.g. \\\"Go Pro\\\" stands out over \\\"Free Trial\\\").\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Four audiences of prototypes\",\"Colleagues, clients, users, yourself.\"],[\"Fidelity\",\"How closely a design matches the look and feel of the final product.\"],[\"Why lo-fi first? (key reason)\",\"People are more willing to talk and give constructive feedback on a sketch than on something that looks finished.\"],[\"Brainstorming: individual or group first?\",\"Individual first; groups coalesce ideas too early.\"],[\"Brainstorming target\",\"Aim for 20 ideas; do not evaluate any.\"],[\"Wireframe\",\"Schematic of a screen's basic outline: lines, shapes, some text, no colors or fonts. A skeleton with placeholders.\"],[\"When to wireframe\",\"After foundational research, once there is an initial site map.\"],[\"Wireframe image placeholder\",\"A rectangle or circle with an X.\"],[\"Wireframing tools\",\"Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq, Pencil.\"],[\"Handoff vs Handshake\",\"Handoff: design then development (sequential). Handshake: parallel with frequent syncs.\"],[\"Breadcrumbs vs progress bar\",\"Breadcrumbs: where you are. Progress bar: where you are and what is left (fixed path).\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What is the power of low-fidelity prototypes?\",\"They are faster, easier and cheaper. Hi-fi is impossible early because details are undecided. People are more willing to talk about a sketch, and a hand-drawn prototype gets more constructive feedback (Konstan). Too much detail pushes feedback to a lower level than needed. Google: present high-level ideas early and details later.\"],[\"Define wireframes and give their purposes.\",\"A schematic for the basic outline of a screen, built from lines and basic shapes with some text, intentionally without colors or fonts, to focus on structure (a skeleton with placeholders). Purposes: decide structure before visuals; get the team aligned early and save resources; get feedback at the right level; catch forgotten or disorganized elements early.\"],[\"What are the types of the design-development process?\",\"Handoff: design is completed and handed to development, one after the other. Handshake: design and development run in parallel with frequent sync points.\"],[\"Give tips for effective brainstorming.\",\"Start individually. Write as many ideas as you can, each a few words or a sentence. Aim for 20. Do not evaluate or exclude ideas. Take breaks or use several sessions. Team sessions are for sharing and adding ideas, not narrowing choices.\"],[\"What are the wireframing industry standards?\",\"Text as horizontal lines or placeholder text; images as rectangles or circles with an X; calls to action as rectangles or circles; proper text annotations.\"],[\"How do prototypes serve communication in design, and what are the four audiences they serve?\",\"Prototypes serve as a way to <b>communicate with stakeholders</b>. They serve four distinct audiences: 1) Colleagues 2) Clients 3) Users 4) Yourself.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"],[\"Describe the wireframing process. Which tools are commonly used for wireframing?\",\"1) Start with <b>pen and paper</b>.<br>2) First goal: <b>reaching a design, not showing a design</b>.<br>3) For each screen, sketch alternative wireframe versions (at least 5 versions).<br>4) Go through some iterations with feedback if available.<br>5) Second goal: a wireframe transfers an idea from your mind to paper as quickly and as easily as possible, so do not focus on details that slow the process, such as straight lines, spacing and alignment, at this stage.<br>6) Make use of tools after the initial iterations of paper wireframes. Commonly used tools: Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq, Pencil.\",\"Student summary \\\"HCI for final\\\"; answer checked against the lectures\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"\\\"High fidelity prototypes are faster, easier and cheaper to generate than low fidelity.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Reversed: LOW-fidelity prototypes are faster, easier and cheaper.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The statement is reversed: it is low-fidelity prototypes that are faster, easier and cheaper to make, which is why you start lo-fi and move to hi-fi later (L8, Power of lo-fi).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Exam 2024/25 & 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"A key advantage of low-fidelity prototypes is:\",\"o\":[[\"They are expensive and slow, which ensures quality\",\"The opposite: lo-fi prototypes are faster, easier and cheaper to make, and that is their power (L8, Power of lo-fi).\"],[\"They provide detailed animations and realistic UI\",\"Detailed, realistic UI is high fidelity, a close match to the final look and feel. Lo-fi prototypes are rough sketches (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"],[\"They allow quick feedback and rapid iteration\",\"Correct. Lo-fi prototypes are fast and cheap, and people talk more freely about a sketch, giving constructive feedback you can quickly iterate on (L8, Power of lo-fi).\"],[\"They eliminate the need for high-fidelity prototypes\",\"Lo-fi does not replace hi-fi: you start with lo-fi and move to hi-fi as the design proceeds (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of a wireframe?\",\"o\":[[\"To showcase the final design with colors and images\",\"Wireframes intentionally have no colors or font styles. Showing the final visual design is the job of hi-fi prototypes, not wireframes (L8, Wireframes).\"],[\"To test backend functionality\",\"Wireframes say nothing about backend code; they are a schematic of a screen's outline, used to decide page structure (L8, Wireframes).\"],[\"To outline the structure and layout of a page or app without detailed design\",\"Correct. A wireframe is a schematic for the basic outline of a screen, a skeleton with placeholders, with no colors or fonts so that it focuses on structure (L8, Wireframes).\"],[\"To write the code for the user interface\",\"Wireframes are drawn, not coded: pen and paper first, then tools like Figma or Balsamiq. They decide structure before any visual design or code (L8, Wireframes).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"Which tools are commonly used to create wireframes?\",\"o\":[[\"Visual Studio and Eclipse\",\"Visual Studio and Eclipse are IDEs for writing code. The lecture's wireframing tools are Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"Microsoft Word and Excel\",\"Word and Excel are office tools, not in the lecture's list. Wireframing tools are Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"Balsamiq, Figma and Sketch\",\"Correct. All three are in the lecture's list (with Adobe XD and Pencil).\"],[\"Final Cut Pro and Adobe Premiere\",\"Final Cut Pro and Premiere are video editors. The listed wireframing tools are Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil (L8, Wireframing process).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Exam 2025/26\"},{\"q\":\"When should wireframes be created?\",\"o\":[[\"Before any user research\",\"Too early: wireframes are made after foundational user research, once you have an initial site map (L8, Wireframes).\"],[\"After foundational user research and an initial site map\",\"Correct. Wireframes come after foundational user research, as soon as there is an initial site map (content hierarchy) to lay out (L8, Wireframes).\"],[\"Only after hi-fi prototypes\",\"Wireframes are lo-fi skeletons, and the lecture says start lo-fi and move to hi-fi, so they come before hi-fi prototypes (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"],[\"At the end of the design process\",\"Wireframes come early: their purpose is to fix structure and catch forgotten elements before colors and images, after foundational research (L8, Wireframes).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is NOT a wireframing industry standard?\",\"o\":[[\"Text as horizontal lines or placeholder text\",\"Showing text as horizontal lines or placeholder text is one of the wireframing standards, so it is not the answer (L8, Wireframing standards).\"],[\"Images as rectangles or circles with an X\",\"Images drawn as rectangles or circles with an X is a listed wireframing standard, so it is not the answer (L8, Wireframing standards).\"],[\"Detailed color schemes and typography\",\"Correct. Wireframes intentionally avoid colors and font styles.\"],[\"Calls to action as rectangles or circles\",\"Calls to action drawn as rectangles or circles is a listed standard. The odd one out is detailed colors and typography (L8, Wireframing standards).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"According to Joyner, brainstorming should start:\",\"o\":[[\"As a group, so ideas merge early\",\"Groups coalesce ideas too early, which is the problem.\"],[\"Individually\",\"Correct. Each member first builds their own set of ideas.\"],[\"With evaluation of each idea\",\"Brainstorming tips say not to evaluate or exclude any idea; the aim is to generate as many as possible, about 20 (L8, Brainstorming).\"],[\"With the final design\",\"Starting with a final design defeats the point of brainstorming, which is to generate many ideas, beginning individually (L8, Brainstorming).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Where should an error message be displayed in a form?\",\"o\":[[\"In a popup after submitting\",\"The guideline is to show it next to the field.\"],[\"Next to the input field where the error occurred\",\"Correct. Error messages sit next to the field where the error occurred, so the user sees exactly what to fix; changing the field border color (e.g. red) is preferred (L8, Form design).\"],[\"At the bottom of the page\",\"The bottom of the page is far from the field that caused the error. The guideline puts the message next to that field (L8, Form design).\"],[\"In the page title\",\"The page title is not where form errors go; the user would have to find the field. Errors belong next to the faulty input (L8, Form design).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which shows where users are AND what is left to do, on a predefined path?\",\"o\":[[\"Breadcrumbs\",\"Breadcrumbs show only where users are, when there are multiple choices.\"],[\"Progress bar\",\"Correct. A progress bar shows where users are and what is left, on a predefined unique path; breadcrumbs only show where they are (L8, Form design).\"],[\"Tabs\",\"Tabs switch between sections but do not show steps left on a fixed path; that is a progress bar's job (L8, Form design).\"],[\"Links\",\"Links are for navigating, not for showing position. The guideline also says important commands should be buttons, not links (L8, Form design).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"The prototype audience list includes:\",\"o\":[[\"Colleagues, clients, users and yourself\",\"Correct. Prototypes communicate with stakeholders, and the lecture names four audiences: colleagues, clients, users and yourself (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"],[\"Only users\",\"Too narrow: users are one audience, but prototypes also serve colleagues, clients and yourself (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"],[\"Only managers\",\"Managers are not in the list. The four audiences are colleagues, clients, users and yourself (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"],[\"Competitors\",\"Competitors are not stakeholders you prototype for. The audiences are colleagues, clients, users and yourself (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of a wireframe?\",\"o\":[[\"To create a visually appealing design\",\"Wireframes are not art: they intentionally have no colors or font styles so the focus stays on structure (L8, Wireframes).\"],[\"To organize and communicate information clearly\",\"Correct. 'A good wireframe is not about artistic drawing, it is all about <b>organizing and communicating information clearly</b> to colleagues' (L8, Wireframes are not art).\"],[\"To finalize the color scheme\",\"A wireframe decides the basic structure of a page <b>before</b> visual considerations like color are added, and it has no colors on purpose (L8, Wireframes).\"],[\"To replace the need for user testing\",\"Wireframes direct stakeholders and users to give feedback at the right level, so they invite feedback rather than replace it (L8, Wireframes are not art).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"What is the first step in the wireframing process?\",\"o\":[[\"Using digital tools to create wireframes\",\"Tools such as Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil come later: 'make use of tools after initial iterations of paper wireframes' (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"Sketching alternative wireframe versions on paper\",\"Correct. The process starts 'with <b>pen and paper</b>', sketching alternative versions of each screen (at least 5) (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"Finalizing the design layout\",\"The first goal is 'reaching a design, not showing a design'; you iterate on several versions, so nothing is final at the start (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"Conducting user testing\",\"Feedback comes after you have sketches: you 'go through some iterations with feedback if available' (L8, Wireframing process). Testing is not the first wireframing step.\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Which of the following tools is commonly used for wireframing?\",\"o\":[[\"Microsoft Word\",\"Word is a word processor and is not in the lecture's list of wireframing tools (Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq, Pencil) (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"Adobe Photoshop\",\"Photoshop is not in the lecture's list; the Adobe tool listed for wireframing is <b>Adobe XD</b> (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"Figma\",\"Correct. <b>Figma</b> is first in the lecture's list of commonly used wireframing tools: Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq, Pencil (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"PowerPoint\",\"PowerPoint is a presentation program and is not one of the wireframing tools the lecture lists (L8, Wireframing process).\"]],\"a\":2,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"A well-designed user interface should provide clear and immediate feedback to user actions.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. The form and navigation guidelines all give users clear feedback on what they did: a successful form submission is confirmed with a <b>confirmation page</b>, error messages appear next to the field where the error occurred, and anything clickable shows an obvious change on hover (L8, Form design guidelines; L9, Navigation).\"],[\"False\",\"The guidelines say the opposite: confirm a successful submission with a confirmation page, show errors next to the field, and show an obvious change on hover, so users always see the result of their actions (L8, Form design guidelines; L9, Navigation).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a midterm question; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"A wireframe is primarily focused on artistic drawing and aesthetics.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says <b>wireframes are not art</b>: a good wireframe is not about artistic drawing, it is about organizing and communicating information clearly. It intentionally has no colors or font styles, to focus on structure (L8, Wireframes are not art).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. A good wireframe is <b>not about artistic drawing</b>; it is all about organizing and communicating information clearly to colleagues, and it intentionally leaves out colors and font styles to focus on structure (L8, Wireframes; Wireframes are not art).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"At the pen-and-paper stage, the goal of wireframing is to reach a design quickly without focusing on details like straight lines and spacing.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. In the pen-and-paper stage, the first goal is <b>reaching a design, not showing a design</b>, and the second is moving an idea from your mind to paper as quickly as possible, so there is no focus on details such as straight lines, spacing and alignment (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"False\",\"The slide says exactly this for the pen-and-paper stage: reach a design (not show one), get the idea onto paper quickly, and do not focus on straight lines, spacing and alignment at this stage (L8, Wireframing process).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Wireframes help in catching problems early in the design process by focusing on structure and function.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. Two of the listed purposes of a wireframe are directing feedback to the right level (<b>structure and function</b>, not colors and typography) and <b>early catching of problems</b> from a forgotten or disorganized element (L8, Wireframes are not art).\"],[\"False\",\"The slide lists both points as purposes of a wireframe: feedback about structure and function, and early catching of problems caused by forgotten or disorganized elements (L8, Wireframes are not art).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"It is recommended to create only one version of a wireframe before moving to digital tools.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The lecture says to sketch <b>at least 5 versions</b> of each screen on paper and go through some iterations before moving to tools like Figma or Balsamiq (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. For each screen you sketch alternative wireframe versions, <b>at least 5</b>, and go through iterations; tools are used only after initial iterations of paper wireframes (L8, Wireframing process).\"]],\"a\":1,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"},{\"q\":\"Proper annotation is important in wireframing to make the wireframe clear to stakeholders.\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. One of the wireframing industry standards is <b>proper annotation</b> with text to make the wireframe clear, and wireframes are about communicating information clearly to colleagues and stakeholders (L8, Wireframing industry standards).\"],[\"False\",\"Proper annotation is listed as a wireframing industry standard, used to make the wireframe clear to others (L8, Wireframing industry standards).\"]],\"a\":0,\"src\":\"Student summary \\\"ليلة الفاينل في HCI\\\" and \\\"HCI for final\\\", listed there as a question from last term's final; answer checked against the lectures\"}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"What does <b>fidelity</b> mean for a prototype?\",\"o\":[[\"The number of screens in the prototype\",\"Fidelity is about look and feel, not screen count.\"],[\"How closely a design matches the look and feel of the final product\",\"Correct. This is the lecture's definition of fidelity.\"],[\"How fast the prototype was made\",\"Lo-fi is faster, but speed is not the definition.\"],[\"How many users tested the prototype\",\"Fidelity has nothing to do with the number of testers.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"What did Konstan say about prototypes?\",\"o\":[[\"High-fidelity prototypes always get the most useful feedback\",\"Konstan's point favors prototypes that look like little effort.\"],[\"Prototypes should never be shown to users\",\"Users are one of the four audiences of prototypes.\"],[\"A prototype that looks like little effort (e.g. hand-drawn) gets more constructive feedback\",\"Correct. This is Konstan's point in the lecture.\"],[\"Adding many details makes people give higher-level feedback\",\"Too many details make people give feedback at a <b>lower</b> level than needed.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"Wireframes intentionally have no colors or font styles.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. They leave out colors and font styles to focus on structure (a skeleton with placeholders).\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture says wireframes intentionally have no colors or font styles.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> At a company, designers and developers work in parallel and meet often to sync. Which process is this?\",\"o\":[[\"Handoff process\",\"Handoff is sequential: design finishes, then it goes to development.\"],[\"Brainstorming\",\"Brainstorming generates ideas; it is not a design-development process.\"],[\"Wireframing\",\"Wireframing is a design activity, not a way of organizing design and development.\"],[\"Handshake process\",\"Correct. In the handshake process, design and development run in parallel with frequent sync points.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"When brainstorming, how many ideas does the lecture tell you to aim for?\",\"o\":[[\"20\",\"Correct. The brainstorming tips say to aim for 20 ideas, each a few words or a sentence, without evaluating or excluding any (L8, Brainstorming).\"],[\"5\",\"5 is the minimum number of sketch versions per screen when wireframing.\"],[\"3\",\"3 is the number of colors in a simple palette (next lecture), not ideas.\"],[\"More than 100\",\"More than 100 is the number of prototypes for the first Microsoft mouse.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Since buttons catch the eye, all commands in a form should be displayed as buttons.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"The guideline has a limit: important commands should be buttons, not links, but you should not display all commands as buttons (L8, Form design).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Important commands should be buttons, not links, but do <b>not</b> display all commands as buttons.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"When wireframing with pen and paper, how many versions of each screen should you sketch at least?\",\"o\":[[\"1\",\"One version means settling on a single idea.\"],[\"5\",\"Correct. The process says to sketch at least 5 versions per screen.\"],[\"20\",\"20 is the number of ideas to aim for when brainstorming.\"],[\"3\",\"3 is too few: the wireframing process says to sketch at least 5 versions of each screen with pen and paper (L8, Wireframing process).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A pricing page shows \\\"Go Pro\\\" and \\\"Free Trial\\\" as two identical grey buttons side by side. Which form guideline does it violate?\",\"o\":[[\"Error messages sit next to the field\",\"There is no error here, so the error-message guideline does not apply. The flaw is two calls-to-action looking identical (L8, Form design).\"],[\"Confirm a successful submission with a confirmation page\",\"Confirmation pages follow a successful submission, and nothing is submitted here. Identical buttons break the primary vs secondary CTA rule (L8, Form design).\"],[\"Make a clear distinction between primary and secondary calls-to-action\",\"Correct. The lecture's example is \\\"Go Pro\\\" standing out over \\\"Free Trial\\\".\"],[\"Remove repetitive actions\",\"Removing repetitive actions means remembering preferences, and nothing repeats here. Identical buttons break the primary vs secondary CTA rule (L8, Form design).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> An online store lets users reach a product through many different category choices. Which element should show users where they are?\",\"o\":[[\"A progress bar\",\"Progress bars are for a predefined unique path, showing what is left.\"],[\"A confirmation page\",\"A confirmation page confirms a successful form submission.\"],[\"A text box with hints\",\"Hints help fill in fields; they don't show location.\"],[\"Breadcrumbs\",\"Correct. Breadcrumbs show where users are when there are multiple choices.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"\\\"At the first pen-and-paper wireframing stage, you should make sure lines are straight and spacing and alignment are correct.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"At this stage the goal is to move the idea from your mind to paper quickly, so the lecture says to ignore straight lines, spacing and alignment (L8, Wireframing process).\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The goal is to move the idea from your mind to paper quickly, so ignore straight lines, spacing and alignment.\"]],\"a\":1}]},{\"n\":9,\"title\":\"Forms, Layout and Navigation Guidelines\",\"notes\":[{\"h\":\"Forms (continued)\",\"pts\":[\"<b>Stick to a simple 3-color palette.</b> More than 3 primary colors is difficult and confuses users.\",\"<b>Alert messages are consistent</b> across the whole site. Do not change color, style or placement.\",\"<b>Close vs Cancel</b>: if there is only one button it should be <b>\\\"Close\\\"</b>. With two buttons (an Action button and one to dismiss) use <b>\\\"Cancel\\\"</b>, not \\\"Close\\\". Cancel is most commonly placed <b>to the right</b> and is <b>not eye-catching</b>.\",\"<b>Error messages</b>: state the problem in the <b>title</b>; describe briefly <b>why it happened</b> under the title; when possible link to <b>complete the task or return</b> to the previous state; give recommendations for the <b>next course of action</b>.\",\"<b>System notice</b>: an \\\"Action\\\" button, a \\\"Remind me later\\\" button and a \\\"Don't show again\\\" box. Notice messages should not be lengthy or generic.\"]},{\"h\":\"Layout\",\"pts\":[\"The most important information is <b>above the fold</b> (the part visible without scrolling).\",\"<b>Related information is grouped</b> by a <b>frame</b> or a <b>separator</b>.\",\"<b>Color groups and structures items</b> (e.g. HR, Technical and Payroll fields).\",\"<b>Related items are logically ordered</b> (First/Middle/Last name; Start/End date; Origin/Destination).\",\"Your <b>logo is in the same place on every page</b> (no shift).\",\"The <b>background is not complicated</b> and does not distract from content.\"]},{\"h\":\"Navigation\",\"pts\":[\"There is a <b>clear call-to-action</b> telling users what to do next. A CTA is an eye-catching button for the most common next step.\",\"It is clear <b>where users are</b> on the site, for example with <b>breadcrumbs</b>.\",\"There are clear <b>links to the Home page and categories on every page</b>.\",\"<b>Navigation tabs are at the top</b> of the page.\",\"There is an <b>obvious change on hover</b> over anything clickable.\",\"The <b>company logo links to the homepage</b>.\",\"Map out <b>\\\"Red Routes\\\"</b>: the series of pages or actions customers most often use to complete tasks.\"]}],\"cards\":[[\"Color palette rule\",\"Stick to a simple 3-color palette.\"],[\"One dismiss button: label?\",\"\\\"Close\\\".\"],[\"Action + dismiss buttons: label?\",\"\\\"Cancel\\\" (not \\\"Close\\\"): to the right and not eye-catching.\"],[\"Parts of a good error message\",\"Problem in the title; why it happened; a link to complete or go back; recommendations for next action.\"],[\"System notice buttons\",\"Action, \\\"Remind me later\\\", \\\"Don't show again\\\".\"],[\"Above the fold\",\"The part of the page visible without scrolling; put the most important info there.\"],[\"Grouping related info\",\"By frame, separator or color.\"],[\"Red Routes\",\"The series of pages or actions customers most often use to complete tasks.\"],[\"Logo rules\",\"Same place on every page, and it links to the homepage.\"]],\"qa\":[[\"What are the guidelines for writing error messages?\",\"State the problem in the title; describe briefly why it happened; when possible link to complete the task or return to the previous state; give recommendations for the next action.\"],[\"List the layout guidelines.\",\"Most important info above the fold; group related info (frame or separator); use color to group; order related items logically; keep the logo in the same place on every page; keep the background simple.\"],[\"List the navigation guidelines.\",\"A clear CTA for what to do next; show where users are (breadcrumbs); links to Home and categories on every page; tabs at the top; obvious hover change on clickables; logo links to the homepage; map out Red Routes.\"],[\"When do you use \\\"Close\\\" versus \\\"Cancel\\\"?\",\"One button only: \\\"Close\\\". Two buttons (an action plus a dismiss): \\\"Cancel\\\", usually on the right and not eye-catching.\"]],\"quiz\":[{\"q\":\"If a dialog has only ONE button to dismiss it, the button should say:\",\"o\":[[\"Cancel\",\"Cancel is used when there is also an action button.\"],[\"Close\",\"Correct. When a dialog has only one button, it should say \\\"Close\\\". \\\"Cancel\\\" is used only when there are two buttons, an action button and one to dismiss (L9, Close vs Cancel).\"],[\"Exit\",\"\\\"Exit\\\" is not a label in the guideline. The rule is \\\"Close\\\" for a single dismiss button and \\\"Cancel\\\" next to an action button (L9, Close vs Cancel).\"],[\"Abort\",\"\\\"Abort\\\" is not a label the guideline uses; it sounds harsh and technical. A lone dismiss button should say \\\"Close\\\" (L9, Close vs Cancel).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"How many primary colors should a form or page use?\",\"o\":[[\"A simple 3-color palette\",\"Correct. The form guideline is to stick to a simple 3-color palette, because more than 3 primary colors is difficult and confuses users (L9, Forms).\"],[\"As many as possible\",\"Many colors break the guideline: more than 3 primary colors is difficult and confuses users, so keep a simple 3-color palette (L9, Forms).\"],[\"Only 1\",\"One color is not the rule. The guideline is a simple 3-color palette; the limit is not going above 3 (L9, Forms).\"],[\"7\",\"7 is more than double the limit. Over 3 primary colors confuses users, so the guideline says a simple 3-color palette (L9, Forms).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"Above the fold\\\" means:\",\"o\":[[\"The page header only\",\"Too narrow: \\\"above the fold\\\" is everything visible without scrolling, not just the header. The most important information goes there (L9, Layout).\"],[\"The part of the page visible without scrolling\",\"Correct. Put the most important information there.\"],[\"The footer area\",\"The opposite: the footer is usually below the fold, out of view until you scroll. Above the fold is the part visible without scrolling (L9, Layout).\"],[\"A folded menu\",\"Nothing is folded: \\\"the fold\\\" is the bottom edge of the first screen, and above it is what users see without scrolling (L9, Layout).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Red Routes\\\" are:\",\"o\":[[\"Error pages\",\"Red Routes have nothing to do with errors. They are the series of pages or actions customers most often use to complete tasks (L9, Navigation).\"],[\"The series of pages or actions customers most often use to complete tasks\",\"Correct. Mapping out \\\"Red Routes\\\" means identifying the series of pages or actions customers most often use to complete tasks, so navigation supports them (L9, Navigation).\"],[\"Links colored red\",\"\\\"Red\\\" is not about link color: Red Routes are the most common paths of pages or actions customers use to complete tasks (L9, Navigation).\"],[\"Forbidden navigation paths\",\"Red Routes are the paths customers use most, not paths they are blocked from (L9, Navigation).\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is a navigation guideline?\",\"o\":[[\"The company logo links to the homepage\",\"Correct. One of the navigation guidelines is that the company logo links to the homepage (and the layout rule keeps it in the same place on every page) (L9, Navigation).\"],[\"Tabs are placed at the bottom of the page\",\"Navigation tabs belong at the top of the page, not the bottom (L9, Navigation).\"],[\"Clickable items look the same on hover\",\"The guideline is the reverse: there should be an obvious change on hover over anything clickable, so users know it can be clicked (L9, Navigation).\"],[\"Home links appear only on the home page\",\"Home and category links belong on every page.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"A good error message should FIRST:\",\"o\":[[\"State the encountered problem in the title\",\"Correct. The first error-message guideline is to state the problem in the title, then briefly say why it happened, link to complete or return, and recommend a next action (L9, Forms).\"],[\"Show a long technical stack trace\",\"A long stack trace is not a brief explanation. The guideline says to state the problem in the title, then describe briefly why it happened (L9, Forms).\"],[\"Apologize repeatedly\",\"Apologies are not among the error-message guidelines. The first step is stating the problem in the title (L9, Forms).\"],[\"Close the application\",\"Closing the app loses the user's work. The guidelines want a link to complete the task or return to the previous state, plus a next action (L9, Forms).\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Where is the \\\"Cancel\\\" button most commonly placed?\",\"o\":[[\"To the left, eye-catching\",\"The guideline says right and not eye-catching.\"],[\"To the right, not eye-catching\",\"Correct. Cancel is most commonly placed to the right and is not eye-catching, so the action button gets the user's attention (L9, Close vs Cancel).\"],[\"At the top of the page\",\"The top of the page is not where dialog buttons go. Cancel is most commonly placed to the right and is not eye-catching (L9, Close vs Cancel).\"],[\"Hidden\",\"Cancel must still be visible, just not eye-catching, and is usually on the right; hiding it would leave users unable to dismiss (L9, Close vs Cancel).\"]],\"a\":1}],\"extra\":[{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A dialog asks \\\"Delete this file?\\\" with a \\\"Delete\\\" button and a second button that dismisses the dialog. What should the second button say?\",\"o\":[[\"Cancel\",\"Correct. With two buttons (an action and one to dismiss), use \\\"Cancel\\\", not \\\"Close\\\".\"],[\"Close\",\"\\\"Close\\\" is used only when there is a single button.\"],[\"Exit\",\"The lecture's rule is \\\"Cancel\\\" next to an action button.\"],[\"Remind me later\",\"That button belongs to a system notice, not a confirmation dialog.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"Which of these does <b>NOT</b> belong in a well-designed system notice?\",\"o\":[[\"An \\\"Action\\\" button\",\"This is one of the parts of a system notice.\"],[\"A long, generic paragraph of text\",\"Correct. Notice messages should not be lengthy or generic.\"],[\"A \\\"Remind me later\\\" button\",\"This is one of the parts of a system notice.\"],[\"A \\\"Don't show again\\\" box\",\"This is one of the parts of a system notice.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"\\\"Alert messages may change color, style and placement from page to page to grab attention.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"This breaks the consistency guideline.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. Alert messages should be consistent across the whole site; do not change color, style or placement.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"Which is <b>NOT</b> a guideline for error messages?\",\"o\":[[\"Describe briefly why it happened, under the title\",\"Describing briefly why the error happened, under the title, is the second error-message guideline, so it is not the answer (L9, Forms).\"],[\"When possible, link to complete the task or return to the previous state\",\"Linking to complete the task or return to the previous state is a listed guideline, so it is not the answer (L9, Forms).\"],[\"Hide the cause and show only a vague \\\"Something went wrong\\\"\",\"Correct. The guidelines say to state the problem in the title and describe why it happened.\"],[\"Recommend the next course of action\",\"Recommending the next course of action is the last error-message guideline, so it is not the answer (L9, Forms).\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A form asks for Last name, then Date of birth, then First name, then Middle name. Which layout guideline does it violate?\",\"o\":[[\"Put the most important information above the fold\",\"The problem is the order of the fields, not whether they are visible.\"],[\"Stick to a simple 3-color palette\",\"Nothing in the scenario is about colors.\"],[\"Keep the logo in the same place on every page\",\"Nothing in the scenario is about the logo.\"],[\"Related items are logically ordered\",\"Correct. Names should be ordered First/Middle/Last, not mixed with other fields.\"]],\"a\":3},{\"q\":\"<b>Scenario:</b> A site's logo is at the top-left of the home page but moves to the top-right on other pages. Which guideline is broken?\",\"o\":[[\"Your logo is in the same place on every page\",\"Correct. The logo should not shift between pages.\"],[\"The background is not complicated\",\"The issue is the logo's position, not the background.\"],[\"Map out Red Routes\",\"Red Routes are the paths customers most often use, not logo placement.\"],[\"Cancel is placed to the right\",\"That is about dialog buttons, not the logo.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"\\\"There should be an obvious change on hover over anything clickable.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"Correct. This is one of the navigation guidelines.\"],[\"False\",\"The lecture lists an obvious hover change as a navigation guideline.\"]],\"a\":0},{\"q\":\"According to the layout guidelines, related information is grouped by:\",\"o\":[[\"Giving each field a different font\",\"Fonts are not the grouping method in the lecture.\"],[\"A frame or a separator\",\"Correct. Related information is grouped by a frame or a separator.\"],[\"Moving it below the fold\",\"The fold is about what is visible without scrolling, not grouping.\"],[\"Making every item a button\",\"Not all commands should be buttons, and buttons do not group information.\"]],\"a\":1},{\"q\":\"In the navigation guidelines, a call-to-action (CTA) is:\",\"o\":[[\"A link to the Home page\",\"Links to Home and categories on every page are a separate guideline. A CTA is an eye-catching button for the most common next step (L9, Navigation).\"],[\"A breadcrumb trail\",\"Breadcrumbs show where users are, not what to do next.\"],[\"An eye-catching button for the most common next step\",\"Correct. A clear CTA tells users what to do next.\"],[\"An alert message\",\"Alert messages inform users; they are not the CTA.\"]],\"a\":2},{\"q\":\"\\\"A rich, detailed background image behind the content is recommended to make the layout more attractive.\\\"\",\"o\":[[\"True\",\"This goes against the layout guideline.\"],[\"False\",\"Correct. The background should not be complicated and should not distract from the content.\"]],\"a\":1}]}],\"exams\":[{\"title\":\"Final Exam 2025/2026\",\"meta\":\"First term · 2 hours · 60 marks · Dr. Mona Lackousha, Prof. Dr. Sherif Mohram\",\"note\":\"This paper was scanned without an answer key. The answers below are worked out from the lectures.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Q1-A · True or False\",\"marks\":\"10 marks · 1 each\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Analyzing academic papers and previous research studies is an example of secondary research.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Papers and studies were produced by someone else, so this is secondary research (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Papers and previous studies were done by someone else, which is exactly the lecture's definition of secondary research (books, journals). Primary is research you conduct yourself (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Tactical research takes place after the design phase.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Tactical (design) research takes place DURING design. The research that follows launch is post-launch research (L3).\",\"ow\":[\"Tactical (design) research happens during the design phase, using feedback on prototypes. Research after launch is post-launch research (L3, Types by WHEN).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"High fidelity prototypes are faster, easier and cheaper to generate than low fidelity.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Reversed. Low-fidelity prototypes are the faster, easier and cheaper ones (L8).\",\"ow\":[\"Reversed: low-fidelity prototypes are the faster, easier and cheaper ones, which is why you start lo-fi and move to hi-fi later (L8, Power of lo-fi).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Individuals interview easier to coordinate than groups' interview.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The comparison lists \\\"easier to coordinate the logistics\\\" under individuals (L6).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The individuals vs groups table lists \\\"easier to coordinate the logistics\\\" as an advantage of individual interviews, so the statement is true (L6).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"A well-designed survey should take a long time to complete to ensure detailed responses.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Keep surveys as small as possible. Too many questions reduce the response rate and reliability (L4).\",\"ow\":[\"Long surveys hurt: the tip is to keep a survey as small as possible, because too many questions reduce the response rate and reliability (L4, Surveys).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"UX Design refers to interactivity, look and feel of product screen or web page.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"That is UI design. UX is the overall experience (L1).\",\"ow\":[\"Interactivity, look and feel of a screen or page is UI design. UX design is the user's overall experience with the product or website (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Survey is common method used in primary research.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Surveys are listed among the common primary methods (L3).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Surveys are one of the lecture's primary research methods, with interviews, focus groups, usability studies and observations, since you run them yourself (L3).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"User feedback is not necessary when designing a user interface.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The whole course is about user-centered design: involve users early and often, and prototypes exist to get feedback (L3, L7).\",\"ow\":[\"User-centered design needs users at every step: \\\"you need users early and often\\\", and prototypes exist to get feedback and iterate (L3; L7).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Focus groups and interviews are examples of primary research methods.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Focus groups and interviews are both in the lecture's list of primary methods (with surveys, usability studies and observations), because you conduct them yourself (L3, Types by WHO).\",\"ow\":[null,\"Focus groups and interviews are both in the lecture's list of primary methods, because you conduct them yourself (L3, Types by WHO).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Qualitative research involves data of numeric nature.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Numeric data is quantitative. Qualitative is about why and how (L4).\",\"ow\":[\"Numeric data (measures, counts) is quantitative. Qualitative research is observations about why and how things happen, often from interviews (L4, Types by WHAT).\",null]}]},{\"title\":\"Q1-B · Multiple choice\",\"marks\":\"10 marks · 1 each\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Why is privacy and security important in user research?\",\"o\":[\"It ensures compliance and protects user data\",\"It makes research faster\",\"It allows sharing all data publicly\",\"It reduces the need for interviews\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Privacy protects users' PII and SPII. The other options are unrelated or the opposite of privacy.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Privacy does not speed research up: the guidelines add care (collect only what you need, avoid linking to PII/SPII, delete after use) (L4).\",\"The opposite of privacy: you should collect only the data you need, keep it apart from PII/SPII and delete it when no longer needed (L4).\",\"Privacy does not replace any research method; interviews are still needed. It matters because it protects user data (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which statement reflects good practice in user research?\",\"o\":[\"Prepare questions ahead but adapt as needed during the session\",\"Never change the script under any circumstance\",\"Use only quantitative methods\",\"Focus on convenience over privacy\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Plan ahead, but \\\"the script is not a bible\\\". Combine methods and keep privacy essential.\",\"ow\":[null,\"The lecture says planning is not strictness: \\\"the script is not a bible\\\", so adapt during the session (L4, General tips).\",\"Methods should be combined in a relevant order, and qualitative research is what gives real understanding, so quantitative-only is wrong (L4).\",\"Privacy and security of user data are listed as essential, so they must never be traded for convenience (L4, General tips).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Quantitative research is often easier for:\",\"o\":[\"Designers only\",\"Engineers and technical people\",\"Marketing teams only\",\"Children\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"L4: quantitative (numeric) data is \\\"easier for engineers and technical people to handle\\\".\",\"ow\":[\"The lecture does not single out designers; it says quantitative (numeric) research is easier for engineers and technical people (L4).\",null,\"Marketing teams are not mentioned. Quantitative research is easier for engineers and technical people (L4, Types by WHAT).\",\"Children have nothing to do with this point. Quantitative research is said to be easier for engineers and technical people (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following best summarizes a good survey design principle?\",\"o\":[\"Long surveys with complex questions\",\"Short, clear, unbiased surveys with participant feedback considered\",\"Surveys without planning\",\"Focus only on quantitative questions\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Keep it small, be clear, be aware of bias, and get feedback from participants.\",\"ow\":[\"The opposite of the tips: keep surveys as small as possible and questions concise, since long surveys hurt response rate and reliability (L4; L5).\",null,\"Research must be planned ahead; a survey without planning breaks the general research tips (L4, General tips).\",\"The lecture says to leave open-ended questions open and to combine methods, so quantitative-only questions are not the principle (L5, Be Unbiased).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Why is privacy and security important in user research? (repeated on page 2)\",\"o\":[\"It ensures compliance and protects user data\",\"It makes research faster\",\"It allows sharing all data publicly\",\"It reduces the need for interviews\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Same question as #1, printed twice on the paper.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Privacy does not speed research up: the guidelines add care (collect only what you need, avoid linking to PII/SPII, delete after use) (L4).\",\"The opposite of privacy: you should collect only the data you need, keep it apart from PII/SPII and delete it when no longer needed (L4).\",\"Privacy does not replace any research method; interviews are still needed. It matters because it protects user data (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What is the primary purpose of a wireframe?\",\"o\":[\"To showcase the final design with colors and images\",\"To test backend functionality\",\"To outline the structure and layout of a webpage or app without detailed design\",\"To write the code for the user interface\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A wireframe is a skeleton with placeholders and intentionally has no colors or fonts.\",\"ow\":[\"Wireframes intentionally have no colors or font styles; showing the final visual design is what hi-fi prototypes do (L8, Wireframes).\",\"Wireframes say nothing about the backend; they are a schematic of a screen's basic outline (L8, Wireframes).\",null,\"Wireframes are drawn (pen and paper, then Figma, Balsamiq…), not coded, and they come before visual design and code (L8).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What kind of tools is commonly used to create wireframes?\",\"o\":[\"Visual Studio and Eclipse\",\"Microsoft Word and Excel\",\"Balsamiq, Figma, and Sketch\",\"Final Cut Pro and Adobe Premiere\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"The lecture lists Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil.\",\"ow\":[\"Visual Studio and Eclipse are IDEs for code. The lecture's wireframing tools are Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil (L8).\",\"Word and Excel are office tools, not in the list. Wireframing tools are Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil (L8).\",null,\"Final Cut Pro and Premiere are video editors. Wireframing tools are Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Balsamiq and Pencil (L8).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is a key advantage of using low-fidelity prototypes?\",\"o\":[\"They are expensive and take time to build, which ensures quality\",\"They provide detailed animations and realistic UI\",\"They allow for quick feedback and rapid iteration\",\"They eliminate the need for high-fidelity prototypes\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Lo-fi is fast and cheap and gets more constructive feedback.\",\"ow\":[\"The opposite: lo-fi prototypes are faster, easier and cheaper to make, and that is their power (L8, Power of lo-fi).\",\"Detailed, realistic UI is high fidelity, a close match to the final look and feel; lo-fi prototypes are rough sketches (L8).\",null,\"Lo-fi does not replace hi-fi: you start with lo-fi and move to hi-fi as the design proceeds (L8, Prototypes and fidelity).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"What is an example of a qualitative primary research method in HCI?\",\"o\":[\"Statistical analysis of survey data\",\"Collecting data of numeric nature\",\"Observing users interacting with a prototype\",\"Analyzing existing system logs\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Observation is primary (you do it) and qualitative (how and why). Statistics and numbers are quantitative, and existing logs are not newly collected.\",\"ow\":[\"Statistical analysis works on numbers, so it is quantitative, not qualitative, even if the survey was primary (L4, Types by WHAT).\",\"Data of numeric nature is the definition of quantitative research, the opposite of what the question asks (L4, Types by WHAT).\",null,\"Existing system logs are data already there, not collected by you through a study, and logs are mostly counts (quantitative), not why/how (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Before a user interview, what should researchers do?\",\"o\":[\"Ask random questions without preparation\",\"Write a script of questions to guide the interview\",\"Ignore the interviewee's context\",\"Focus only on technical details\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"L4: \\\"effort should be done before the interview to write a script of the questions\\\".\",\"ow\":[\"The lecture says to plan ahead and write a script of questions instead of asking random questions (L4, General tips).\",null,\"Ignoring context is not a preparation step. Researchers should plan ahead with a script of questions (L4, General tips).\",\"User research is about users' needs and pain points, not only technical details. The preparation step is writing a question script (L4).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Question 2\",\"marks\":\"20 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"A. Compare between primary research and secondary research. (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"<table><tr><th></th><th>Primary</th><th>Secondary</th></tr><tr><td>Collected by</td><td>You (or someone you hire)</td><td>Someone else</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Surveys, focus groups, interviews, observations, experiments</td><td>Looking at existing data: books, journals, reports</td></tr><tr><td>Qual. or quant.?</td><td>Either</td><td>Either</td></tr><tr><td>Benefit</td><td>Specific to your needs; you control the quality</td><td>Usually cheap and quick</td></tr><tr><td>Drawback</td><td>Costs more and takes longer</td><td>Can be too old or not specific enough</td></tr></table>\",\"why\":\"The table in L4 slide 6 is exactly what's being asked. Draw it.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"B. Mention the points that should be taken into consideration to ask good survey questions and give examples. (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"<b>Be Clear</b>: label numeric scales (1 = Highly dissatisfied … 5 = Highly satisfied); avoid overlapping ranges (0-2, 3-5, 6-10); add detail when in doubt; timebox frequency (\\\"in the past seven days…\\\").<br><b>Be Concise</b>: plain language (\\\"How clean was the car?\\\").<br><b>Be Specific</b>: avoid super-big ideas; avoid double-barrel questions (split \\\"speed and availability\\\"); avoid internal conflict (split \\\"food\\\" into temperature, appearance, flavor).<br><b>Be Expressive</b>: emphasize opinions (\\\"too high, too low, or about right?\\\"); ranges instead of yes/no; levels of agreement; allow multiple selections.<br><b>Be Unbiased</b>: add \\\"Other: ___\\\"; leave open-ended questions open; avoid leading questions; avoid loaded words (\\\"wasted\\\" becomes \\\"spent\\\").<br><b>Be Usable</b>: progress bar, consistent page length, logical order, alert unanswered questions, preview the survey.\",\"why\":\"Name the 6 principles and give one \\\"don't / instead\\\" example for each. That covers the marks.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"C. What is meant by user research based on \\\"When\\\"? (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"<b>1. Foundational (strategic / generative)</b>: before anything is designed. It identifies user problems and what to build by talking with users about their pain points, and reveals opportunities the team would not think of.<br><b>2. Tactical (design)</b>: during the design phase. It informs how the product should be built, using user feedback on prototypes.<br><b>3. Post-launch</b>: after release. Did we succeed? Does the system meet user needs?\",\"why\":\"From L3 slides 11–12.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"D. What are the types of design–development process? (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"<b>Handoff process</b>: the design phase finishes completely, then the design is handed over to development (sequential, one handover point).<br><b>Handshake process</b>: design and development run <b>in parallel</b> and synchronize at many points during the project, so issues are caught early.\",\"why\":\"From L8 slide 11 (the timeline figure: one handover point versus many sync points).\"}]},{\"title\":\"Question 3\",\"marks\":\"20 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"A. Design a mobile application for booking doctors. Target audience: patients of all ages including adults and elderly users, plus doctors and clinic administrators. Purpose: search doctors, view specialties, check appointments, book or cancel consultations simply. Think about a layout of the interface that you consider usable and easy.\",\"ans\":\"<b>1. Start from user research.</b> Elderly users need <b>large text and big touch targets</b> (like the truckers case). Keep a small number of steps and plain language.<br><b>2. Home screen (above the fold):</b> logo at the top-left (same place on every screen, links home); a big <b>search bar</b> (\\\"Search doctor, specialty…\\\"); specialty icons in a grid (Cardiology, Dentistry…); \\\"My upcoming appointment\\\" card; bottom <b>tab bar</b>: Home · Search · Appointments · Profile.<br><b>3. Doctor list:</b> filters (specialty, area, price, rating); each card shows photo, name, specialty, rating, next free slot and a <b>primary CTA \\\"Book\\\"</b>.<br><b>4. Booking flow</b> (a fixed path, so use a <b>progress bar</b>: Doctor → Date/Time → Details → Confirm); remember the patient's data (<b>remove repetitive actions</b>); fields with <b>hints and formats</b>; <b>errors next to the field</b> with a red border.<br><b>5. Confirmation page</b> after booking (\\\"Appointment booked with Dr. X, Sun 10:00\\\") with \\\"Add to calendar\\\".<br><b>6. Cancel appointment:</b> a dialog with an action button \\\"Cancel appointment\\\" and a secondary, not eye-catching \\\"Keep\\\" button on the right; consistent alert style.<br><b>7. Visual rules:</b> a 3-color palette, simple background, related info grouped by frames, a clear primary vs secondary CTA.<br><b>8. Other roles:</b> doctors and admins get a schedule view (day/week) and a manage-slots screen.<br><b>9. Process:</b> sketch 5+ paper wireframes per screen, test with patients and elderly users, iterate, then build hi-fi in Figma.\",\"why\":\"Marks come from applying lecture guidelines: user research, wireframes, forms, layout and navigation (L8–L9). Mention the guideline name next to each design decision.\"}]}]},{\"title\":\"Final Exam 2024/2025\",\"meta\":\"First term · 2 hours · 60 marks · Dr. Mona Lackousha, Dr. Mohamed Awad\",\"note\":\"The official solved paper marks T/F #2 and #7 as True and MCQ #3 as \\\"Survey\\\". These conflict with the lectures. Both the key and the lecture-based answer are shown for those questions.\",\"sections\":[{\"title\":\"Q1-A · True or False\",\"marks\":\"15 marks · 1 each\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"It is important to understand the tasks that the user requires to do.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"User research covers the users AND their tasks (L1).\",\"ow\":[null,\"User research means learning about the users AND the tasks they try to complete, and the user really interacts with the task, not the system (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Tactical research takes place after the design phase.\",\"a\":1,\"key\":\"Official key: True (√)\",\"why\":\"L3 says tactical research \\\"takes place during the design phase\\\". After the design phase would be post-launch research. The key likely reads \\\"after\\\" loosely; answer from the lecture unless your doctor says otherwise.\",\"ow\":[\"Tactical (design) research happens during the design phase, using feedback on prototypes. Research after it is post-launch research (L3, Types by WHEN).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"High fidelity prototypes are faster, easier and cheaper to generate than Low fidelity.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Reversed. Low-fidelity prototypes are the faster, easier and cheaper ones; hi-fi is not even possible early because details are undecided (L8, Power of lo-fi).\",\"ow\":[\"Reversed: low-fidelity prototypes are the faster, easier and cheaper ones, which is why you start lo-fi and move to hi-fi later (L8, Power of lo-fi).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Interview is most common research technique.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The lecture describes the interview as the most common research technique, valued for deeper understanding through open \\\"why\\\" questions and follow-ups (L6, Interviews).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The lecture opens its interviews section by calling the interview the most common research technique, so the statement is true (L6, Interviews).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Individuals interview easier to coordinate than groups interview.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Individuals are easier to coordinate logistically.\",\"ow\":[null,\"The individuals vs groups table lists \\\"easier to coordinate the logistics\\\" as an advantage of individual interviews, so the statement is true (L6).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Primary research done by someone else.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Research by someone else is secondary. Primary is research you conduct yourself.\",\"ow\":[\"Research done by someone else (books, journals) is secondary research. Primary research is research you conduct yourself, like interviews and surveys (L3).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"\\\"Be biased\\\" is important point for a good survey.\",\"a\":1,\"key\":\"Official key: True (√)\",\"why\":\"The principle is \\\"Be UNbiased\\\". The key probably treats this as a typo for \\\"Be unbiased\\\". As written, the statement is false.\",\"ow\":[\"One of the six principles is \\\"Be Unbiased\\\" (avoid leading and loaded questions, allow \\\"Other\\\"), so being biased is never good for a survey (L5).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"UX Design refers to interactivity, look and feel of product screen or web page.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Interactivity, look and feel of a screen or page is the definition of UI design. UX design is the user's overall experience with the product or website (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\",\"ow\":[\"Interactivity, look and feel of a screen or page is UI design. UX design is the user's overall experience with the product or website (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"PII is specific details that could be used to identify a user such as names, phone number.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"This is the lecture's PII definition: details that could identify a user, with examples names, home address, email address and phone number (L4, Privacy and security).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement matches the lecture's PII definition: details that could identify a user, such as names, home address, email and phone number (L4).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Survey is more expensive than interview.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Surveys are among the cheapest methods; interviews need time per person and incentives.\",\"ow\":[\"Reversed: surveys are one of the cheapest methods, while interviews cost more because they are synchronous and interviewees expect incentives (L4; L6).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"HCI is interaction between user and computer OR relationship between users and computer systems.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"This is the L1 definition word for word: HCI is the interaction between user and computer, or the relationship between users and computer systems (L1, What is HCI?).\",\"ow\":[null,\"This is word for word the lecture's definition: HCI is the interaction between user and computer, or the relationship between users and computer systems (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"UI Design covers a user's overall experience with the product or website.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A user's overall experience with the product or website is UX design. UI design is only the interactivity, look and feel of a screen or page (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\",\"ow\":[\"The overall experience with a product or website is UX design. UI design is the interactivity, look and feel of a screen or page (L1, UI vs UX vs HCI).\",null]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"S/W development is not just a process of creating software, it is also a process of learning how to create the S/W best suited for its purpose.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"L1 says \\\"software development is research\\\": it is not just creating software but also learning how to create the software best suited for its purpose (L1, UI design process).\",\"ow\":[null,\"The statement is taken from the lecture's point that \\\"software development is research\\\": learning how to build the software best suited to its purpose (L1).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Prototype is one that enables you to get useful feedback and iterate on your design.\",\"a\":0,\"why\":\"This is the lecture's definition of a good prototype: it enables useful (constructive) feedback and iteration, and need not resemble the final product (L7, Prototyping).\",\"ow\":[null,\"This is the lecture's definition of a good prototype: one that enables useful, constructive feedback and iteration on the design (L7, Prototyping).\"]},{\"type\":\"tf\",\"q\":\"Qualitative research involves data of numeric nature.\",\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Numeric data (measures, counts) is quantitative research. Qualitative research is observations about why and how things happen, often from interviews (L4, Types by WHAT).\",\"ow\":[\"Numeric data (measures, counts) is quantitative. Qualitative research is observations about why and how things happen (L4, Types by WHAT).\",null]}]},{\"title\":\"Q1-B · Multiple choice\",\"marks\":\"5 marks · 1 each\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"HCI Terms interchangeably used:\",\"o\":[\"Human Computer Interaction\",\"Human Machine Interaction\",\"Man Machine Interaction\",\"All the above\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"HCI (Human Computer Interaction), HMI (Human Machine Interaction) and MMI (Man Machine Interaction) are used interchangeably for the same field, so all three are correct (L1, What is HCI?).\",\"ow\":[\"Human Computer Interaction is HCI itself, but HMI and MMI are equally valid terms, so choosing only this one is incomplete (L1).\",\"HMI is one interchangeable term, but HCI and MMI are too, so the complete answer is \\\"All the above\\\" (L1).\",\"MMI is one interchangeable term, but HCI and HMI are too, so the complete answer is \\\"All the above\\\" (L1).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Sensitive Personally Identifiable Information (SPII) such as:\",\"o\":[\"names\",\"home address\",\"email address\",\"Financial account numbers\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Names, address and email are regular PII. Financial account numbers are SPII.\",\"ow\":[\"Names are ordinary PII: they identify a user but are not in the sensitive list (SSN, license, passport, financial accounts, date of birth) (L4).\",\"Home address is listed as PII with names, email and phone; it is not one of the SPII examples (L4, Privacy and security).\",\"Email address is plain PII in the lecture's list. SPII is data whose loss could cause financial harm, embarrassment or discrimination (L4).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Plan ahead and prepare your questions before……..\",\"o\":[\"Interview\",\"Survey\",\"Observation\",\"none of the above\"],\"a\":0,\"key\":\"Official key: (b) Survey\",\"why\":\"L6's \\\"Tips for good interview\\\" begins \\\"Plan ahead and prepare your questions before the interview time\\\", and L4 says to write an interview script. The lecture wording matches \\\"Interview\\\". The key marks Survey, so mention the lecture sentence if asked.\",\"ow\":[null,\"Surveys must be planned too, but this sentence is the interview tip: \\\"Plan ahead and prepare your questions before the interview\\\" (L6). The official key reportedly marks this option.\",\"Observation is not what this sentence is about. The tip quoted is from the interview tips: prepare your questions before the interview (L6).\",\"An answer is in the lecture: the interview tips start with \\\"Plan ahead and prepare your questions before the interview\\\" (L6).\"]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Tips for good interview:\",\"o\":[\"Listen Well\",\"don't Leave sometime between interviews\",\"Take notes\",\"a & c\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Listen well and take notes are the two keys. (b) is the opposite of the real tip.\",\"ow\":[\"Listen well is one of the two keys during an interview, but take notes is the other, so the full answer is \\\"a & c\\\" (L6).\",\"This reverses the real tip: the lecture says to leave some time between interviews (L6, Tips for a good interview).\",\"Take notes is one of the two keys, but listening well is the other, so the full answer is \\\"a & c\\\" (L6).\",null]},{\"type\":\"mcq\",\"q\":\"Data that includes descriptions from interviewees:\",\"o\":[\"Quantitative data\",\"Qualitative data\",\"average data\",\"gathering data\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Interviewees' descriptions explain why and how things happen, which is the lecture's definition of qualitative data; quantitative data is numeric (measures, counts) (L4, Types by WHAT).\",\"ow\":[\"Quantitative data is numeric (measures, counts). Descriptions from interviewees explain why and how, so they are qualitative (L4).\",null,\"\\\"Average data\\\" is not a research data type. By WHAT, data is only quantitative (numeric) or qualitative (why and how) (L4).\",\"\\\"Gathering data\\\" is just collecting data, not a data type. Descriptions from interviews are qualitative data (L4).\"]}]},{\"title\":\"Question 2\",\"marks\":\"20 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"A. What is the difference between primary research and secondary research? (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"Primary: collected by you (surveys, focus groups, interviews, observations, experiments); specific to your needs and you control the quality; costs more and takes longer.<br>Secondary: collected by someone else (existing data, books, journals); cheap and quick; can be too old or not specific enough.<br>Both can be qualitative or quantitative.\",\"why\":\"L4 comparison table.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"B. Evaluate the following survey questions: (i) Do you use social media? (ii) Why do you like to exercise? (iii) In the past seven days, how much time have you wasted on social media? (iv) On a scale of 1 to 4 with 1 meaning 'a lot' and 4 meaning 'not at all', how much do you enjoy exercising? (v) On a scale of 1 to 6 with 1 meaning 'not at all' and 6 meaning 'a lot', how much do you like social media? (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"<b>(i)</b> A <b>yes/no question</b> (not expressive). Use ranges: \\\"In the past seven days, how much time have you spent on social media? 0 / &lt;1 / 1-2 / 3-5 / 6-9 / 10+ hours\\\".<br><b>(ii)</b> A <b>leading question</b>: it assumes the person likes exercising. Ask \\\"How do you feel about exercising?\\\" or first ask whether they exercise.<br><b>(iii)</b> A <b>loaded question</b>: \\\"wasted\\\" is judgmental. Use \\\"spent\\\".<br><b>(iv)</b> An <b>ambiguous / reversed scale</b>: 1 = \\\"a lot\\\" runs against the usual low-to-high direction, and a 4-point scale has no neutral middle.<br><b>(v)</b> A <b>changing number of options</b> (6 here versus 4 in iv) and the <b>scale direction is reversed</b> compared with (iv). Keep the same scale length and direction across the survey.\",\"why\":\"This is the L5 exercise. Its answer labels were: ambiguous scale, changing number of options, reversing scale, leading questions, yes/no questions.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"C. What are the tips for good interview? (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"Plan ahead and prepare your questions before the interview.<br>During the interview, two keys for success:<br>• Listen well: the user should be talking most of the time.<br>• Take notes: one asks and the other takes notes (audio recording is not enough); note emotions, reactions and voice.<br>Leave some time between interviews.\",\"why\":\"L6 slide 4, which is also the official answer.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"D. What is the power of low-fidelity prototype? (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"• Lo-fi prototypes are faster, easier and cheaper to generate.<br>• Hi-fi is not feasible at the beginning because decisions about many details have not been made.<br>• People are much more willing to talk when given a sketch than something that looks finished.<br>• Konstan: a prototype that looks like little effort (hand-drawn) elicits more significant constructive feedback.<br>• Many details cause people to give feedback at a lower level than needed.<br>• Google: early, present high-level ideas; later focus on details like app screens.\",\"why\":\"L8 slides 5–6.\"}]},{\"title\":\"Question 3\",\"marks\":\"20 marks\",\"items\":[{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"A. Define Wireframes. (5 marks)\",\"ans\":\"A schematic for the basic outline of a screen in a system.<br>• Built using lines and basic shapes (rectangles, circles…) with some text; the lines look as if made of wires.<br>• Intentionally has no colors or font styles, to focus on structure.<br>• Wireframe = skeleton with placeholders.\",\"why\":\"The official answer (L8 slide 13). Adding the 4 purposes earns full marks.\"},{\"type\":\"written\",\"q\":\"B. If you are involved in the design of a mobile application for online shopping, think about a layout of the interface that you may consider usable and easy. (15 marks)\",\"ans\":\"<b>Research first:</b> who shops (ages, devices, low-end phones?) and their top tasks (search, compare, buy, track order), which are the <b>red routes</b>.<br><b>Home (above the fold):</b> logo top-left that links home; search bar; category chips; offers banner; bottom tab bar: Home · Categories · Cart · Orders · Account.<br><b>Product list:</b> grid cards (image, name, price, rating) with filters and sort; breadcrumbs (Home › Men › Shoes) show where the user is.<br><b>Product page:</b> images, price, size/color selectors; a primary CTA \\\"Add to cart\\\" stands out and \\\"Add to wishlist\\\" is secondary.<br><b>Checkout</b> (a fixed path, so a <b>progress bar</b>: Cart → Shipping → Payment → Confirm): saved address and card (remove repetitive actions), hints/formats in fields, errors next to the field.<br><b>Confirmation page</b> with order number and tracking.<br><b>Visual rules:</b> 3-color palette, simple background, grouping by frames, consistent alerts, obvious hover/press states, Cancel on the right and not eye-catching.<br><b>Process:</b> paper wireframes (5+ versions per screen) → feedback → Figma hi-fi → test with users → iterate.\",\"why\":\"Examiners reward applying named guidelines from L8–L9. Label each decision with the rule it follows.\"}]}]}]}");
